hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5401","4610 - And with what Yunus narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to me when I discussed with him the question of one who sells goods then dies or goes bankrupt, and a man finds his goods intact. He said to me: He is more entitled to them in death and bankruptcy. Then he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, then he mentioned the like of Muhammad's hadith from Ibn Abi Fudayk. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith only returns to Abu al-Mu'tamir, who is unknown, not known who he is. We never heard mention of him except in this hadith. Whoever is of this standing is not among those whose narration may be used as proof in this matter - besides that, had it been established, the hadith of al-Zuhri from Abu Bakr from Abu Hurayrah would be more deserving than it, for it was reported by imams whose reports establish proof, whom what one like Abu al-Mu'tamir - unknown, not known who he is - reported must not be set against. Had one pondered Abu al-Mu'tamir's hadith, he would see no proof in it, for in it is: Any man who goes bankrupt or dies - which may be upon doubt, so the hadith returns to its being unknown what in it of what is mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): is it about bankruptcy or death? We found no scholar taking all that is in this hadith except Malik ibn Anas. As for others, we mentioned their sayings in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"5402","726 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his divorcing Hafsah, and his taking her back after that. 4611 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Isma'il ibn al-Khalil al-Kufi narrated to us; and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us - Ibn Abi Dawud said: Salih ibn Salih informed me, and Ahmad said in his hadith: from Salih ibn Salih, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah, then took her back."
"5403","4612 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5404","4613 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Isma'il ibn al-Khalil al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Umar, who said: Umar entered upon Hafsah, my sister, while she was weeping. He said: What is with you - perhaps the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced you. He had divorced you once, then took you back for my sake. Abu Ja'far said: This Salih ibn Salih is Ibn Salih ibn Hayy, who reports from al-Sha'bi; Abu Ali; and al-Hasan ibn Salih. This indicates he had three sons from whom knowledge was taken: Ali, al-Hasan, and Salih. As for Ali and al-Hasan, they were born in one pregnancy."
"5405","As Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim al-Qattan al-Kufi Abu Muhammad narrated to me, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad, a man of Kufah, narrated to me, he said: My grandfather narrated to me, he said: Salih ibn Hayy said: I said to al-Sha'bi: Two sons were born to me tonight. He said: What did you name them? I said: I named one Ali and the other Hasan. He said to me: You did well. May Allah bless them for you, and exalt Ali and make Hasan good. Strengthening this is that al-Bukhari mentioned in his book: Abd Allah ibn Salih ibn Salih ibn Hayy al-Hamdani heard from Abthar ibn al-Qasim; Amr al-Naqid heard from him. Abu Ja'far said: As for Ali and Hasan, they left no offspring, and their deaths were early, as I heard Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi say: Ali ibn Salih and Mis'ar ibn Kidam died in the year 155, and al-Hasan ibn Salih died in the year 167."
"5406","4614 - And Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn Salih informed me, from Musa ibn Ulayy, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah. Jibril came to him and said: Take her back, for she fasts much and prays at night. Abu Ja'far said: This Amr ibn Salih is a man of Egypt among those dwelling in al-Hamra, known as Batn al-Dayr."
"5407","4615 - And Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah with one divorce. Jibril came to him and said: O Muhammad, you divorced Hafsah with one divorce. She fasts much and prays at night, and she is your wife in this world and in Paradise. An objector said: How do you accept the like of this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he divorces a wife of his wives who is his wife in this world and in Paradise, when Allah had given the wives of His Prophet - she among them - choice between this world and the Hereafter, and they chose Allah and His Messenger over this world. Allah thanked them for that, kept him upon them and kept them upon him, until He made them after his death as they were in his life, for they are confined to him and forbidden to all other people. Our answer to him in that was: Though he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced her, he did not thereby remove her from his wives deserving in this world and the Hereafter what the wives he did not divorce deserved. His divorcing her was a divorce that did not cut the bond between him and her, for it was a revocable divorce. Then, by Allah's praise and His favor regarding her, there was his taking her back to what she was upon before his divorcing her, may Allah be pleased with her. If this objector says: Had her waiting period expired without his taking her back, would she thereby leave the Mothers of the Believers so as not to be a mother to them as before? Our answer to him in that was: Had that been, she would not have left the Mothers of the Believers, but would remain a mother to them after him. Her sanctity upon them would remain as before that, and she is a wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Paradise - just as death would not remove her from that had he died leaving her, while after his death she still deserves maintenance from what he spent upon her in his life, for she is confined to him after his death as she was confined to him in his life. In what we mentioned is clarification of what this objector imagined. And success is from Allah."
"5408","727 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the cause in which was sent down: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of night and day are signs for those of understanding. 4616 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Ya'qub al-Qummi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Quraysh came to the Jews and said: What signs did Musa bring you? They said: His staff, and his hand white to beholders. They came to the Christians and said: How was Isa among you? They said: He would heal the blind and the leper and give life to the dead. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Call upon your Lord for us to make al-Safa gold for us. He called upon Him with that, so this verse was sent down: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth - the verse - so let them reflect upon it. In this hadith is that the cause in which this verse was sent down was Quraysh's asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to call upon Allah to make al-Safa gold for them, and his calling with that, and that Allah revealed to him in that this verse. It is reported from Ibn Abbas through another channel in that."
"5409","4617 - What al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Imran al-Sulami, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Quraysh said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Call upon your Lord for us to make al-Safa gold for us; if it becomes gold in the morning we will follow you. He called upon his Lord, so Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and said: Your Lord greets you with peace and says: If you wish, it becomes gold for them in the morning, and whoever disbelieves after it among them, I will punish him a painful punishment I never punished anyone of the worlds. And if you wish, I will open for them the door of repentance and mercy. He said: Rather, O Lord, repentance and mercy. In this hadith is Jibril's giving choice, from Allah, to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between the two things mentioned in this hadith, and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) choosing of them what is mentioned of his choosing. By that we understood that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose of these two things he was given choice between was out of dislike to choose the other cause of them, lest Quraysh disbelieve after that and the punishment Allah threatened them with befall them, if He did for them what they asked then they disbelieved in it after that - as He did with nations before them after He showed them the signs they had asked of Him. His choosing for them the other meaning of the two he was given choice between was care for them, compassion toward them, and choosing for them better for them than what they chose for themselves. Then Allah revealed to His Prophet after that, as argument against them, alerting them, and informing them, that they have with them of His signs what is greater than what they asked..."
"5410","4618 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, from Abu Janab al-Kalbi, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, who said: I entered with Abd Allah ibn Umar and Ubayd ibn Umayr upon A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them, while she was in her curtained chamber. She said: Who are these? We said: Abd Allah ibn Umar and Ubayd ibn Umayr. She said: O Ubayd ibn Umayr, you are as the first said: Visit seldom and love increases. Ibn Umar said: Leave us from this idle talk of yours; narrate to us the most wondrous you saw of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She wept severe weeping, then said: All his affair was wonder. One night he came to me, and I had entered my bed; he entered with me until his skin touched my skin. Then he said: O A'ishah, permit me to worship my Lord. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I love your nearness and love your desire. She said: He stood to a waterskin in the house and performed ablution from it, then recited the Qur'an, then wept until I thought his tears reached his lap. Then he sat and supplicated and wept until I thought his tears reached his waist. Then he lay on his right side, placing his right hand under his right cheek, then wept until I thought his tears reached the ground. Then Bilal came to him after calling the adhan and greeted; when he saw him weeping, he said: O Messenger of Allah, you weep though Allah forgave you what preceded of your sin and what follows? He said: Why should I not weep when there was sent down upon me tonight: Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of night and day - the verse. Woe to whoever recites it then does not reflect upon it. Woe to you, O Bilal - shall I not be a grateful servant? In this hadith is Allah's sending down this verse upon him in the night he was with A'ishah, and what was between him and his Lord..."
"5411","728 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as evidence for the obligation in what scholars differed about: the ruling of two men judging between them - is it binding upon them as a judge's ruling upon them, so that a judge raised to may not annul it if it is of what some scholars hold though he holds otherwise? 4619 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ammar ibn Khalid al-Wasiti narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Malik - meaning al-Muzani - informed us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Umar said: When three are on a journey, let them appoint one of them. That is a commander the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed."
"5412","4620 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Ali ibn Bahr ibn Barri and Abu Muslim Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus narrated to us, they said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan informed me, from Nafi' freedman of Ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When three are on a journey, let them appoint one of them. Nafi' said: I said to Abu Salamah: Then you are our commander. Abu Ja'far said: In these two hadiths is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the commander people appoint over themselves, when far from his commanders, like his commanders over them in the obligation of hearing from them and obeying them, in what their commanders command them when in their presence. When that is so in command, its like is in judgment: when two disputing men appoint a judge between them in what they dispute, that judge's affair in what they appointed him is like the judgment upon them of the judge their imam - to whom appointing judges over them belongs - appointed, in what binds them of judgment for them and against them. This is a question scholars disputed. A group of them said: What that judge judges between the two who appointed him, then raised to the judge the imam appointed for people - he examines it; if it agrees with what he holds in it, he executes it, and if it opposes what he holds though other scholars hold it, he rejects it. Among scholars holding that were Abu Hanifah and his companions. And among them are those who said: The judge to whom that judgment is raised may neither reject nor annul it, unless it is outside the sayings of all scholars - then he rejects and annuls it. If it is not so, he may neither reject nor annul it; he must execute it as he executes the judgment of a judge before him among judges. Among scholars holding that were Ibn Abi Layla and the jurists of Madinah. Al-Shafi'i held both views. The more deserving of the two views with us in that, most resembling truth, is what Ibn Abi Layla and the Madinans said in it, due to their consensus - and their opponents' - upon what necessitates that: we saw they do not differ that had those two men wanted, after that judgment between them, to reject that judgment from them, or one of them wanted it before reaching the judge, that is neither for them nor for either of them, since it bound them by the judging of the judge in it between them with what was judged between them in it. Since that was so in its binding them before reaching the judge, then it reached the judge while binding them, and the course of judges in what reaches them of what already bound before that is strengthening it, not annulling it - he must thereby strengthen that judgment between those two men and execute it between them, as he executes the judgment of a judge who judged between them among the judges of the imams who appoint them judgments among people. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5413","729 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the first two rak'ahs of prayers whose rak'ahs exceed two, to four or three: is one lengthened over the other in recitation, or equalized? 4621 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr with the Fatihah of the Book and a surah, lengthening in the first, and letting us hear the verse."
"5414","4622 - And Bakkar narrated to us: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father ... then he mentioned the like, adding: He would recite in the last two rak'ahs with the Fatihah of the Book. He would lengthen the first rak'ah of Zuhr, and the first rak'ah of the morning prayer."
"5415","4623 - And Bakkar narrated to us: Abu Dawud, author of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah ... then he mentioned the like, adding: He would recite with us in the first two rak'ahs of the Asr prayer."
"5416","4624 - And Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would lengthen recitation in the first of the Zuhr prayer over recitation in its second. This meaning is of what scholars differed in: some held in it what is in this hadith, agreeing with it - among them Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And some held equalizing recitation in the first two rak'ahs of these prayers - among them Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf. They all agreed about recitation in the first rak'ah of the dawn prayer: it is lengthened in recitation over its second rak'ah. We looked into what they differed in of that: do we find any reports indicating contrary to what is in the hadith of Abu Qatadah of that, or not?"
"5417","4625 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadhan, from al-Walid Abu Bishr, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand in Zuhr in the first two rak'ahs, in each rak'ah the measure of thirty verses' recitation; in the last two, half that. He would stand in Asr in the first two rak'ahs the measure of fifteen verses, and in the last two half that."
"5418","4626 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5419","4627 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur ibn Zadhan informed us, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Siddiq, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: We would estimate the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Zuhr and Asr. We estimated his standing in Zuhr the measure of thirty verses, the measure of Surat al-Sajdah, in the first two rak'ahs; in the last two, half that. We estimated his standing in the first two rak'ahs of Asr the measure of the last two of Zuhr, and his standing in the last two rak'ahs of Asr half that."
"5420","4628 - And Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: Thirty of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, gathered and said: Come, let us measure the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he does not recite aloud of prayer. No two men among them disagreed. They measured his recitation in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr the measure of thirty verses; in the last two rak'ahs, half that. In Asr, in the first two rak'ahs, the measure of half the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr; in the last two rak'ahs, the measure of half the last two rak'ahs of Zuhr. In this hadith is equalizing recitation in the first two rak'ahs of the Zuhr and Asr prayers. What is in these reports we mentioned in this section of this chapter was more deserving with us than what is in the first reports we mentioned in the preceding section of it, for these two prayers and what is like them of prayers divide into two parts: the latter part of them has equal what is recited in the first two rak'ahs of it. Like that, by reasoning, in the former part of them, recitation in its first two rak'ahs is equal. This was strengthened by what Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas did in addressing Umar, repelling the Kufans' saying he does not pray well, which Umar praised him for."
"5421","4629 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us; and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq also narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us - the wording is Bakkar's - from Shu'bah: Abu Awn al-Thaqafi informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Samurah say: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to Sa'd: They complained about you in everything, even prayer. Sa'd said: As for me, I lengthen in the first two, and shorten in the last two. I do not fall short in what I follow of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar said: That is the assumption of you - or: such is the assumption of you."
"5422","4630 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu Dawud, Wahb ibn Jarir, Muhammad ibn Kathir, and Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us ... then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5423","4631 - And as Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us: Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from Mis'ar, from Abu Awn, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, then he mentioned the like."
"5424","4632 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, then he mentioned the like."
"5425","4633 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Umar - meaning Ibn Aban - narrated to us, al-Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir - meaning Ibn Samurah - then he mentioned the like."
"5426","4634 - And as Ishaq narrated to us: Harun ibn Abd Allah - meaning al-Hammal - narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, from Shayban, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, then he mentioned the like. In this hadith is Sa'd's informing Umar that he would lengthen in the first two and shorten in the last two, following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, and Umar's praising him for that. In that is what confirmed what we mentioned: that the ruling of the first part of these prayers is lengthening in recitation, and the ruling of the other part is shortening. It is reasonable that when the latter part's two rak'ahs are equal in shortening, the former part's two rak'ahs are equal in lengthening. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5427","Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi informed us, Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi' narrated to me, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, would recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr with the Mother of the Qur'an and a surah, and would not recite anything in the last two. Al-Zuhri said: Jabir ibn Abd Allah would recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr with the Mother of the Qur'an and a surah, and in the last two with the Mother of the Qur'an. Al-Zuhri said: The people follow their imam."
"5428","And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Dhakwan, from A'ishah, that she would recite in the last two rak'ahs with the Fatihah of the Book, saying: They are but supplication. Abu Ja'far said: We wanted to examine that, to know which Asim this is: is he Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, so we do not make his hadith a proof, due to what isnad scholars say about him - or is he Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, so we make him a proof?"
"5429","We found Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Dhakwan, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, he said: She would recite, or command, the Fatihah of the Book in the last two. By that we understood that this Asim is Ibn Abi al-Najud, not Ibn Ubayd Allah. And we understood that A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, would recite it as supplication, not as she recites other Qur'an in prayer in other than those two rak'ahs. Then we considered: is anything reported in that from other than A'ishah and Ali among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"5430","We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Ubayd freedman of Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik, that Ubadah ibn Nusayy informed him he heard Qays ibn al-Harith say: Abu Abd Allah al-Sunabihi informed me that he came to Madinah in the caliphate of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, and prayed Maghrib behind Abu Bakr. He recited in the first two rak'ahs with the Mother of the Qur'an and a surah, a surah of the short Mufassal. Then he stood in the third rak'ah, and I drew near him until my garments almost touched his garments. I heard him recite the Mother of the Qur'an and this verse: Our Lord, do not deviate our hearts after You guided us, and grant us from Yourself mercy. Indeed You are the Bestower."
"5431","And we found Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, al-Awza'i and Malik narrated to us, they said: Abu Ubayd narrated to us, Ubadah ibn Nusayy narrated to me, from Qays ibn al-Harith, then he mentioned the like. Ubadah said: I attended Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz while he was saying to Qays, asking about this hadith, so he narrated it. Umar said: I never left it since I heard you narrate it - though before that I was upon other than it. I said: What is it, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: I used to recite: Say, He is Allah, One."
"5432","And we found Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Awn informed us, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', from al-Sunabihi, who said: I prayed Maghrib behind Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him. I drew near him until my garments touched his garments, or almost. He recited in the first two rak'ahs with the Fatihah of the Book and a surah, and recited in the last rak'ah with the Fatihah of the Book, saying: Our Lord, do not deviate our hearts after You guided us - to His saying: the Bestower. Then he said takbir and bowed. Yazid said: Muhammad ibn Rashid informed me, from Makhul, who said: By Allah, it was not recitation, but it was supplication."
"5433","And we found Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywah al-Kindi narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from al-Sunabihi - not mentioning anyone between them - then he mentioned the like of his hadith we mentioned from him in this chapter."
"5434","And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Abu Abd al-Rahman - not mentioning anyone between them - saying: from Abu Abd al-Rahman, not saying from Abu Abd Allah - then he mentioned the like. In this hadith is what strengthened what those who said went to: that recitation in the last two rak'ahs is but supplication and glorification, not like recitation in the first two rak'ahs of prayers. This is of what one who said it did not say by opinion, nor deduction, nor derivation - for its like is not said by opinion, deduction, or derivation, but by tawqif. What is of this course, opposing it is not sound, nor saying otherwise. Ibrahim al-Nakha'i also held this view."
"5435","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Glorification is more beloved to me in the last two rak'ahs. Likewise al-Thawri used to say in that."
"5436","As Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from al-Ashja'i, from Sufyan. As for Abu Hanifah and his companions: they held that recitation in them is more beloved to them than glorification in them. And Allah is the granter of success."
"5437","731 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever is called to the judgment of the Messenger to judge between him and his opponent, and does not come, has no right. 4635 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Marwan ibn Ja'far ibn Sa'd ibn Samurah ibn Jundub narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Khubayb ibn Sulayman ibn Samurah ibn Jundub narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Sa'd ibn Samurah ibn Jundub, from Khubayb ibn Sulayman, from his father, from Samurah ibn Jundub: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful. From Samurah ibn Jundub to his sons. As for what follows: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: When a man disputes with another, and one of them calls his fellow to the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to judge between them, and he refuses to come, he has no right. We considered this hadith. The best that occurred to us in it was what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah used to relate to us from Hilal ibn Yahya: that its meaning is that among a man's rights, when a man claims against him before a judge a claim in the absence of the one claimed against, is that he sends to the one claimed against, until he hears the claimant's claim against him, and until the judge hears from him what there is of acknowledgment of it or denial of it. Then the judge does in that what he does in it. If he is called for that and does not respond, that right leaves him, and the judge must appoint an agent for him - that agent being as he had he appointed him to that station. Then he hears a claimant's evidence if he establishes it with him for what he claimed, and judges with it if its integrity is established with him, as he judges with it against him had he been present - except that he keeps him upon his proof if he has any in that, or upon an exit if he has any in it. This is a question of fiqh scholars differed in. Among them are those holding this view: Abu Yusuf and many Basrans. Among them are those who hear no evidence against him in that, nor appoint an agent for him in it, until the one claimed against attends, so there is from him in that what there is of acknowledgment of it or denial. Among those holding that: Abu Hanifah and Muhammad. And among them are those who hear from the..."
"5438","732 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The most modest of people in killing are the people of faith - in its isnad and text. 4636 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ghundar narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mughirah, from Shibak, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most modest of people in killing are the people of faith."
"5439","4637 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, who said: I sat with Alqamah, and Alqamah said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most modest of people in mutilation are the people of faith. Abu Ja'far said: Shu'bah and Abu Awanah differed over Mughirah in this hadith's isnad: Shu'bah inserted Shibak in its isnad between Mughirah and Ibrahim, while Abu Awanah inserted no one between them in it. There is also disagreement over Hushaym in this hadith's isnad from Mughirah."
"5440","4638 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mughirah narrated to us, from Shibak, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The most modest of people in killing are the people of faith. In Hushaym's hadith in Bishr ibn Adam's hadith is agreement with Shu'bah in this hadith's isnad from Mughirah. More than one of Hushaym's companions opposed him in it, reporting it from him in agreement with Abu Awanah in its isnad. Among them is Sa'id ibn Mansur."
"5441","4639 - As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The most modest of people in killing are the people of faith. And among them: Musa ibn Dawud."
"5442","4640 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like. And among them: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Dawlabi."
"5443","4641 - As Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Dhuhli al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Dawlabi narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. And among them: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti."
"5444","4642 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Hunayy ibn Nuwayrah, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. Abu Ja'far said: I heard Ibn Abi Dawud say: I said to Amr ibn Awn: Did Hushaym hear this hadith from Mughirah? He said: Yes; he narrated it to us, saying in it: Mughirah informed us. I never heard mention of Shibak in it ever. And I heard Ibn Abi Dawud say: Hushaym would sometimes mention Shibak in it, except that when he said in it: Mughirah informed us, he did not mention Shibak in it; and when he did not say: Mughirah informed us in it, he mentioned Shibak in it. Abu Ja'far said: It is possible he heard it from Mughirah, sometimes mentioning Shibak in it and sometimes not, so the reports from him in it do not contradict. Then we considered: did any besides Mughirah report it from Ibrahim?"
"5445","We found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah, who said: It is said: The most modest of people in mutilation are the people of faith - not mentioning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it."
"5446","And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: We were with Alqamah in the mosque, and he saw people hurrying toward the palace gate. He said: What is with them? I said - or a man said: Ziyad, or Ibn Ziyad, is mutilating Ibn al-Muka'bar. He said: The best of people in killing is the Muslim - not mentioning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor Abd Allah, in it. We know no one reporting this hadith from Ibrahim besides Mughirah and Mansur. Then we returned to this hadith's text, and found some people demanded in it a meaning, saying: You reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Uraniyyin - what was from them regarding his milch camels, of their killing the herdsman in it and driving them away, and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sending after them until they were caught - that he cut their hands and feet, branded their eyes, and left them in the lava field until they died. So the hadith of Abd Allah you mentioned in this chapter, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), repels what you reported from him in it of what he did with the Uraniyyin. It also opposes what you reported from him besides that."
"5447","4643 - He mentioned what al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from Shaddad ibn Aws, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you kill, do killing well; when you slaughter, do slaughtering well. Let one of you sharpen his blade and rest his slaughtered animal. Since that is what must be observed with other than Adam's sons, observing it in whose killing is lawful of Adam's sons is more deserving. Our proof against him in that was: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did with the Uraniyyin was before the revelation of the brigandage verse. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in that was Allah's ruling. In that deed at that time, it was as among His rulings is stoning married adulterers until killed by that - though they flee, they are followed until their lives are taken. In that is what may allow extended duration. When that is so with married adulterers, it is not objectionable that the punishment in what was from the Uraniyyin of what was from them was what was from them, though duration extended in it until they died. Then Allah returned the ruling in their likes to what He sent down in the brigandage verse. In that is what indicates it is not exceeded beyond it to other than it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutilation, and commanded with what is in the hadith of Shaddad: that Allah's punishments are not exceeded to other than them with what is more than them. By Allah's praise and favor it became clear there is no contradiction in any of these reports. And Allah we ask for success."
"5448","733 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the prices of dogs: their lawfulness and the prohibition of them. 4644 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Nasr ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog."
"5449","4645 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la and al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Qays ibn Habtar, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is unlawful."
"5450","4646 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Abd al-Karim al-Jazari narrated to us, from Qays ibn Habtar, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The price of the dog is unlawful."
"5451","4647 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Mas'ud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog, the dowry of the prostitute, and the fortuneteller's fee."
"5452","4648 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas informed him, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5453","4649 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Mas'ud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are illicit gain: the price of the dog, the dowry of the prostitute, and the fortuneteller's fee."
"5454","4650 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Harun ibn Isma'il al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz, that al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to him: that Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is foul."
"5455","4651 - And Fahd narrated to us: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, Abu Sufyan narrated to me, from Jabir - he affirmed it once, and once doubted about Abu Sufyan - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade the price of the dog and the cat."
"5456","4652 - And al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like, without doubt."
"5457","4653 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Ma'ruf ibn Suwayd al-Judhami informed me that Ulayy ibn Rabah narrated to them that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is not lawful."
"5458","4654 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog and the dowry of the prostitute."
"5459","4655 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The price of the dog is of illicit gain."
"5460","4656 - Fahd narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the dog. These reports we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter forbid the prices of dogs with different wordings. Among them: what forbids them with no cause mentioned in it - which may mean they are unlawful like things made unlawful by the Shari'ah, and may mean it is for baseness in it, though not unlawful - as he forbade the cupper's earning for baseness in it, though not unlawful like other things the Shari'ah made unlawful. For it is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the cupper's earning, his forbidding it."
"5461","4657 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us: that Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', or Rafi' ibn Rifa'ah - the doubt is from them - came to the Ansar's gathering and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the cupper's earning, and commanded us to feed it to our water-carrying camel."
"5462","4658 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah: that Muhayyisah asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the cupper's earning, and he forbade him to eat his earning. He kept reviewing him until he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Feed it to your water-carrying camel, and feed your slave with it."
"5463","4659 - And as Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah al-Harithi, from his father, that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the cupper's earning, then he mentioned the like."
"5464","4660 - And as Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Muhayyisah, one of Banu Harithah, from his father, then he mentioned the like. His forbidding the cupper's earning was not because it is unlawful - do you not see he permitted the asker to feed it to his water-carrying camel and his slave? Had it been unlawful, he would not have permitted him that. Since it was not unlawful, it is reasonable his forbidding him it was for baseness in it, not for other than that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to debase themselves. And among them: what mentions with his forbidding it his making it illicit gain - which may mean like the first meaning, since it is reported from him about the cupper's earning that it is illicit gain, though not because it is unlawful, but because it is base. From what is reported from him in that..."
"5465","4661 - What Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of illicit gain is the cupper's earning. He did not dislike that because it is unlawful, but because it is base. And among them: what mentions with his forbidding it that it is foul - which may mean like the first meaning too, since it is reported from him about the cupper's earning that it is foul."
"5466","4662 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Harun ibn Isma'il al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz, that al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to him that Rafi' ibn Khadij narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper's earning is foul. Abu Ja'far said: That was not because it is unlawful, but because it is base. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade his ummah to debase themselves with things that debase them, though not unlawful for them in his Shari'ah like the unlawfulness of things the Shari'ah made unlawful. It is possible he forbade them the prices of dogs for like this meaning. Then we considered: is anything reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the lawfulness of the prices of useful dogs?"
"5467","4663 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan al-Miqsami informed us, al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the price of the cat and the dog, except the hunting dog. In this hadith is that the dog whose price is forbidden is other than the hunting dog - the dog in which there is no benefit. We reported in Jabir's hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding the price of the cat, the like of what is in it of his forbidding the price of the dog. We know no disagreement among scholars about the cat's price: it is not unlawful, but base. Like it is the dog's price coupled with it in that hadith. It is also possible the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the dog's price meant all dogs, and that was from him at the time he commanded killing dogs, leaving none of them - for he had commanded that, forbidding any of them be left. It is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that..."
"5468","4664 - What Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his voice, commanding the killing of dogs."
"5469","4665 - And what Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs."
"5470","4666 - And what Fahd narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of all dogs. He sent through the regions of Madinah that they be killed."
"5471","4667 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Harun ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, Ibn Bint Abi Rafi' narrated to me, from Abu Rafi', that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the spear to Abu Rafi' and commanded him to kill all the dogs of Madinah, until killing brought him to an old woman's dog. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to kill it."
"5472","4668 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us; and what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad ibn Tahla narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Rafi', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to kill dogs. I went out killing them, seeing no dog but killing it, until I came to such-and-such place - he named it - and there was a dog circling a house. I went to kill it, when a person called me from inside the house: O servant of Allah, what do you want to do? I said: I want to kill this dog. She said: I am a woman in a house beset by beasts, and this dog drives beasts from me, repelling from me what there was. Go to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mention that to him. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded me to kill it."
"5473","4669 - And what Fahd narrated to us: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, that Jibril, peace be upon him, promised the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an hour he would come in. The hour passed and he did not come. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and there was Jibril at the door. He said: What prevents you entering the house? He said: In the house is a dog, and we do not enter a house in which is a dog or an image. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the dog be taken out, then commanded dogs be killed. It is possible his forbidding the prices of dogs was at the time this was their ruling. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted some of them."
"5474","4670 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Amir al-Duba'i narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mughaffal, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the killing of dogs, then said: What is it with me and dogs? Then he permitted the hunting dog and another dog - Sa'id forgot it."
"5475","4671 - And as Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hanzalah ibn Abi Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard Salim ibn Abd Allah say: I heard Ibn Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever keeps a dog, except a trained hunting dog or a livestock dog, two qirats are deducted from his reward every day."
"5476","4672 - And as Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever keeps a dog, except a hunting or livestock dog, two qirats are deducted from his deeds every day."
"5477","4673 - And as Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like."
"5478","4674 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Arim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like."
"5479","4675 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like - except that he said: one qirat."
"5480","4676 - And as Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid informed me that Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Shana'i informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever keeps a dog that does not suffice him for crops nor udders, one qirat is deducted from his deeds every day. Al-Sa'ib said to Sufyan: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Qiblah."
"5481","4677 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, then he mentioned with his isnad the like."
"5482","4678 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, Yazid ibn Khusayfah informed me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like - except that he did not mention al-Sa'ib's saying to Sufyan: Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"5483","4679 - And as al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Hakam, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever keeps a dog other than a crop or hunting dog, two qirats are deducted from his deeds every day."
"5484","4680 - And as al-Husayn narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like - except that he said: except a trained dog or a livestock dog."
"5485","4681 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Bujayr ibn Abi Bujayr, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned dogs and said: Whoever takes a dog that is not a hunting dog or a livestock dog, one qirat is deducted from his reward every day."
"5486","4682 - And as Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to me, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever keeps a dog, one qirat is deducted from his deeds every day - except a field or livestock dog. Abu Ja'far said: What he permitted of them exited from what his forbidding fell upon. By that his forbidding exited from the unlawfulness that had preceded from him in it. Abu Ja'far said: Except that it is reported that the dogs killed in Madinah were neither hunting dogs nor livestock dogs."
"5487","4683 - As Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his voice, commanding the killing of dogs. Dogs were killed, except a hunting or livestock dog."
"5488","4684 - Ibn Shihab said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever keeps a dog that is not a hunting, livestock, or land dog, two qirats are deducted from his reward every day. When we stood upon the differing states of dogs in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - in one state all killed, in a state some killed and the rest not - and what we reported from him of his forbidding their prices may have been in the state in which keeping them is not lawful, and may have been in all states - it was not permitted to carry that upon its being at the time of permitting what was permitted in them, without what necessitates carrying it upon it - especially with our reporting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his excepting from his forbidding dogs' prices the hunting dog. In that is what indicates his forbidding dogs' prices was only in the prices of dogs other than the hunting dog, and other than the dogs whose taking he permitted, upon what we reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports. This is a chapter scholars differed in: a group held all dogs' prices unlawful - among them Malik and al-Shafi'i. A group forbade the prices of what may not lawfully be benefited from of them, and permitted the prices of other than that of what may lawfully be benefited from of them - among them Abu Hanifah and all his companions. It is the more deserving of the two views by analogy with us, since the dogs returned to permissibility - though their meat is not eaten - are returned to the rulings of domestic donkeys whose meat is not eaten. Since domestic donkeys' prices are lawful, the prices of permitted, benefited-from dogs are likewise. And Allah we ask for success."
"5489","734 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what scholars differed in of the meaning of Allah's saying: And do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction - of what is reported from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the cause in which its revelation was, and of what some of them interpreted it upon. 4685 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, Aslam Abu Imran narrated to me, he said: We were at Constantinople, with Uqbah ibn Amir over the Egyptians and a man over the Syrians. A great rank of Romans came out of the city, and we lined up against them. A Muslim man charged the Romans until he entered among them, then came out to us. People shouted to him: Glory be to Allah - he threw himself with his hand into destruction. Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), stood and said: O people, you interpret this verse upon this interpretation. It was only sent down about us, the Ansar: when Allah strengthened His religion and its supporters multiplied, we said among ourselves to one another, secretly from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Our wealth has been wasted; if we stayed in it and repaired what was wasted of it. So Allah revealed in His Book, refuting what we intended: And spend in Allah's way, and do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction. So destruction was in the staying we wanted: to stay in our wealth and repair it. We were commanded to raid. Abu Ayyub never ceased raiding in Allah's way until Allah took him. In this hadith is that the destruction mentioned in this verse is destruction in religion. Destruction and perishing are one in Arab speech - thus Wallad the grammarian narrated to us, from al-Masadiri, from Abu Ubaydah. Its meaning was: whoever reaches the state of abandoning raiding and refraining from spending in Allah's way, as the Ansar were upon then intended otherwise, is doom. Like it is what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)..."
"5490","4686 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear a man say: People are doomed, he is the most doomed of them."
"5491","4687 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. That was upon doom in religion, not in other than it. Then we considered what is reported from the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the meaning of this verse with them, of what they did not mention in it that its revelation was in it, as Abu Ayyub mentioned in his hadith we mentioned from him."
"5492","We found Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyaynah say: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays, who said: A man said to Umar - his maternal uncle having been killed: O Commander of the Faithful, people claim my uncle is among those who threw themselves with their hands into destruction. He said: Rather he is among those who sell the life of this world for the Hereafter. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith does not mention the cause for which what was said about his uncle was said - except that we know with certainty it is among the causes of fighting in Allah's way."
"5493","And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, that a man said to al-Bara: If I charge a battalion of a thousand with the sword, is that of destruction? He said: No; destruction is that a man commits a sin, then throws himself with his hands, saying: I will not be forgiven."
"5494","And we found Muhammad ibn Zakariyya Abu Shurayh and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas about: And spend in Allah's way, and do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction. He said: Spend in Allah's way, and do not withhold spending in Allah's way, lest you perish."
"5495","And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, who said: He spends in Allah's way though he has nothing but an arrowhead. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning he spends in Allah's way from little wealth as he spends from much of it - as warning him not to leave that, lest he enter the threat we mentioned."
"5496","And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Sulayman, from Abu Wa'il, who said: Hudhayfah said in interpreting this verse, about spending. Shu'bah said: I narrated it to Yunus, and he said: May Allah have mercy on al-Hasan - he said nothing but I found for it a basis."
"5497","And we found Fahd narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Shayban the grammarian narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Salih freedman of Umm Hani, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying: And spend in Allah's way, and do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction. He said: Let none of you say: I am doomed, finding nothing. If he finds nothing but an arrowhead, let him strive with it in Allah's way. All those from whom we reported these reports inform that the destruction mentioned in the verse we recited is not in meeting the enemy with fighting, when the one meeting them has no obedience with him securing him from their killing him - and that in his doing that he is not blameworthy. An objector said: How do you accept this when you reported in this verse's interpretation its contrary?"
"5498","He mentioned what Fahd ibn Sulayman and Harun ibn Kamil together narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth al-Zuhri informed him that they besieged Damascus. A man of Azd Shanu'ah rushed alone toward the enemy, facing them. The Muslims faulted him for that and raised his report to Amr ibn al-As, who was over a corps of the corps. Amr sent to him, turned him back, and said to him: Allah says: Indeed Allah loves those who fight in His way in ranks, as though a compact structure. And He said: And do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction. This is Amr ibn al-As placing meeting the enemy as that man sought to meet them among destruction. Our answer to him in that was that what was from Amr has no report about the cause in which the verse was sent down. The hadith of Abu Ayyub has the report about the cause in which it was sent down; in Abu Ayyub's report is the authoritative fixing of the cause in which it was sent down. They did not know its revelation nor the cause intended by its revelation in it except from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by his reciting it to them and informing them the cause in which it was sent down. Amr ibn al-As's saying in his hadith we reported from him may have been his interpretation of it, which was broad for him, since it bore what he interpreted it upon. Had he stood upon what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposing that, he would have held to it, returned his interpretation to it, and not said in its interpretation its contrary. Whoever seeks in fighting the enemy, interpreted in this hadith of Amr of what he seeks thereby harming the enemy - his fellow is praised for it. And Allah knows best what Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, intended in the hadith we reported from him in this chapter, until he recited for it the verse he recited: Those who sell the life of this world for the Hereafter - the noblest of ranks and highest. And there was from Ja'far ibn Abi Talib on the day of Mu'tah the like of that."
"5499","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, who said: My foster father - one of Banu Murrah - narrated to me, he said: He attended Mu'tah with Ja'far ibn Abi Talib and his companions, may Allah be pleased with them. I saw Ja'far when fighting engaged him: he charged upon his reddish horse, then hamstrung it, and fought the people until killed. He was the first man to hamstring in Allah's way that day. Abu Ja'far said: That was from him in the presence of the remaining commanders with him - in the presence of Abd Allah ibn Rawahah, and in the presence of who succeeded him in fighting: Khalid ibn al-Walid, whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised and named for that the Sword of Allah - and in the presence of other Muslims besides them, without their denying it of him. From what we know with certainty: it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his deed, and he did not deny it of him, nor forbid Muslims its like. That indicates this deed is among the noblest deeds, its reward among the greatest rewards from Allah - and that this verse's interpretation we recited is as we reported from Abu Ayyub in its interpretation, not as other than it opposing that. And Allah we ask for success."
"5500","735 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Urwah ibn Mudarris: whoever does not attain the standing at Jam' has no Hajj. 4688 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah ibn Mudarris al-Ta'i, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I exhausted myself and wore out my mount. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever attains Jam' with the imam standing, stands with the imam, then pours forth with people, has attained Hajj. Whoever does not attain it has no Hajj. Abu Ja'far said: This meaning - that whoever misses the standing at Jam' has no Hajj - we know no one bringing it in this hadith from al-Sha'bi besides Mutarrif. As for the group of al-Sha'bi's companions, they do not mention it in it - among them Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid."
"5501","4689 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Safar and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah ibn Mudarris, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Jam' and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there Hajj for me? I wore out my mount. He said: Whoever prayed with us this prayer, stood with us before that, and poured forth from Arafah by night or day, his Hajj is complete and his ritual impurity ended."
"5502","4690 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah ibn Mudarris, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. And among them: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah and Dawud ibn Abi Hind."
"5503","4691 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi; and Zakariyya from al-Sha'bi; and Dawud ibn Abi Hind from al-Sha'bi, who said: I heard Urwah ibn Mudarris ibn Aws ibn Harithah ibn Lam al-Ta'i say: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Muzdalifah and said: O Messenger of Allah, I came from the two mountains of Tayy. By Allah, I came only having exhausted myself and worn out my mount, leaving no mountain of these mountains but I stood upon it. Is there Hajj for me? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever witnessed with us this prayer - the dawn prayer at Muzdalifah - having stood at Arafah before that by night or day, his Hajj is complete and his ritual impurity ended. Sufyan said: Zakariyya added in it - he was the most retentive of the three for this hadith - he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I came this hour from the two mountains of Tayy, having exhausted my mount and tired myself. Is there Hajj for me? He said: Whoever witnessed with us this prayer and stood with us until we pour forth, having stood before that at Arafah by night or day, his Hajj is complete and his ritual impurity ended. Sufyan said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind added, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when dawn flashed, then he mentioned the hadith."
"5504","4692 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Zakariyya narrated to us, from Amir, who said: Urwah ibn Mudarris ibn Aws ibn Harithah ibn Lam narrated to us that he performed Hajj in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), attaining people only at night at Jam'. He went to Arafat at night, poured forth, then returned to Jam'. He came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I worked myself and wore out my mount. What great part of Hajj is for me? He said: Whoever prayed with us the morning prayer at Jam', stood with us until we pour forth, having poured forth from Arafat before that by night or day, his Hajj is complete and his ritual impurity ended. And among them: Mujalid ibn Sa'id al-Hamdani."
"5505","4693 - As Umar ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi' al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah ibn Mudarris, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Jam' - meaning Muzdalifah - and said: O Messenger of Allah, I exhausted myself and wore out my mount. No mountain of Arafah's mountains remains but I stood upon it. Is there Hajj for me? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Whoever prayed with us this prayer of ours, having come to Arafah before that by night or day, his Hajj is complete and his ritual impurity ended. Abu Ja'far said: We considered this meaning Mutarrif added, from al-Sha'bi, over al-Sha'bi's companions in this hadith - after standing upon that the jurists of the cities around whom fatwa turns, in the Two Sanctuaries and other cities besides them, do not differ that whoever misses the standing at Jam', having stood at Arafah before that, is not in the ruling of one whose Hajj is missed; he attained Hajj, missing of it what blood suffices for - except a small-numbered group of them holding that whoever misses the standing at Jam' in his Hajj after dawn rises, his Hajj is missed. They made missing the standing at Jam' before dawn rise like missing the standing at Arafah in Hajj, until dawn rises. We know no one before them reported holding this view besides Alqamah ibn Qays. We found that meaning may bear what the people of that view carried it upon, and may bear other than that: what is meant by it is emphasis and stressing regarding lagging from Muzdalifah. What is said in that of what is in that hadith is like what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we reported in what preceded in this book of ours of his saying: There is no faith for one with no trustworthiness, no religion for one with no covenant. That was not from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that one with no trustworthiness is out of faith, into its opposite; rather he is in faith below the faith with whose people is trustworthiness. Likewise his saying: No religion for one with no covenant - he did not mean he has no religion, but meant: he has no religion like the religion with one who has covenant. Like that is what is in Mutarrif's hadith, of what we mentioned: his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever does not attain it has no Hajj, may mean: he has no Hajj like the Hajj of one attaining that prayer with him. We found what indicated that to us by inference and derivation: we found the standing at Arafah of Hajj's core - Hajj does not suffice without attaining it, nor is complete but with it. No one is excused leaving it, with excuse or without. Jam' was otherwise, for we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted his wife Sawdah to pour forth from it before standing..."
"5506","4694 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim informed us, from his father, from A'ishah, who said: Sawdah was a slow, heavy woman. She sought the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to pour forth from Jam' before standing, and he permitted her. I wished I had sought his permission and he permitted me. Like that is what was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we reported in what preceded in this book of ours, of his sending the weak of his household forward from Jam' by night. Since the standing at Jam' is of what is lifted by excuse, unlike the standing at Arafah which is not lifted by excuse or otherwise, we understood that what is lifted by excuse is not of Hajj's core; its like is like tawaf: of it, the tawaf of visitation is obligatory, indispensable for the pilgrim, its obligation not lifted from him by excuse or otherwise - unlike the farewell tawaf, lifted from the menstruating woman, excused by menstruation in leaving it. In what we mentioned is sound proof that the standing at Jam', since it falls by excuse in some state from the pilgrim, indicates it is not of Hajj's core; it is of what blood suffices in, as suffices in leaving tawaf between Safa and Marwah. And success is from Allah."
"5507","736 - Chapter explaining the problematic report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rulings of lost property. 4695 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us; and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us; they said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir; and Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, Isa ibn Yunus informed us - then they all joined, saying: al-Walid ibn Kathir al-Makhzumi informed us - Isa said: he was trustworthy in hadith - from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Asim and Amr, the two sons of Sufyan ibn Abd Allah, that Sufyan ibn Abd Allah found a purse and brought it to Umar. He said: Announce it a year. If recognized, so be it; otherwise it is yours. It was not recognized. I met him the next year and mentioned it to him. He said: It is yours; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us with that. He said: I have no need of it. Umar took it and placed it in the public treasury."
"5508","4696 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abu Ubaydah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar al-Kufi informed us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Amr and Asim, the two sons of Sufyan ibn Abd Allah ibn Rabi'ah, from their father, that he picked up a purse, then he mentioned the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is Umar's informing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said about lost property: it belongs to the picker after the year he announces it in, when he finds no one recognizing it. We considered the meaning of his saying in that: is it upon conferring ownership of it to him, or not? We found reported from Umar in that of what he said in it after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)..."
"5509","What Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Mu'awiyah ibn Abd Allah ibn Badr al-Juhani, that his father informed him that he stopped at a stop on the Syrian road, and found a purse with eighty dinars. He mentioned it to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said to him: Announce it at mosques' doors, and mention it to whoever comes from Syria, a year. When a year passes, do with it as you wish."
"5510","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Abd Allah ibn Zayd, from his father, that he brought Umar a purse with a thousand dirhams. He said: I announced it and found no one recognizing it. Umar said to him: Announce it a year. If you find its owner, otherwise enjoy it. Malik and Shu'bah differed over Ayyub ibn Musa in the name of the man they both narrated from him this hadith, and in his father's name: each in his narration from him said what we mentioned in his narration from him. Allah knows best the correctness in that, what it is. What in this hadith agreed with what is in Sufyan ibn Abd Allah's hadith we reported before it. Then we found from Umar about lost property's ruling after the year what is more deserving than this..."
"5511","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Shayban informed us, from Abu Nawfal al-Urayji, from his father, he said: I found a purse containing wealth, so I announced it and found no one who recognized it, so I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: I found a purse and announced it, and found no one who recognizes it, so he said: Announce it for a year; if you find one who recognizes it, hand it to him, otherwise bring it to me at the end of the year. He said: So I announced it for a year and found no one who recognized it, so I came to him and informed him, and said: Relieve me of it, O Commander of the Believers. He said: I will not do so. I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Commander of the Believers, relieve me of it. He said: I will not do so, but if you wish I will tell you the way out of it. I said: What is the way out of it? He said: If you wish, give it in charity; if its owner comes, you give him the choice between having the reward, and if he refuses, you repay him his wealth, and the reward is yours. Abu Jafar said: This Abu Nawfal al-Urayji is the son of Abu Aqrab of Kinanah of Quraysh, and his name is Muawiyah ibn Muslim ibn Amr ibn Abu Aqrab; thus said Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Yahya ibn Main, while others said otherwise. His father had accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is of the people of Makkah, except that he moved from it and settled in Basrah. Abu Nawfal narrated from Ibn Abbas, and Shuabah is among those who narrated from him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from Umar is the clarification of the ruling of the lost item after announcement, and that it is giving it in charity. And the correction of what was reported from him among what we mentioned from him in this chapter is that what is meant by his saying, otherwise it is yours, is not by way of granting ownership of it, but rather it is yours to dispose of it in what it must be disposed in. This is what we found from Umar concerning the rulings of the lost item after the year. And there has been reported from Ali ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning that something that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding a lost item Ali found in his time, though the hadith mentioning that is of interrupted chain, which in our view cannot be used as proof, but what prompted us to bring it is that al-Shafii has used it as proof against us in our preventing the finder from consuming it after the year if he is free of need of it."
"5512","4697 - And it is what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abu Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, that he said: Ali ibn Abu Talib found a dinar and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: I found this. He said: Announce it. So as long as Allah willed passed, then he said: I have announced it and found no one who recognizes it. He said: Then do with it as you wish. He said: So I went and pawned it for three dirhams for food and fat; and while it was so, its owner came seeking it, and he recognized it, so Ali came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: This is the owner of the dinar. He said: Pay it to him. So Ali paid it to him after they had eaten from it. Al-Shafii said: So in this hadith is proof that the lost item is lawful for the finder after the year, even if he is free of need of it, because if it returned to charity it would not have been lawful for Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, because he is of the core of Banu Hashim, and charity is unlawful for him. So our answer to him concerning that was: This is an interrupted hadith, the like of which cannot be used as proof, especially as one of its narrators is Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abu Namir, about whose narration the people of chains say what they say; and if your opponent used the like of this as proof against you, you would not allow it to him, so how is it lawful for you to use it as proof against your opponent? And what is correct in our view from Ali ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning the ruling of the lost item after the year is like what we reported concerning it from Umar. As Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shuabah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Asim ibn Damrah, he said: A man came to Ali ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I found a purse of dirhams and announced it, and found no one who recognizes it. He said: Give it in charity; if its owner comes and is pleased, the reward is his, otherwise you guarantee it, and the reward is yours. And there has been reported from Ubayy ibn Kab, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the ruling of the lost item after the year."
"5513","4698 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shuabah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Suwayd ibn Ghafalah say: I went out for Hajj and found a whip, so I took it. Zayd ibn Suhan said: Leave it. I said: I will not leave it for the beasts; I will take it and benefit from it. Then I met Ubayy ibn Kab and mentioned that to him, and he said: You did well. I found a purse in it one hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: Announce it for a year. So I announced it for a year and found no one who recognized it. Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Announce it for a year. So I announced it for a year and found no one who recognized it. Then I came to him the third time, and he said: Announce it for a year. So I announced it for a year and found no one who recognized it. He said: Memorize its number and its container; if its owner comes, otherwise enjoy it. Shuabah said: Then Salamah doubted, and does not know whether it was three years or one year. Salamah said: This hadith pleased me, so I said it to Abu Sadiq, and he said: I heard it from Ubayy as you heard it from Suwayd."
"5514","4699 - And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: I went out for Hajj and found a whip, so I took it. Zayd ibn Suhan said: Leave it. I said: I will not leave it for the beasts; I will take it and benefit from it. Then I met Ubayy ibn Kab and mentioned that to him, and he said: You did well. I found a purse in it one hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I took it and mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Announce it for a year; if you find one who recognizes it, hand it to him, otherwise benefit from it."
"5515","4700 - And what Ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us: Abu Mamar narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Ubayy ibn Kab, he said: I picked up one hundred dinars in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. He said: Announce it. So I announced it a year, then I came to him and said: I announced it a year and found no one who recognizes it. He said: Announce it a year. So I announced it a year and found no one who recognizes it. Then I came to him and said: I announced it a year and found no one who recognizes it. He said: Announce it. So I announced it a year and found no one who recognizes it. Then I came to him and said: I have announced it a year and found no one who recognizes it. He said: Memorize its number and its tie and enjoy it. Al-Shafii, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And Ubayy ibn Kab had been among the most well-off of the people of Madinah. And our answer to him concerning that was: The well-off state of Ubayy ibn Kab that was mentioned was only after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); as for before that, he had been poor. And the proof of that is."
"5516","4701 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Thumamah, he said: Anas said: Abu Talhah had a land, so he made it for Allah, mighty and majestic, then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Place it among the poor of your relatives. So he gave it to Hassan ibn Thabit and Ubayy, Ubayy said, from Thumamah, from Anas, and they were closer to him than me. Abu Jafar said: So we understood thereby that there is no proof for one who holds concerning the lost item after the year what al-Shafii holds concerning it, in this hadith of Ubayy. And there has been reported from others than those we mentioned among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the lost item after the year the like of what we reported concerning it from Umar and Ali. Among them: Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5517","As Fahd narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Amir, and he is Ibn Shaqiq, from Abu Wail, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, bought a servant for seven hundred dirhams, then sought its owner and did not find him, so he announced it for a year, and did not find its owner, so he gathered the poor and began giving them, saying: O Allah, on behalf of its owner; if he comes, it is on my behalf, and the price is upon me. Then he said: Thus is done with the stray lost item."
"5518","And among them: Abd Allah ibn Abbas. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Ibn Abu Dhib narrated to us, from al-Mundhir ibn Abu al-Mundhir, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas with a purse of musk, and said: I found this. Ibn Abbas said: Announce it; if you find its owner, otherwise give it in charity; if its owner comes, give him the choice between the reward and compensation."
"5519","And among them: Abu Hurayrah. As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas conveyed to me, from Ali ibn Mabad. And as Ibrahim ibn Sulayman narrated to me: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abu Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah concerning the man who finds the lost item, he said: He announces it; if he does not find its owner, he gives it in charity; if its owner comes, he gives him the choice: if he wishes, the reward is his, and if he wishes, he gives him the price, and the reward is his."
"5520","And among them: Abd Allah ibn Umar. As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abu Unaysah, from al-Hurr ibn al-Sayyah, he said: While I was sitting with Ibn Umar, a man came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I have found this garment, and I have announced it a year, and found no one who recognizes it, and this is the day of Tarwiyah and the people disperse. He said: Announce it in the season at Arafat until the people depart. He said: What if no one recognizes it, what do I do with it? Abd Allah ibn Umar said to him: Value it at a just value and give it in charity if you wish, and you are liable whenever its owner comes seeking it; if he takes its price from you, the reward is yours, and if he wishes his reward to be his, he lets it continue for its purpose. And if you wish, value it at a just value and wear it, and you are liable for it whenever its owner comes seeking it, you pay him its value; and if no seeker comes for it, it is yours if you wish. Abu Jafar said: And what we found from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whom we mentioned in this chapter concerning the ruling of the lost item after the year is the command to give it in charity, except what is in this hadith of Ibn Umar of his permitting its finder to wear it if he wishes. That is among what may possibly be permission for that due to harm he saw in him indicating his need, so permission to wear it for that. So how can anyone differ from these, especially as among them is one who heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning it what we reported from him in this chapter, then said it is this saying we mentioned from him, for it is among what we know with certainty that he did not depart in what he said of that from what he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say concerning it. If one arguing adduces in that the hadith of Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani which."
"5521","4702 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas and Sufyan al-Thawri informed me that Rabiah ibn Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to them, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him, and asked him about the lost item. He said: Know its container and its tie, then announce it a year; if its owner comes, otherwise it is yours to deal with. The answer to him concerning that was: What we mentioned in our interpretation of the hadith of Umar and the hadith of Ubayy ibn Kab suffices us from repeating it here as an answer to what he asked about. And among those who held concerning the lost item what we have selected in this chapter, of disliking its consumption after the year in which he announces it for its finder unless he is in need of it, is Abu Hanifah and the rest of his companions. And Allah is the Grantor of success."
"5522","737 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his prohibition of the lost item of the pilgrim. 4703 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, Ibn Abu Hazim narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the lost item of the pilgrim. Abu Jafar said: So we considered this hadith to ascertain the meaning intended by it, Allah willing. The best that occurred to us concerning that, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in it, is that Hajj gathers people of different lands who disperse from their Hajj to their homelands, then perhaps they never meet after that. So one who finds what fell from them, if what predominates in his heart is that he cannot reach its owner, was commanded not to take up its lost item, fearing it remain in his liability until he meets his Lord with it; and that it is unlike the lost item whose owner he hopes to meet to hand it to him, his taking it being to preserve it for him, not for other than that. And Allah is the Grantor of success."
"5523","738 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the lost item of Makkah. 4704 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, and Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, both saying: Abu Yusuf informed us, from Yazid ibn Abu Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning Makkah: None shall pick up its lost item except one who announces it."
"5524","4705 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal and Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said concerning Makkah: None shall pick up its lost item except one who announces it. And this hadith has been reported with wording other than this wording."
"5525","4706 - As Bakkar narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us, Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abu Kathir narrated to us; and as Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abu Kathir; then both together said: From Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning Makkah: Its stray shall not be picked up except by one who announces it."
"5526","4707 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us: Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, Ibn al-Darawardi informed us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, in what I was told from him, used to say: Their meanings differ. As for the meaning of none shall pick up its lost item except one who announces, i.e., whoever sees a lost item there, his way is to pick it up with his hand, then say: Whose is this among you, O people? And the meaning of his saying, its lost item shall not be picked up except for one who announces, is that one who sees its lost item may not take it, unless he hears a man saying: Who found such-and-such agreeing with what he saw, so he picks it up with his hand, then says: Is it this? So we considered what we reported in this chapter and what al-Nadr ibn Shumayl said concerning it. We found what he said correct, and in that was what indicated what is in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman which we reported in the preceding chapter, of avoiding the lost item of the pilgrim, and that it is unlike the lost item whose owner the one attempting to pick it up hopes to meet to deliver it to him, and that it is unlike other lost items in which he does not hope for that. And Allah is the Grantor of success."
"5527","739 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to the people regarding the necklace of his daughter Zaynab when he saw it among the wealth gathered for ransoming the captives: If you see fit to release her captive to her and return to her what is hers, then do so. 4708 - Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Yahya al-Shajari narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Aishah, she said: When the people of Makkah sent concerning ransoming their captives, Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sent concerning ransoming her husband Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi, and she sent therein a necklace of hers which Khadijah had given her when he married her. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the necklace, he was moved with intense tenderness until his eyes shed tears, and said: If you see fit to release her captive to her, and to return to her what is hers, then do so. They said: O Messenger of Allah, may our fathers and mothers be ransom for you. So they released him and returned to her what was hers. Then someone said: What need was there in this for them, when the favor in that belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not to them. Do you not see the hadith of Jubayr ibn Mutim when he spoke to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning them, and an elder said: Had he come to me, meaning his father al-Mutim ibn Adi, I would have released them to him. And we have reported this hadith in what preceded of this book of ours. And our answer to him concerning that was: What was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Jubayr was only at the time when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had the right to kill them, and to him belonged favoring them by sparing their killing. And what was in the hadith of Aishah was only after their ransom had protected their blood, and what they ransomed with became wealth whose ruling is the ruling of spoils which became for those who advanced upon it what they have therein. So it was not proper to release their wealth from it except with what their souls consented to. And it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) referred that to an aspect of the spoils by compensating its people to whom he directed that what he saw fit to compensate them from those spoils, until it settles in its entirety in the places where it must settle. And Allah is the Grantor of success."
"5528","740 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the place in which women observe itikaf. 4709 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended itikaf, prayed Fajr, then entered the place in which he intended to observe itikaf. He intended to observe itikaf in the last ten, so he commanded and a tent was pitched for him, and Aishah commanded and a tent was pitched for her, and Hafsah commanded and a tent was pitched for her. When Zaynab saw their two tents, she commanded a tent and it was pitched for her. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned in the evening, he said: What is this? Is it righteousness you intend?! So he did not observe itikaf in Ramadan and observed itikaf ten days of Shawwal."
"5529","4710 - And al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, she narrated to him, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended itikaf, so Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, sought his permission to observe itikaf with him, and he permitted her, so she pitched her tent. Hafsah asked her to seek permission for her to observe itikaf with him. When Zaynab saw that, she pitched along with them, and she was a jealous woman. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw their tents and said: What is this? Is it righteousness you intend?! So he left itikaf until he broke fast from Ramadan, then he observed itikaf in ten of Shawwal."
"5530","4711 - And Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us; and al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us; Yunus said in his hadith: Malik informed him, and al-Rabi said in his hadith: He said: I heard Malik narrating; then both together said: From Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and they did not mention Aishah in their two hadiths."
"5531","4712 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, from al-Awzai, Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, Amrah narrated to me, from Aishah, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports is the intention of those among his wives who intended itikaf with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, and the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those among them whom he permitted in that. This is a chapter of jurisprudence in which the people of knowledge differed. One group among them says: Women observe itikaf in the mosques as men observe itikaf, and it is not lawful for them to observe itikaf in other than them; this is the saying of the jurists of the Hijaz. Another group among them says: Rather they observe itikaf in the mosques of their houses, and do not observe itikaf in other than them among the congregational mosques as men observe itikaf; among those who held that was Abu Hanifah and his companions. So we considered this hadith: Is there in it proof for what the Hijazis held of what we mentioned from them of what they held in this chapter, or not? We found what is in it of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted therein to those among his wives whom he permitted. We found that was only upon an itikaf of theirs with him therein. We have seen women travel with their husbands and with others than them among their mahrams to distant journeys, while it is not lawful for them to do that with others than their husbands and others than their mahrams. So it is possible that what was allowed thereby to those among his wives whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted in itikaf in the mosque was due to his being with them therein by right of marriage between them and him; and it is possible that it was due to their inviolability to all Muslims other than him, so itikaf in the mosque was thereby allowed to them, and that was not allowed to others than them who are otherwise. Thereby it is negated that there be in this hadith proof for what the Hijazis adduced of what we mentioned from them. And we looked thereafter into the rulings of women after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding coming to the mosques."
"5532","4713 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seen what women innovated after him, he would have prevented them from the mosques, as the women of Banu Israil were prevented. Abu Jafar said: So the saying of Aishah in this, she being trusted in what she said, along with her knowledge, understanding and vigilance, indicated that women only had coming to the mosques in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as allowed, for a state they were upon, and they departed from it after him to its opposite. Thereby what had been allowed to them of their coming to them as they used to come in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was negated. And when they were so in the lifetime of Aishah, they were after her death further from that. And when that was so, we understood that if they are to observe itikaf, it is only from them in other than the mosques, not in the mosques. And with Allah is success."
"5533","741 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his command to the finder to call witnesses over what he picked up. 4714 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashii, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever picks up a lost item, let him call two just persons to witness, and not conceal nor alter it; if its owner comes, he is most entitled to it, otherwise it is the wealth of Allah which He gives to whom He wills."
"5534","4715 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid, and he is al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif, from Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashii, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes a lost item, let him call two just persons to witness, and let him preserve its container and its tie, and not conceal nor alter it; if its owner comes, he is most entitled to it, otherwise it is the wealth of Allah which He gives to whom He wills. Abu Jafar said: Thus we found this hadith from the two narrations of Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar and Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Khalid al-Hadhdha. And we found it from the narration of Shuabah, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, with an addition to that."
"5535","4716 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, Shuabah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif, from Iyad ibn Himar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever picks up a lost item, let him call one just person or two just persons to witness, then not conceal nor hide it; if its owner comes, he is most entitled to it, otherwise it is the wealth of Allah which He gives to whom He wills. Abu Jafar said: And what is in this hadith, let him call one just person or two just persons to witness, is in our view, and Allah knows best, upon the doubt of Shuabah in what he heard from Khalid concerning that, because he used to narrate from memory, and memory may contain the like of this. Hushaym also used to narrate from memory, and the like of this is known from his memory. Abd al-Aziz, his hadith was from his book. So what the two reported in our view of that is more worthy than what Shuabah reported therein, because two are more worthy of preservation than one. Then we found this hadith from the narration of Hammad ibn Salamah, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, differing from what we mentioned before it in its chain, and falling short in its text from them."
"5536","4717 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Iyad ibn Himar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the lost item, and said: It is announced, and not hidden nor concealed; if its owner comes, otherwise it is the wealth of Allah which He gives to whom He wills. And we found with Hammad ibn Salamah also this hadith with the like of this meaning in its text, from Abu Hurayrah."
"5537","4718 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Musa ibn Ismail informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This is a chapter of jurisprudence in which its people differed. One group among them says: If the finder leaves calling witnesses over the lost item when he picks it up, though he only picked it up to preserve it for its owner and to return it if he finds him, his hand over it is a liable hand, and its compensation to its owner is upon him if it is lost from his hand; and if he called witnesses when he picked it up upon that, his hand over it was a trustworthy hand, with no liability upon him therein if it is lost from his hand; among those who said that among them was Abu Hanifah. Another group among them says: His hand over it is a trustworthy hand, whether he called witnesses when he picked it up upon what we mentioned he should call witnesses upon, or did not call witnesses upon that, when he only picked it up intending thereby preserving it for its owner and delivering it when able to him; among those who said that were Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. So we considered what they differed in of that, and the worthier of the two schools we mentioned therein in our view was what the second group of the two groups we mentioned said, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only commanded taking the lost item to preserve it for its owner and to return it to him, and that is among what the reality of what the finder is upon thereof cannot be reached, nor known except by his saying; and because it is possible that he calls witnesses that his taking it is to return it to its owner and to preserve it for him, while in reality it is otherwise. And since what we mentioned is so, we understood that what is referred to in what the finder takes the lost item upon, of what makes him liable and what makes him trusted, is what he is in reality upon thereof, and what none of the creatures other than him knows of him. Thereby what this group said concerning that was established, and what the other group said therein was negated. One imagining, among those to whom this hadith came upon what Shuabah reported it of mentioning two just persons or one just person, imagined that that was only intended as proof for the owner of the lost item, if the finder or another than him to whose hand it passes disputes it with him, so those whom the finder called to witness over it among just persons testify for him over it, so he deserves it thereby, or from one just person so he swears with him and deserves it thereby; and he mentioned that that is proof for judging with the oath along with the witness. So we considered what he said of that and found it corrupt, because the calling to witness which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded therein, if it was for what he mentioned and was not upon the doubt of Shuabah in what he reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, there was in it shortcoming toward the owner of the lost item in what he reaches his lost item with when disputed over it, since he may be an unpubescent child, or a mukatab not yet freed, so he is among those who cannot swear with his witness and have judged for him what he seeks. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the furthest of people from shortcoming in anything by his saying, or in leaving commanding calling two just persons to witness in that. So commanding calling two just persons to witness from one who may not be proof in that of what this imaginer mentioned, and in what we mentioned is the negation of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and the hadith returns upon what Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar and Hushaym ibn Bashir reported therein. And Allah we ask for success."
"5538","742 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the rulings of stray animals. 4719 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us: Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Mundhir, from Mundhir, and he is Ibn Jarir, he said: We were in al-Bawazij and the cattle went out in the evening, and Jarir saw among them a cow he did not recognize, so he said to the herdsman: What is this cow? He said: A cow that joined the cattle; I do not know whose it is. So Jarir commanded regarding it, and it was driven away until it disappeared, then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None shelters the stray except one astray. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith was the informing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people that none shelters the stray except one astray, and Jarir acted after him upon what he, peace be upon him, said regarding the cow that joined his cattle. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) warning regarding strays, and his informing the people that they are the burning of the Fire."
"5539","4720 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Ala Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abu Muslim al-Jadhmi, from al-Jarud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire."
"5540","4721 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Yazid, brother of Mutarrif, from Abu Muslim al-Jadhmi, from al-Jarud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"5541","4722 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said, meaning al-Qattan, narrated to us, Humayd narrated to me, al-Hasan narrated to us, from Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, from his father, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of Banu Amir, and he said: Shall I not carry you? We said: We find stray camels on the road. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire."
"5542","4723 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, Shuabah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while we were upon lean camels, and said: O Messenger of Allah, we pass by al-Jurf and find camels and ride them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire."
"5543","4724 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, Abu Usamah informed us, from Sufyan, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah, from Mutarrif, from al-Jarud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire, so do not approach it."
"5544","4725 - And what Ahmad narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Bazi informed us, from Yazid, meaning Ibn Zuray, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif, from Abu Muslim al-Jadhmi, thus he said: From al-Jarud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stray of the Muslim is the burning of the Fire, so do not approach it, three times. Abu Jafar said: Then we considered whether there is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates whether that saying applies in all cases or in a specific among them."
"5545","4726 - We found Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Bakr ibn Sawadah informed him, from Abu Salim al-Jayshani, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shelters a stray, he is astray, so long as he does not announce it."
"5546","4727 - And we found Fahd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bakr ibn Sawadah informed him, from Abu Salim al-Jayshani, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. So we understood thereby that the sheltering which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in the first hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, and in the second hadiths we mentioned after it, is only the sheltering with which there is no announcement. And we understood by this hadith that the sheltering with which there is announcement is praiseworthy on the part of its doer, and that he therein is unlike the astray one whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made astray by sheltering the stray. So we considered: Do we find it reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than these reports?"
"5547","4728 - We found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas and Sufyan al-Thawri informed me that Rabiah ibn Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to them, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him, and asked him about the lost item. He said: Know its container and its tie, then announce it a year; if its owner comes, otherwise it is yours to deal with. He said: Then the stray of sheep? He said: It is for you or for your brother or for the wolf. He said: Then the stray of camels? He said: With it are its water-skin and its shoes; it comes to water and eats trees until its owner meets it."
"5548","4729 - And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, both saying: Said ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, al-Qaqa ibn Hakim narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was asked about the stray of sheep. He said: It is for you or for your brother or for the wolf. And he was asked about the stray of camels, and said: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its shoes; leave it until its owner finds it."
"5549","4730 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith and Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that a man of Muzaynah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him: What do you think about the stray of sheep? He said: Eaten food for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf; detain your brother's stray for him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you think about the stray of camels? He said: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its shoes, and the wolf is not feared for it; it eats herbage and comes to water until its seeker comes. Abu Jafar said: So in what we reported of the two hadiths of Abd Allah ibn Amr and Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani was what indicated the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to take what he permitted taking of the strays found. That taking, upon the authentication of the hadith of Abu Salim from Zayd ibn Khalid, is the taking with which there is announcement, not other than it. And therein regarding the stray of camels was what its apparent sense is contrary to that, because therein is: What have you to do with it? With it are its water-skin and its shoes; it comes to water and eats trees until its owner meets it. So it is possible that that was only commanded regarding the stray of camels because no fear is upon it, so leaving it was thereby allowed for one who finds it, since there is no fear upon it, so taking it is allowed for him on account of it. Then we found what may be feared for it other than the wolf, of what is feared for it from blameworthy hands which are not trusted with it, nor known thereby if it falls into them; while the hands which it does not fear, trusted with it and known thereby after taking it, are otherwise. And what is in the hadith of Abu Salim from Zayd ibn Khalid permits taking it to announce it and to return it to its owner whenever able to him, because this hadith of Zayd ibn Khalid does not differentiate between strays of camels and strays of other than them. And there has been reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicated this meaning."
"5550","As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar narrating that Thabit ibn al-Dahhak narrated to him that he found a camel, and mentioned it to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he commanded him to announce it. He said: I have announced it, and my servant preoccupied me. He mentioned that he said to him: Release it where you took it."
"5551","And as Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not mention his servant therein."
"5552","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: al-Qanabi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Thabit ibn al-Dahhak is a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He took the stray camel to announce it, and Umar stood upon that from him and did not denounce it to him, nor rebuke him for taking it. That indicated concerning the matter of strays of camels what we mentioned. The rulings of strays in our view are the same as the rulings of the lost item. One differing with us in that differed, claiming the lost item is other than strays, and that strays are what strays by itself, and the lost item is otherwise; so he made the rulings of the lost item what is in the hadith of Zayd ibn Khalid, and made the rulings of strays upon what is in the other hadiths, permitting taking the lost item and forbidding taking strays. So we considered what he said of that and found the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, refutes it, and it is His saying, mighty and majestic: Then it will be said to them: Where are those you used to associate, besides Allah? They will say: They have strayed from us. So He, mighty and majestic, made their losing them a straying of them by them from them. And the like of that is what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Aishah losing her necklace: Your mother has lost her necklace, so seek it. That indicated that loss of what has a soul and what has no soul may both be called stray. And in what we mentioned is what indicated that the rulings of strays and the rulings of the lost item in all what we mentioned are the same. Thus Abu Hanifah and his companions used to say in this. We mentioned their disagreement concerning the hand of the finder: some made it liable if its finder does not call witnesses that he only picked up what he picked up for announcement and preservation for its owner; and some made it a trustworthy hand with no liability with it, whether its finder called witnesses when he picked it up or did not call witnesses. Then we found a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating its ruling, that it is a trustworthy non-liable hand, and it is what."
"5553","4731 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us. 4732 - And what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, both saying: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us; Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman said in his hadith: From Rabiah ibn Abu Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Said, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, that he heard Zayd ibn Khalid; and Rawh said in his hadith: He said: Yahya ibn Said and Rabiah narrated to me, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani; then both together said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the lost item of gold and silver. He said: Know its tie and its container, then announce it a year; if no one recognizes it, enjoy it, and let it be a trust with you; if a seeker comes for it someday, pay it to him. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas and Sufyan al-Thawri, from Rabiah, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, which we mentioned in what preceded of this chapter. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that the lost item is a trust with its finder until he meets its owner, without any calling to witness he mentioned in his picking it up, like the trust; so the one in whose hand it is is trusted over it, not liable for it. In that is what indicated that its being in the hand of its finder, when he intends thereby what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him, his hand therein is a trustworthy hand over it, not a liable hand for it. And we found also in the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Amr which we mentioned in this chapter, from the hadith of Amr ibn Shuayb, the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his questioner about the stray of sheep: Detain for your brother his stray, what indicated that his taking it to detain it for his brother is a commanded taking, and with the commanded taking there is no liability upon one commanded with it. His saying, Detain for your brother his stray, may possibly not be intended for one stray to the exclusion of another, and be upon all strays. Allah knows best the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby. And Allah is the Grantor of success."
"5554","743 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the mosque founded upon piety, which of the mosques it is. 4733 - Yunus narrated to us: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Unays ibn Abu Yahya, freedman of the Aslamis, he said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Said al-Khudri that a man of Banu Khudrah and a man of Banu Amr ibn Awf disputed about the mosque founded upon piety. The Awfi said: It is our mosque at Quba. The Khudri said: It is this mosque, the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they went out and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about that. He said: It is this mosque, the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in that is much good."
"5555","4734 - And Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, Sahbal ibn Muhammad ibn Abu Yahya informed us, he said: I heard my uncle Unays ibn Abu Yahya narrating from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The mosque founded upon piety, it is this, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant his mosque."
"5556","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Sahbal narrated to us, from Unays ibn Abu Yahya, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The mosque founded upon piety is the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5557","4735 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Humayd al-Kharrat, he said: I heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abu Said al-Khudri passed by me, so I said to him: How did you hear your father mention concerning the mosque founded upon piety? He said: My father said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the house of one of his wives, and said: O Messenger of Allah, where is the mosque founded upon piety? He took a handful of pebbles and struck the ground with it, and said: It is this mosque of yours, the mosque of Madinah. He said: So I said: I witnessed your father mentioning this."
"5558","4736 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, al-Layth narrated to me; and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: It was read to Shuayb ibn al-Layth: Your father informed you; and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, from al-Layth; then all together said: From Imran ibn Abu Anas, from Said ibn Abu Said al-Khudri, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that he said: Two men disputed about the mosque founded upon piety. One man said: It is the mosque of Quba. The other said: It is the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is this mosque of mine."
"5559","4737 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Rabiah ibn Uthman, from Imran ibn Abu Anas, from Sahl ibn Sad, he said: Two men disputed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the mosque founded upon piety, one of them of the people of al-Aliyah, the other of the people of Madinah. One said: It is the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The other said: It is the mosque of Quba. That was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It is this mosque of mine. Abu Jafar said: These reports were sound in chains, acceptable in narrators, all informing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except the hadith of Ibn Marzuq in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not mentioned, that it is the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Some people claimed it is the mosque of Sad ibn Khaythamah, meaning the mosque of Quba, and they mentioned it from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr."
"5560","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, he said: The mosque of Quba is the mosque founded upon piety. They said: Among what confirms that is the building by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions of it with their hands, and they mentioned concerning that."
"5561","4738 - What Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us: My uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: The first to carry a stone for the qiblah of the mosque of Quba was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Abu Bakr carried another stone, then Umar carried another, then Uthman carried another. I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you not see these following you? He said: Indeed, they are the commanders of the caliphate after me. And they mentioned along with that, among what they adduce as proof for this saying of theirs, an interrupted hadith, and it is."
"5562","4739 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Arim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: It was mentioned that Banu Amr ibn Awf built a mosque, and sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come to them and pray in their mosque. When their brothers Banu Ghanm ibn Awf saw that, they envied them and said: We too shall build a mosque as our brothers built, and send to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); perhaps Abu Amir will pass by us and pray in it. So they built a mosque and sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come to them and pray in their mosque as he prayed in the mosque of their brothers. When the messenger came to him, he rose to go to them, or intended to go to them, then Allah the Exalted revealed: And those who took a mosque for harm and disbelief and division among the believers, up to His saying: Their building which they built will not cease to be doubt in their hearts unless their hearts are cut. And they strengthened that with a connected hadith, and it is."
"5563","4740 - What Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Sadaqah narrated to us, Utbah ibn Abu Hakim narrated to us, Talhah ibn Nafi narrated to me, Abu Ayyub al-Ansari and Jabir ibn Abd Allah and Anas ibn Malik narrated to me that when this verse was revealed: Therein are men who love to purify themselves, and Allah loves the purifiers, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Ansar, Allah has praised you well for purification; what is this purification of yours? They said: We perform ablution for prayer, wash from major impurity, and cleanse with water. He said: It is that, so adhere to it. They said: That indicated that the mosque in which this verse came down is other than the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the mosque of Quba, because in the verse is: Therein are men who love to purify themselves, and they are the Ansar to the exclusion of others. And among our proof against the sayer of that saying was: Those men were in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because his mosque was inhabited by the Muhajirun and the Ansar and others than them of his companions. So there was not in this hadith what indicates contrary to the first hadiths. And the hadith of Ibrahim from Arim was an interrupted hadith, the like of which does not resist the connected hadiths which we reported at the beginning of this chapter. Thereby it was established that the mosque founded upon piety is the mosque mentioned therein, and it is the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his city, not other mosques. And Allah we ask for success."
"5564","744 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his cursing the women visitors of graves and those who take mosques and lamps over them. 4741 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shuabah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the women visitors of graves, and those who take mosques and lamps over them."
"5565","4742 - And Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us: Abu Mamar narrated to us, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the women visitors of graves, and those who take mosques and lamps over them. So we considered this hadith and found it possible that that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before his permitting visiting graves. And we found it possible that he meant thereby all the things mentioned in this hadith, of taking mosques over graves and lamps along with that, the reaching to that being by visiting the graves over which that is taken, while visiting graves so long as that was not taken before it is lawful. So we looked into what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his permitting visiting graves after it had been forbidden."
"5566","4743 - We found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Zubayd al-Yami narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he alighted with us while we were near a thousand men. He prayed two rakahs with us, then turned to us with his face, his eyes shedding tears. Umar stood to him, ransoming him with father and mother, saying: What is with you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I sought permission from my Lord to seek forgiveness for my mother, and He did not permit me, so my eyes shed tears out of mercy for her from the Fire. I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, and let their visiting increase you in good. I had forbidden you from the meat of sacrifices after three, so eat and keep what you wish. I had forbidden you from drinks in vessels, so drink in whatever vessel you wish, but do not drink intoxicant."
"5567","4744 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban informed him, that al-Wasi ibn Habban informed him, that Abu Said al-Khudri narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbade you from visiting graves, so visit them, for in them is a lesson. I forbade you from nabidh; now make nabidh, but I do not permit intoxicant. I forbade you from the meat of sacrifices, so eat and store."
"5568","4745 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith was the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to visit graves after his prohibition of visiting them before that. So it became strong in our hearts that the curse mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Abbas fell only upon those who take mosques and lamps over them, not upon their visitors specifically among those in whose visiting there is no intent toward a mosque taken over them, nor toward a lamp lit over them. Likewise was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his cursing the Jews and Christians for their taking the like of this meaning over the graves of their prophets."
"5569","4746 - As Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me that Aishah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, said: When death came upon the Messenger of Allah, he began casting a khamisah of his over his face; when distressed, he uncovered it from his face. He said: While so, he says: The curse of Allah upon the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as mosques, warning against the like of what they did. Abu Jafar said: So by this hadith we ascertained the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in warning against taking graves as mosques. Thereby we ascertained that the curse in the hadith of Ibn Abbas was only for one whose way is this, not for others than him among visitors of graves, not for the like of that, but for other than it among what he, peace be upon him, permitted visiting them for. And we aimed at this hadith of Ibn Abbas and Aishah because therein is that that saying was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at his death, and that there is no abrogator for it. Thereby we were sufficed from mentioning what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his cursing the Jews and Christians for their taking the graves of their prophets as mosques, among what may possibly have been from him, peace be upon him, after that in this meaning, other than this speech. And Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask for success."
"5570","745 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then what his Companions and their followers and others than them among the people of language said regarding the khatans of a man, who they are, and regarding his ashhar, who they are. 4747 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Bakkar al-Harrani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Qusayt, from Muhammad ibn Usamah ibn Zayd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for you, O Ali, you are my khatan and father of my child, and you are from me and I am from you. So in this hadith was what we understood thereby that the husband of a man's daughter is a khatan to him. And there has been reported from Abd Allah ibn Masud what enters into this chapter."
"5571","What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, regarding: And He made for you from your wives sons and hafadah, al-Nahl 72, he said: The hafadah are the akhtan."
"5572","And what Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, the like of it. That was in our view, and Allah knows best: Allah the Exalted made for His servants sons, and they are the males, and daughters whom they marry to those who are of their hafadah, i.e., of their helpers, and of those who enter into their company. And there has been reported from Ibn Abbas concerning that."
"5573","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Shuabah, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas regarding His saying, sons and hafadah, he said: They are the children. Abu Jafar said: This was not in our view contrary to what we reported from Ibn Masud, because what is in this is that they are the children from whom are the daughters who are the cause of the akhtan mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Masud. And there has been reported from those after the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that."
"5574","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb narrated to us, from Shuabah, from Asim, from Zirr, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud asked me about the hafadah. I said: They are the helpers."
"5575","And what Ibrahim narrated to us: Arim narrated to us, Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, he said: al-Hasan said: The hafadah are the servants; and the people of Madinah said: The husbands of the daughters."
"5576","And what Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan narrated to us: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from al-Hasan regarding the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And He made for you from your wives sons and hafadah, he said: The sons are your sons and the sons of your son; and the hafadah are what hastens for you and works for you and helps you. Abu Jafar said: This in our view has no disagreement with what we mentioned before it of the saying of one who said they are the husbands of the daughters, because it is possible that when they become husbands of their daughters, they become for them helpers and servants."
"5577","Muhammad ibn al-Hasan had said in his book al-Ziyadat, which al-Hajjaj ibn Imran conveyed to us and informed us he took it from Safwan ibn al-Mughallis, from Abu Sulayman al-Juzjani, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, that he said: The akhtan of a man are the husbands of his daughters and his sisters and his paternal aunts and his maternal aunts and every female possessor of inviolable kinship from him; and his ashhar are every possessor of inviolable kinship from his wife. He did not report in that any disagreement between him and any of his companions. Ibn al-Sikkit mentioned in his book Islah al-Mantiq, he said: I asked al-Asmai: Who are the akhtan? He said: Everything from the side of the woman, they are the akhtan, like the mother of the woman and her sister and her paternal aunt; and the ashhar gather all this. It is said: So-and-so married among Banu so-and-so and married to them. He said: Ibn al-Arabi differed with him regarding the ashhar, saying: The sihr is the husband of a man's daughter and his brother and his father and his paternal uncle; and the akhtan are the father of the woman and her brother and her paternal uncle. Abu Jafar said: So we considered what was said regarding these two meanings and found what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said in restricting the possessors of inviolable kinship in the two meanings mentioned in this chapter, to the exclusion of others than them among those who are in kinship like them without their kinships being inviolable, we found that of his saying meaningless, since in what was reported from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the people of language and eloquence, is what refuted that, and it is what was reported from them of what they said when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Juwayriyah bint al-Harith ibn Abu Dirar."
"5578","4748 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abu Zaidah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the captives of Banu al-Mustaliq, Juwayriyah bint al-Harith fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, or of a cousin of his. She contracted for her freedom, and she was a sweet comely woman; hardly anyone saw her but she captivated him. She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to seek his help in her contract. By Allah, it was only that I saw her at the door of the chamber and disliked her, and knew he would see from her the like of what I saw. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I am Juwayriyah bint al-Harith ibn Abu Dirar, chief of his people, and there befell me of the matter what was not hidden. I fell in the share of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, or of a cousin of his, and he contracted with me, so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his help upon my contract. He said: Is there for you something better than that? She said: What is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I pay your contract and marry you. She said: Yes. He said: I have done so. The news went out to the people that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, so they said: The in-laws of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and released what was in their hands. She said: By her marrying her, one hundred households of Banu al-Mustaliq were freed. We know no woman greater in blessing upon her people than her. So in this hadith is that the people said when there reached them the marrying by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Juwayriyah, to her people, the in-laws of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so they made them thereby in-laws to him, though among them was one who is not a kinsman of hers. That indicated that the ashhar of a man are the people of his women who are his wives, among those whose kinships between them and his wives are inviolable or not inviolable. This was like what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said regarding a man's kinship and his relatives by marriage: they are upon every possessor of inviolable kinship among men and women upon the children of the father to whom they trace, on the side of men the furthest father in Islam, and on the side of women the furthest father in Islam, whether they were possessors of inviolable kinship or not, with no regard to those fathers in the pre-Islamic age. This is a saying Abu Yusuf also said, as al-Kaysani narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf in his dictation to them. Like that in our view regarding his ashhar is that this meaning be followed therein also. And there has been reported in the hadith of al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas and Rabiah ibn al-Harith their saying to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: You had attained the sihr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning his marrying his daughter, and we did not envy you for it."
"5579","4749 - As Ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, that Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Nawfal ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib narrated to him that Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabiah ibn al-Harith narrated to him, he said: Rabiah ibn al-Harith and al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib gathered, saying: If we sent these two lads, mine and al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas, over the charity, they would deliver what people deliver and attain what people attain. While they were so, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came and stood over them, and they mentioned that to him. Ali said: Do not do that, for by Allah he will not do so. Rabiah ibn al-Harith said: Nothing prevents you this but your envying us. By Allah, you attained the sihr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we did not envy you for it. Ali said: I am Abu Hasan; send them. So they went and he lay down, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. So in this hadith was the saying of Rabiah ibn al-Harith to Ali: You attained the sihr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and we did not envy you for it. Someone said: So in this is what indicated that Ali was a sihr to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by marrying his daughter. Our answer to him concerning that was: There is not in this hadith what necessitates that, because the meaning of Rabiah's saying to Ali, You attained the sihr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), i.e., you attained that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a sihr to you by marrying you his daughter, as is said to a man: You attained the favor of so-and-so, meaning you attained the favor that was from so-and-so, not that the one who attained the favor was the favor from him; rather it was from another than him to him. And the like of that also is what was reported of the saying of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding himself."
"5580","4750 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Yahya, meaning al-Awsi, narrated to us: al-Zuhri narrated to us, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us that Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar narrated to him, he said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Allah sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the truth, and I was among those who responded to Allah and His Messenger and believed in what he was sent with. Then I emigrated the two emigrations and attained the sihr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By Allah, I neither disobeyed him nor deceived him until Allah, mighty and majestic, took him. So the meaning of that is like the meaning of what we mentioned in its like regarding Ali ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. And when there was established regarding the ashhar what we mentioned, that they are the relatives by marriage of the husbands of the daughters, whether the kinships of their relatives by their wives are inviolable or not inviolable, likewise were the akhtan who are the husbands of the daughters and the husbands of the sisters and the husbands of the paternal aunts and the husbands of the maternal aunts: their relatives who are of the husbands of these women are like the relatives of the wives in what we mentioned, who thereby became ashhar to the husbands, alike therein whether one's kinship from the husbands of these women is inviolable or not inviolable. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us, from Abu Ubayd in his book al-Ansab, that he mentioned Asim ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid, a man of Banu Hilal. He said: He had governed Khurasan for Abu Jafar, and he mentioned that from the speech of Abu Ubayd to Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Abbasi, and recited to him for this Asim, he said: Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is what indicated that the relatives of the woman are ashhar to her husband, whether their kinships from her are inviolable or their kinships from her are not inviolable. And there has been reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, what enters into this meaning."
"5581","What Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ismail ibn Raja, from Umayr, the Shaykh said, and he is one of the freedmen of al-Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, he said: He said: Seven were forbidden by lineage, and seven by marriage. Then he recited: Forbidden to you are your mothers and your daughters, up to His saying: And the daughters of the sister. This is of lineage, and the rest of the verse is of marriage, and the seventh is: And do not marry those women whom your fathers married except what has passed. So in this hadith is that Allah the Exalted forbade seven by marriage, i.e., He forbade a man to marry those by whose marrying him others than him of his relatives become ashhar to him. In that is what indicated what we mentioned. And Allah we ask for success."
"5582","746 - Chapter explaining the difficulty of the hadith of Abu Musa regarding the camel claimed by two men, between whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged, and what was differed over whether that was with proof established by each of them or with other than that. 4751 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Said ibn Abu Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abu Burdah, from his father, from Abu Musa, he said: Two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over a camel, neither of them having proof, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it between them in halves."
"5583","4752 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, from Said ibn Abu Arubah, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abu Burdah, from his father, from Abu Musa: Two men disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over a thing he named, neither of them having proof, so he judged between them."
"5584","4753 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Amr ibn Ali informed us, Abd al-Ala, meaning al-Sami, narrated to us, Said, meaning Ibn Abu Arubah, narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abu Burdah, from his father, from Abu Musa: Two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over an animal, neither of them having proof, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it between them in halves. In this hadith, from the narration of Said ibn Abu Arubah, was that that judging was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between those two men, without proof established by either of them for his claim. Hammam ibn Yahya reported this hadith from Qatadah with this chain, differing therein from Said."
"5585","4754 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah informed us, from Said ibn Abu Burdah, from his father, from his grandfather, that two men disputed over a camel, and each of them brought two witnesses, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it between them."
"5586","4755 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, Qatadah narrated to us, from Said ibn Abu Burdah, from his father, from Abu Musa, that two men disputed over a camel, and each of them brought two witnesses, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it between them. So Hammam differed from Said in the text of this hadith, as we mentioned. Then we considered: Did anyone other than Said and Hammam report this hadith from Qatadah?"
"5587","4756 - We found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abu al-Mada, judge of al-Massissah, informed us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa: Two men claimed an animal they found with a man, and each of them established two witnesses that it is his animal, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it between them in halves. Thus Muhammad ibn Kathir reported this hadith from Hammad, from Qatadah, mentioning it from al-Nadr, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa. Abu Kamil Muzaffar ibn Mudrik differed from him therein, reporting it from Hammad ibn Salamah."
"5588","4757 - As Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: Abu Kamil Muzaffar ibn Mudrik narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Abu Burdah ibn Abu Musa, and he did not mention therein his reporting it from his father, that two men claimed an animal with a man, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: So in the narration of Hammad from Qatadah was agreement with Hammam from Qatadah in the text of this hadith, though in its chain he fell short of Abu Musa, stopping it at Abu Burdah. The authentication of these reports necessitates that the worthier of them in what Said and Hammam differed over is what Hammam reported, because therein is mention of judging by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that thing between its two claimants, and judging is only with proofs, not with possessions lacking proofs. What is from the judge in the like of this with possessions, not proofs, is only said to be that he left it in their hands due to equality of their standings therein; it is not said he judged except with proofs to the exclusion of other than them. When Hammam and Hammad agreed upon what they agreed upon of what we mentioned, it became strong in our hearts that what the two reported from Qatadah is worthier than what Said reported from him contradicting it, because two are worthier of preservation than one. Someone said: This hadith, its basis is corrupt."
"5589","4758 - He mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Simak ibn Harb informed us, from Tamim ibn Tarafah, that two men claimed a camel, and each of them established two witnesses, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged it between them in halves. He said: I informed Abu Burdah of that, and he wrote with it to al-Hajjaj, who wrote: Judge with it. This sayer said: So this hadith returns to Abu Burdah taking it from Tamim ibn Tarafah, not from his father Abu Musa. Our answer to him concerning that was: What he said is not necessitated by what was mentioned, because it is possible that Abu Burdah wrote to al-Hajjaj with what Tamim ibn Tarafah narrated to him so al-Hajjaj knows that Abu Burdah has in this hadith a corroborator other than him in the generality; for Simak ibn Harb, with the people of knowledge of hadith, does not equal Qatadah, nor Said ibn Abu Burdah. And when that is so, no regard is to be given to this narration of his; the hadith proceeds upon what we authenticated it upon of what Said and Hammam differed over, returning to what Hammam reported, for what he deserved thereby of what we mentioned, and for the agreement of Hammad ibn Salamah with him upon its text. This is a question of jurisprudence in which its people differed. One group among them held in that to this hadith; among this group who held to it were Abu Hanifah and his companions. Another group among them held to casting lots between the two claimants; whichever draws the lot deserves what he claimed, and it is judged for him. There has been reported in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith, though interrupted, and it is."
"5590","4759 - What al-Muradi narrated to us: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us; then both together said: From al-Layth, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: Two men disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over a matter, and each of them brought upright witnesses upon one number, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots between them and said: O Allah, You judge between them. Another group among them says: He judges it to the holder of the purer of the two proofs, the more apparent in piety and the more excellent; among those who said that among them was Malik ibn Anas along with others than him of the people of Madinah. In the analogy of their saying comes: When the two proofs are equal in that, it is judged between the two claimants in halves. Another group among them says: He judges it between the two claimants according to the number of witnesses of each of them; if they are equal in number, it is judged between them in halves. They reported that from Ali ibn Abu Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5591","As Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Hanash ibn al-Mutamir, that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was disputed to over a mule; one of the two disputants established five witnesses that it is his, born with him, and the other established two witnesses that it was born with him. He judged for the holder of the five five-sevenths of it, and for the holder of the two two-sevenths. When they differed in that, we looked into what they differed over thereof, to know the worthiest of what they said therein. We began with the saying of one who said lots are cast between them. We found casting lots had been used in the beginning of Islam and judged with."
"5592","4760 - As Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Awn al-Umari narrated to us, or Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi. Abu Jafar said: I doubt which of the two narrated it to me from him: From al-Ajlah, from al-Shabi, from Abd Allah ibn Abu al-Khalil, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was in Yemen and was brought a woman whom three had intercourse with in one purity. He asked two: Do you affirm the child to this one? They did not affirm. Then he asked two: Do you affirm the child to this one? They did not affirm. Then he asked two until he finished asking two by two, and they did not affirm. So he cast lots among them and attached the child to the one upon whom the lot came out, and imposed upon him two-thirds of the blood-money. That was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he laughed until his molars appeared."
"5593","4761 - And as Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Harb narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Sufyan informed us, from al-Ajlah, from al-Shabi, from Abd Khayr al-Hadrami, from Zayd ibn Arqam, then he mentioned the like of it. So in this hadith was the judging of Ali in the claimed lineage we mentioned by lots, until he made the child thereby for one of those who claimed him, and imposed upon him along with that of the blood-money what he imposed upon him thereof; and the standing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon that and his leaving censure of him for what was therein from him. Then we found Ali was brought the like of that after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and did not apply therein that ruling."
"5594","As Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Abu al-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qabus ibn Abu Zibyan, from Abu Zibyan, from Ali, he said: Two men came to him who had intercourse with a woman in one purity. He said: The child is between you both, and he belongs to the remaining of you. Thereby we understood that Ali did not leave what he had judged of the casting of lots we mentioned from him, upon which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and did not denounce to him, except to what is worthier than it of what abrogated it, returning the ruling in the time in which he judged with it to other than what it was upon in the first time in which he judged therein with what he judged. In that is what necessitates the negation of judging by lots in lineages and in other than them of the claimed things over which the proofs established are equal. Then secondly with what the one we mentioned from him said, that he looks to the better of the two proofs testifying to that and the more evident in righteousness and judges with it: we found that of what reason also repels, because Allah the Exalted said: And call two just ones of you to witness, and He, mighty and majestic, said: From those you approve of witnesses. So He informed us whom He made for us judging with his testimony, and he is the possessor of justice. The people of justice may differ in what they are upon thereof, some being higher in rank therein than some. Since that was so, we understood from Allah, mighty and majestic, that He made for us to judge with the testimony of one whose justice we ascertained, whether with him was of excellence what exceeds that or not. Thereby this saying was negated. Then thirdly with looking into the saying of one who said he judges therein by the number of witnesses: we found it also meaningless, because when Allah the Exalted commanded us to judge with two just witnesses, we understood from Him, mighty and majestic, that they are as more than them in number. When that was so, multitude of number and paucity were alike therein. When these three sayings were negated with what we mentioned, and there was not in this chapter of what we found the people of knowledge said therein other than the four sayings we mentioned from them therein, the three we mentioned thereof being negated, the last saying thereof was established, and leaving it to what contradicts it was not obligatory: that the claimed be judged for its two claimants whose proofs therein are equal, with equality, especially as that was reported from a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of eminent rank in knowledge, and there was not reported from any of them therein contrary to what he said therein."
"5595","As Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib and Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abu Layla, he said: Two men disputed to Abu al-Darda over a horse, and each of them established proof that it is his horse, born to him, neither selling nor gifting it. Abu al-Darda said: One of you is a liar. Then he divided it between them in halves. Then Abu al-Darda said: How we need the chain of Banu Israil. He was asked: What is it? He said: It used to descend and seize the neck of the wrongdoer."
"5596","And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu Asim narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abu Layla, he said: I was sitting with Abu al-Darda, then he mentioned this hadith."
"5597","And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, he narrated to him, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, that two men disputed to Abu al-Darda over a horse or a mule, and brought their testimonies in agreement, so he judged it between them. He said: How the people need the chain, seizing the neck of the wrongdoer. We know no one among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein disagreeing with Abu al-Darda. In this hadith, of the speech of Abu al-Darda, is what must be stood upon and by which the excellence of his knowledge and his rank in the meaning he is of its people be known: his saying to the two claimants of that horse: One of you is a liar. He aimed the verification of lying therein at one of the two claimants, not at either of the two proofs. The aspect of that in our view, and Allah knows best, is that with him was encompassing knowledge regarding the two claimants of the lying of one of them in what he claimed, because a thing cannot be owned while another than him owns it. The two proofs were not with him likewise, since it was possible that one of them saw the mare that bore that horse in the hand of one of its two claimants, so it was thereby broad for it to testify it is his; then it exited the ownership of the one in whose hand it saw it without it knowing thereof, by a sale from him or by other than it of modes of transfers; then the other saw it in the hand of the other claimant, then the birth was after that. So it was broad for each of those two proofs to testify that that birth was in the ownership of the one in whose hand it saw the mare that bore it, in the ownership of the one who claimed it, among those whose hand it knew was upon what bore for him. Allah charged no one in the matters He made His creation worship in testifying with them, in accepting it from one who testifies with it along with his apparent justice, except carrying his affair upon its apparent and leaving seeking its inward from him. When the two proofs stood therein upon what they stood upon, and the witnesses were excused thereby in their testifying with them, in that was the negation of impugnment from them therein, the obligatoriness of their justice therein, judging with what the servants were commanded to judge with of the proofs whose justice was established with them, leaving applying conjectures with them, and referring the matter to what Allah, mighty and majestic, raised from His servants its knowledge, referring them therein to what they stood upon of what He commanded them to judge with its like. And Allah we ask for success."
"5598","747 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is in the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning the administering of oaths to the emigrant women over what he used to administer oaths to them upon. 4762 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abu Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from al-Agharr, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from Abu Nasr, from Ibn Abbas regarding His saying, mighty and majestic: When the believing women come to you as emigrants, test them. He said: When a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to embrace Islam, he made her swear by Allah, mighty and majestic: She did not go out out of hatred of a husband; and by Allah, mighty and majestic: She did not go out desiring one land over another; and by Allah, mighty and majestic: She did not go out seeking world; and by Allah, mighty and majestic: She went out only out of love for Him, mighty and majestic, and for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is the administering of oaths by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those who came to him of women for emigration to him, over what was mentioned therein of his administering oaths to them upon. This enters into a chapter of jurisprudence in which its people differed: a man passes with his wealth by the tithe-collector of the Muslims, who seeks its zakat from him, and he says: I delivered it to the poor who deserve its like, or I delivered it to a tithe-collector I passed before you. Some said: He makes him swear upon that if he suspects him of what he said to him, and leaves him with his wealth; among them Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafii. Among them one who believes him upon that and does not make him swear upon it; among them Malik and al-Thawri. They held in that that this is worship whose worshippers are trusted over it, and making them swear by suspicions of them therein, other than what was obligatory upon them therein, is not obligatory; and they hold that oaths over claimed things only become obligatory for the two claimants after their knowledge that they had been of those sought thereby; and that applying suspicions with them, other than what was obligatory upon them therein, is not broad for one who suspects that of them. In that is what negates that there be upon the one claimed against therein, by suspicions not realities, an oath. This saying was what stood in our hearts and what we held in this meaning, until we stood upon what is in the hadith we reported in this chapter of the administering of oaths by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the emigrant women to him, over what he used to administer oaths to them upon of what was mentioned in the hadith we reported in that, as protection for Islam. Like that administering of oaths in what was differed over of what we mentioned is for one in charge of charities, as protection for Islam and fulfillment of the rights of its people from those upon whom they became obligatory to them. And Allah we ask for success."
"5599","748 - Chapter explaining the difficulty of the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his reciting: And man is most argumentative of things, upon the saying of Ali, peace be upon him, when he said to him and to Fatimah, peace be upon them: Will you two not pray? Our souls are but in the hand of Allah, mighty and majestic; if He wills to raise us, He raises us. 4763 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn informed me that Husayn ibn Ali narrated to him, from Ali ibn Abu Talib, peace be upon him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knocked at his and Fatimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), door at night, and said: Will you not pray? I said: O Messenger of Allah, our souls are but in the hand of Allah, mighty and majestic; if He wills to raise us, He raises us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away when I said that to him, not returning anything to me. Then I heard him, while turning away, striking his thigh and saying: And man is most argumentative of things."
"5600","4764 - And Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, both saying: Abu Salih Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5601","4765 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Said informed us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5602","4766 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Hanifah ibn Marzuq and al-Walid ibn Salih narrated to us, both saying: Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Uqayl, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say in his hadith: While striking his thigh."
"5603","4767 - And Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"5604","4768 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Numan al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me that Ali ibn Husayn informed him, from his father, from Ali ibn Abu Talib, peace be upon him, then he mentioned the like of the two hadiths of Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman which we mentioned in this chapter."
"5605","4769 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad informed us, he said: My uncle narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather Ali ibn Abu Talib, peace be upon him, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: By this hadith we ascertained that Muhammad ibn Ishaq did not narrate it to Bishr ibn al-Numan by hearing, and that he only narrated it to him by concealment. So we considered the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And man is most argumentative of things, when Ali said to him what he said of what was mentioned from him in this hadith: Was that due to dislike from him of what he said to him thereof, or for other than it? We found he, peace be upon him: Bilal said to him when they slept past Fajr until the sun rose upon them, after Bilal had said to him in the night: I shall wake you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Where is what you said, O Bilal? Bilal said to him: Taken by my soul is what took your soul. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not denounce that of his saying. We mentioned this hadith with its chain in what preceded of this book of ours. Thereby we understood that what was from him, peace be upon him, of his reciting: And man is most argumentative of things, when Ali said to him what we mentioned from him of what he said to him in this hadith, was not due to his disliking it from him. How would he denounce it from him when it is truth? Rather that was from him out of his admiration of it from Ali, because when he said to him and to his daughter, peace be upon them: Will you two not pray, intending thereby from them to take their share of prayer in the night, and not be distracted from that by sleep nor by other, Ali said to him thereupon: Our souls are but in the hand of Allah; He raises them whenever He wills, i.e., we did not leave what you called us to and urged us upon of what is better for us than what we are upon, by choice from us of what we are upon over what you called us to; rather the sleep which we have no device to repel from ourselves, because it is a thing befalling us from Allah, mighty and majestic, which we cannot repel from ourselves. So that saying from Ali, peace be upon him, was the best of answers to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he addressed him and his wife with. So from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was his reciting what he recited of what was mentioned from him in this hadith, out of his admiration of that from Ali, and because in what he recited of the Quran is what indicates that there is from man of argument what is in the best of answers to the speech spoken, and among what is praiseworthy from him. And Allah we ask for success."
"5606","749 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding dislike of mine gold and his informing that there is no good in it. 4770 - Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, both saying: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abu Amr, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that a man seized a debtor of his for ten dinars, saying: By Allah, I have nothing with which to pay you today. He said: By Allah, I shall not part from you until you give me, or bring me a guarantor who guarantees for you. He said: By Allah, I have no payment, and find no one to guarantee for me. He said: So he dragged him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, he seized me and I asked him respite one month, but he refused until I pay him or bring him a guarantor. I said: By Allah, I have no guarantor, nor find payment today. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you grant him respite but one month? He said: No. He said: Then I guarantee it for him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guaranteed it, and the man went. He brought him at what he promised him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: From where did you get this gold? He said: From a mine. He said: We have no need of it; there is no good in it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid it for him. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the gold that man brought him, when he informed him he took it from some mines, is that there is no good in it. Someone said: Is all the gold in people's hands, which they spend in their zakats and in their women's dowries and in the prices of their sales, but from the mines in which it is found? Thereby he rejected this hadith being acceptable from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: There has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what is taken from mines what contradicts what is in this hadith."
"5607","4771 - He mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an egg of gold he found in some mines, saying: Take it, O Messenger of Allah, for by Allah I own nothing else this morning. He turned away from him, then he came to him from his left and said the like of that, and he turned away from him. Then he came to him from before him and said the like of that. He said: Bring it, angry. He took it and threw it with a throw; had it hit him it would have wounded or maimed him. Then he said: One of you comes with all his wealth and gives it in charity, then sits begging people. There is no charity except after sufficiency."
"5608","4772 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Salman al-Farisi narrated to me his hadith from his mouth. He mentioned his hadith at length, saying therein: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Contract. I asked my owner that and persisted with him until he contracted with me upon reviving for him three hundred palm trees and forty ounces of silver. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Help your brother with palms. Every able man helped me with thirty and twenty and fifteen and ten. Then he said to me: O Salman, go and dig for them; when you intend to place them, do not place them until you come and inform me, so I am the one who places them with my hand. I undertook my digging, my companions helping me, until we dug their drinking channels, three hundred saplings. Every man brought what he helped me with of palms. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and began placing them with his hand, leveling their soil over them, descending until he finished from them all. By Him in whose hand is my soul, not one of them perished, and the dirhams remained. While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was one day among his companions, a man of his companions came to him with the like of an egg of gold he found from some mines, giving it in charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What did the poor Persian freedman-contractor do? Call him to me. I was called to him and came. He said: Go and pay it for what is upon you of wealth. I said: Where will this fall of what is upon me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Allah shall pay with it for you. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: The matter therein we do not know as was related, since it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said in that hadith what he said therein before mines became lawful for people; for they are in the view of some people of knowledge of the spoils, and the fifth is obligatory therein due to its obligatoriness in spoils. Among those who said that regarding mines were Abu Hanifah and his companions. Spoils had been unlawful for those before this nation among nations and for this nation for a period of Islam, until Allah, mighty and majestic, made them lawful for them as mercy from Him to them and lightening from Him upon them. Before Allah, mighty and majestic, made them lawful for them, there was no good for them in what is found therein. They are in the view of other people of knowledge of the charity wealth, and they are the people of the Hijaz. So it is possible that was before Allah, mighty and majestic, obligated zakat upon His servants in their wealth; so what was found, when they took it from mines, was not wealth for them with good therein for that. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, obligated zakat therein, so it returned to other than what it was upon before that, becoming of what has good and nearness to Allah, mighty and majestic, the obligated therein to Him being paid. What we mentioned indicated that that was only upon what was mentioned in that hadith in the state of the ruling therein regarding what is found in mines, contrary to the ruling regarding what is found therein after that. Also possible is another aspect: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had guaranteed for that man the debt upon him, that debt became upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and upon him was its payment to whom it belongs. That debt was but ten struck dinars. When that man guaranteed for came to him with what he brought him of what he found in the mine he found, and it is not struck dinars, but unstruck gold, and that with people is below struck dinars of its like, its payment being payment for what had become upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his guaranteeing it for what had been upon him; and of the law of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was that the best of people are best in payment, and he was worthiest of people thereof. Had he paid to the man who bears for him that gold, as payment for the struck dinars borne for him, he would not have paid well. He, peace be upon him, is furthest of people from that, so he disliked taking it for that, paying to the one he guaranteed for him from his wealth dinars with no deficiency upon him therein, nor dislike with him in taking them. This is a good interpretation. What we mentioned in this chapter of what we carried what we reported therein upon, and of our diverting it to what we diverted it to, negated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that there be in anything we reported from him therein contradiction or disagreement. And Allah we ask for success."
"5609","750 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the gold which Ali, peace be upon him, sent to him from Yemen, paying it to those he paid it to of those whose hearts are reconciled: Is therein what indicates the obligatory regarding what is found in mines is charity, or not? 4773 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both saying: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq, father of Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abu Num, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali, peace be upon him, to Yemen, and he sent to him a nugget of its soil. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it among four: Al-Aqra ibn Habis, Uyaynah ibn Badr, Zayd al-Khayr al-Tai, and Alqamah ibn Ulathah al-Amiri, then one of Banu Kilab. Quraysh angered, saying: He gives the chiefs of Najd and leaves us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I only give them to reconcile them."
"5610","4774 - And Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Abu Num al-Bajali, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: Ali, peace be upon him, sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a small nugget in its soil from Yemen, and he divided it among four: Al-Aqra ibn Habis al-Tamimi, Alqamah ibn Ulathah al-Amiri, then one of Banu Kilab, Uyaynah ibn Badr al-Fazari, and Zayd al-Khayl al-Tai, then one of Banu Nabhan. He said: Quraysh and the Ansar angered, saying: He gives the chiefs of Najd and leaves us. He said: I reconcile them. Someone said: In the diverting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that gold found in the mine to those whose hearts are reconciled is what indicated it is of the wealth from which those whose hearts are reconciled are given, and it is the zakat wealth. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: He has no proof thereby in what he mentioned, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to reconcile the hearts of those people from charities, as this sayer mentioned, and used to reconcile them from other than them."
"5611","4775 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave from the spoils of Hunayn one hundred camels: Uyaynah ibn Badr, and al-Aqra ibn Habis one hundred camels. Since what we mentioned was so, it was negated that there be in the first hadith proof for this sayer of what he imagined to be proof for him in what he adduced therein. And Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask for success."
"5612","751 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the rulings of the sayings and acts of the intoxicated, and the ruling by which he is intoxicated, what it is. Abu Jafar said: The first we begin with in this chapter is the saying of Allah, majestic His praise: O you who believe, do not approach prayer while intoxicated until you know what you say. So in this verse was the prohibition by Allah, mighty and majestic, to them in the state in which wine was lawful for them, from approaching prayer when they drank it, until they became not knowing what they say. We reported in what preceded of this book of ours the hadith of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when prayer was established, used to call: Let no intoxicated approach prayer. In this is what indicated that when they had been forbidden what they were forbidden thereof, there remained of their intellects a remnant by which they know what they were forbidden, and do not enter prayer upon it. In that is what indicated that the intoxicated deserving the name of intoxication is not he who does not distinguish earth from heaven nor woman from man, as Abu Hanifah used to say in that; rather he with whom is confusion due to intoxication, having become of its people, so confusion befalls him thereby in his sayings and in his acts, until he cannot restrain himself therefrom, as Abu Yusuf used to say therein. There has been reported in that what indicated this meaning. 4776 - As Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: A man of the Ansar invited Ali and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and they partook of wine, meaning before it was forbidden. They put Ali forward in Maghrib prayer, and he recited: Say, O disbelievers, and mixed it up. Then was revealed: O you who believe, do not approach prayer while intoxicated. He said: Thereby we understood that the intoxication whose holder is called intoxicated, entering the rulings of its people, is what Abu Yusuf made intoxicated by what occurs therein by intoxication, not the other intoxicated whom Abu Hanifah made intoxicated by the states he mentioned of what occurs therein. Someone said: This is an interrupted hadith, not of what should be adduced as proof in this chapter with its like. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: Though it is interrupted in the narration of al-Firyabi from Sufyan, others than him of the narrators of Sufyan raised it, among them Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi and others; they mentioned it from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali. Abu Jafar al-Razi reported it from Ata ibn al-Saib raised, as we mentioned."
"5613","4777 - Of what Ahmad ibn Shuayb conveyed to us in his book in his handwriting, ordering us to copy it to narrate it to us: In it was that Ahmad ibn Said, meaning al-Ribati, informed him, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Sad al-Dashtaki, informed us, he said: Abu Jafar, meaning al-Razi, narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, made food for us, inviting us; we ate and he gave us wine to drink, and it took hold in us. Prayer came and they put me forward, and I recited: Say, O disbelievers, I do not worship what you worship, and we worship what you worship. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Do not approach prayer while intoxicated. So this hadith became connected in chain. When we ascertained the intoxicated whose rulings shift thereby from the rulings of the sound to contrary thereof of the rulings of their opposites, we sought what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that meaning also."
"5614","4778 - We found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn al-Muhajir al-Ghanawi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to us, from his father, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man called Maiz ibn Malik came to him, saying: O Prophet of Allah, I have committed adultery, and I want you to purify me. He said to him: Return. In the morning he came to him also, confessing adultery with him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Return. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to his people, asking them about him, saying: What do you say about Maiz ibn Malik? Do you see any harm in him, or deny anything of his intellect? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we see no harm in him, nor deny anything of his intellect. Then he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the third time, confessing adultery with him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, purify me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent asking them about him, and they said as they said the first time: We see no harm in him, nor deny anything of his intellect. Then he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and acknowledged adultery with him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding him, and a pit was dug for him, and he was placed in it to his chest. Then he commanded the people to stone him. Buraydah said: We used to discuss among us, the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that had Maiz ibn Malik sat in his dwelling after his confession three times, he would not have sought him; he only stoned him at the fourth. So in this hadith, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his asking the people of Maiz about him: Do you deny anything of his intellect? Without specifying therein a cause of what his intellect is denied by, of intoxication or other than it: thereby we understood that when anything of his intellect is denied, he exits thereby from the rulings of the sound whose acknowledgments are accepted, to others than them whose acknowledgment is not accepted; and that equal therein is the ruling of the causes by which is denied of the intellects of their holders what is denied, of madness and other than it. In that is the entry of intoxication into that meaning. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also regarding Maiz what exceeds what is in this hadith."
"5615","4779 - And it is what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Juzjani informed me, he said: Yahya ibn Yala ibn al-Harith narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Ghaylan ibn Jami narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: Maiz ibn Malik came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, purify me. He said: Woe to you, return and seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him. Then he came to him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, purify me. He said: Woe to you, return and seek Allah, mighty and majestic, for forgiveness and repent to Him. He returned not far, then came, saying: O Messenger of Allah, purify me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of that, until at the fourth, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: From what shall I purify you? He said: From adultery. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked: Is there madness in him? He was informed he is not mad. He asked: Did you drink wine? A man stood and smelled his mouth, finding no smell of wine in him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you married? He said: Yes. So he commanded regarding him and he was stoned. So in this hadith was the uncovering of the states of Maiz by which his acknowledgment of adultery is repelled, and the obligatoriness of the hadd thereby upon him: intoxication is among them; and that intoxication is the intoxication we mentioned from Abu Yusuf, not the intoxication we mentioned from Abu Hanifah. In that is what indicated that the intoxicated with whom is confusion he cannot restrain from himself enters thereby the rulings of one with whom is that confusion by madness, becoming in his rulings in what was caused by intoxication like the mad in his rulings in what resembles the madness he has. Then we sought the aspect therein from the sayings of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5616","We found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abu Dhib narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: An intoxicated was brought to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and it was said: He divorced his wife. The view of Umar was to flog him and separate him from her. Aban ibn Uthman narrated to him that Uthman said: There is no divorce for the mad nor for the intoxicated. Umar said: This informs me from Uthman. So he flogged him and returned his wife. Al-Zuhri said: I mentioned it to Raja ibn Haywah, who said: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan read to us a letter from Muawiyah therein the sunan: Every divorce is permissible except the divorce of the mad. Someone said: You reported from Uthman what you reported in this chapter, and reported therein from Muawiyah what contradicts it therein. Like that was reported from Ali ibn Abu Talib, peace be upon him."
"5617","He mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuabah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Amash, he said: I heard Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabiah, that Ali, peace be upon him, said: Whoever divorces, we permit his divorce, except the divorce of the witless."
"5618","And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabiah, from Ali, he said: Every divorce is permissible except the divorce of the witless. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: What we reported from Ali and from Muawiyah is not contrary to what we reported from Uthman of what we mentioned, because witlessness may be of madness and may be of intoxication as it is of madness. So the meaning of their saying therein returned to the saying of Uthman therein. Someone said: Though the intoxicated lost his intellect by his intoxication, he brought intoxication upon himself by his act, so he was not thereby like the mad upon whom madness came without his act. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: We saw the mad, their rulings in their state of madness do not differ by difference of the causes of their madness, whether by their acts and their taking things that were causes of their intellect's departure, or by madness occurring in him with no cause from him therein, in the adherence of the rulings of the mad to them: the dropping of obligations from them, and the lifting of intent from them in their offenses in killing, so there is no retaliation upon them therein, and the blood-moneys of those they killed are upon their clans. Since that was so, and what is observed in the departure of the intellects of the sound is their intellects' departure, not the causes that were causes of their intellects' departure, likewise the intoxicated: upon him is his intellect's departure, not the cause by which his intellect departed. So by his intellect's departure he has the ruling of one with no intellect, with no regard therein to difference of the causes of his intellect's departure. The like of that also is what was agreed upon regarding the sound able to pray standing, upon whom Allah, mighty and majestic, obligated to pray it so: had he broken his leg until he became unable to stand for prayer, that he pray it so, that his obligation is to pray it sitting as he is able to pray it, and that of what his ruling therein in inability to stand, his praying so, is in the ruling of inability to stand by what befalls him returning him to that state, of the acts of Allah, majestic and mighty, with him, then of the acts of His servants like it with him; and that no making up of prayer standing is obligatory upon him though he returns to ability thereof. In what we mentioned is proof that the divorce of the intoxicated and the rest of his sayings and the rest of his acts return to the rulings of the sayings of others than him of those gone in intellect, and to the rulings of the acts of others than him of those gone in intellect. This is contrary to what Abu Hanifah and his companions and al-Shafii used to say therein, and contrary to what Malik used to say therein, of their permitting his divorce; except that Malik said: Had I known he did not understand, I would not have permitted his divorce. So he is as it were more excused than others therein, not that it had been binding upon him not to divorce by doubt until he knows with certainty the obligatoriness of divorce; for what was known with certainty is not lifted except with what removes it with certainty. Likewise the obligations of Allah, mighty and majestic, upon His servants in their prayers and in other than them of their worships. We saw no jurist attributed to reflection among the people of sects but upon what we mentioned, and it is the saying in our view which contrary is not lawful, nor can one of understanding adopt other than it. And Allah we ask for success."
"5619","752 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his joy at the saying of Mujazziz al-Mudliji regarding Zayd ibn Harithah and Usamah his son, when he saw their feet exposed and their faces covered: These are feet, some of them from others. 4780 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah: Mujazziz al-Mudliji entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw Usamah and Zayd with a velvet cloth over them, their heads covered. He said: These are feet, some of them from others. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me joyful."
"5620","4781 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, the lines of his face shining. He said: Have you not seen that Mujazziz just looked at Zayd ibn Harithah and Usamah ibn Zayd, saying: Some of these feet are from some. Someone said: Had there been in physiognomy but what is in this hadith, therein would be what indicated that with its people thereby is knowledge - these are the meanings of his words, though our words are not his words. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: We did not deny that with the people of physiognomy by physiognomy is knowledge; rather it is not of the sciences by which certainty is attained in what you hold certainty is attained thereby, of the claimed disputed lineages. Rather it is in our view like the knowledge of merchants of the goods they differ, they and others, in knowledge of their kinds and in knowledge of their lands: one says: It is of such land, another says: It is of such land, differing in their lands in which they were made; those of knowledge among them distinguish in what they say therein by attribution therein, until some say: It is of the making of so-and-so, hitting thereby the reality of the matter therein. That is not of what a ruling must be applied with; rather it is knowledge some know and others ignore. Like that is physiognomy in which people differ: some know it, some ignore it, some attribute it to a maker in particular, as the physiognomist attributes the child to a particular man. As it was not lawful to judge with the claimed goods by the testimony of one who testified it is of the work of so-and-so - one of those claiming it without his presence - for his falling upon his making it, likewise the child must not be judged with by the saying of the physiognomist that he is of the sperm of that man whom he never saw before that time, nor heard from him acknowledgment of what he attributed to him. What he says therein is knowledge by which he distinguishes from others with no knowledge with its like; and one in whose heart the like of that falls, or one who knew the reality of the matter therein before that, may rejoice with it. With nothing of that is obligatoriness of decree nor obligatoriness of ruling with it. Had it been obligatory to apply that in what we mentioned, it would have been obligatory to apply it in tracking traces which the people of knowledge thereby distinguish by what they know thereof: one who said to his slave: If you entered such place today, you are free, and the slave claims after that day passed that he had entered therein that day, his master belying him therein, and a group of the people of knowledge of tracking traces testify over a footprint seen in that place that it is the foot of that slave, that they judge with their saying and free him against his master thereby; or his master said to him: If you had entered this house before this saying of mine, you are free, and the slave claims he had entered it before that, his master denying that, and a group of the physiognomists testify therein, testifying this is his foot, that it be judged thereby and they free him against his master."
"5621","4782 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A group of a clan of Banu so-and-so came to the Messenger of Allah, embracing Islam and pledging him, while the epidemic, and it is pleurisy, had fallen. They said: O Messenger of Allah, this pain has fallen; if you permit us, we go out to the camels and be among them. He said to them: Go out and be among them. They went out, killed one of the two herdsmen, went with the camels, while the other came wounded, saying: They killed my companion and went with the camels. He said: With him were youths of the Ansar near twenty. He sent them to them, sending with them a tracker tracing their tracks, and they came to them. He cut their hands and feet and branded their eyes. He said: In their consensus that they do not apply the sayings of the physiognomists in what we mentioned is what binds them not to apply their sayings in what we mentioned before that in this chapter. If someone says: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, applied their sayings in lineages, judging with them therein. He mentioned."
"5622","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuabah narrated to us, from Tawbah al-Anbari, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Umar, that two men shared the purity of a woman; she bore, and Umar called the physiognomists. They said: He took resemblance from them both. So he made him between them. And what."
"5623","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib: Two men shared the purity of a woman, and she bore them a child. They rose to Umar ibn al-Khattab, who called for them three physiognomists. He called for dust and spread it; the two men and the lad trod therein. Then he said to one of them: Look. He looked, facing and crossing and turning back, then said: Shall I conceal or proclaim? Umar said: Rather conceal. He said: He took resemblance from them both; I know not of which of them he is. He seated him. Then he said to the other: Look. He looked, facing and crossing and turning back, then said: Shall I conceal or proclaim? He said: Rather conceal. He said: He took resemblance from them both; I know not of which of them he is. He seated him. Then he commanded the third, who looked, facing and crossing and turning back, then said: Shall I conceal or proclaim? He said: Proclaim. He said: He took resemblance from them both; I know not of which of them he is. Umar said: We track traces, thrice he says it, and Umar was a tracker. He made him for them, they inheriting him and he inheriting them. Said said to me: Do you know who is his asabah? I said: No. He said: The remaining of them. He said: So this Umar applied in lineages the sayings of the physiognomists, making the claimed child between its two claimants altogether by their saying, that from him in presence of others than him of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who did not denounce that to him nor differ with him therein. That indicated their following him upon it. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah and His help: Umar did not judge therein with the sayings of the physiognomists, because they only said in the hadith of Said ibn al-Musayyib that they know not of which of them he is, due to his taking resemblance from them; so Umar made the child of them both. That is other than what the physiognomists said. That indicated Umar did not judge with what the physiognomists were ignorant of, with the saying of the physiognomists which they were ignorant of; rather he judged therein with other than it: the claim of its two claimants thereof with their hands upon him, and the permissibility of the saying of each of them therein. As for the hadith of Tawbah, his making him between them, it is possible that was from him after what preceded it in the hadith of Said ibn al-Musayyib. After all this, the one arguing against us with these two hadiths of Umar does not make the child the son of two men. When he does not make him the son of two men, while Umar made him their son, this hadith was against him, not for him. This sayer said: It was reported from Umar in this story other than this saying in these two hadiths. And it is."
"5624","What Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abu al-Zinad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Hatib, from his father, thus Bahr narrated to us; rather it is Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father, he said: Two men came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, over a lad of pre-Islamic birth, one saying: This is my son, the other saying: This is my son. Umar called for them a tracker of Banu al-Mustaliq, asking him about the lad. The Mustaliqi looked at him, then looked, then said to Umar: By Him who honored you, I find them both shared in him altogether. Umar stood to him and struck him with the stick until he lay down, then said: By Allah, looking took you to other than a course. Then he called the mother of the lad, asking her. She said: This one, for one of the two men, had overcome me among people until I bore him children. Then he had intercourse with me as he used to do, and I conceived as I see; then a flow of blood befell me until it occurred in my soul there is nothing in my belly. Then this other had intercourse with me. By Allah, I know not of which of them he is. Umar said to the lad: Follow whichever of them you wish. So he followed one of them. Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib said: As if I see him following one of them, and he went with him. Umar said: May Allah fight the brother of Banu al-Mustaliq. Abu Jafar said: What is in the end of this hadith of the saying of its narrator, Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib said: As if I see him following one of them, indicated upon what we mentioned in its chain that it is from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father. He said: So in this hadith, Umar said to the lad: Follow whichever of them you wish. This is contrary to what is in the two hadiths of Ibn Umar and Ibn al-Musayyib you mentioned. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, majestic and mighty, and His help: What is in the two hadiths of Ibn Umar and Ibn al-Musayyib is regarding a boy not expressing himself, with the hands of its two claimants upon him; so he returned its ruling to what they say therein, making him to them both. What is in the hadith of Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman is regarding another boy expressing himself: had one of the two men claimed him, or a man in whose hand he is with no hand upon him other than his, and he repelled that, his claiming him would not be accepted for his repelling it thereof. So Umar did not judge him to them for that, returning the matter therein to what the claimed lad says therein. Thus we say regarding the lad not expressing himself: when two men claim him, their hands upon him with no hand upon him besides their hands, he is their son altogether. When expressing himself, he is not made their son altogether by their claiming him; he is made the son of the one of them whom he believes in what he claims therein. So we were those adhering to what was reported from Umar in all these reports, while he therein was otherwise. What he adduced against us for his saying in what we mentioned returned as proof for us against him in what he differed with us therein. In the hadith of Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman is what indicated Umar did not apply the saying of the physiognomist for other than the meaning we mentioned: had the saying of the physiognomist been applied therein, the claimed child, when believing one of its two claimants, would have the saying of the physiognomist as proof for the other that he is his son, being as a child claimed by two men, one of them believed and the other belied, and the other establishes proof he is his son, that he is taken for him thereby, worthier than the saying of the lad. In their leaving taking for him with the saying of the physiognomist therein is that there was no meaning to the saying of the physiognomist with him, of obligatoriness of ruling thereby in lineage nor in other. If someone says: How is what you mentioned lawful, with the saying of the physiognomist with him meaningless, while he called them and asked them about what was disputed to him therein, and that was only with need to their saying? Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: He had need to their saying whether the child may be of two men, and that is not impossible, so there is applied therein what must be applied by the saying of the two claimants of the child, not by the saying of the physiognomists; or it is impossible, so he does not occupy with that nor say anything therein. The proof that that was so:"
"5625","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Awf ibn Abu Jamilah narrated to us, from Abu al-Muhallab: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, judged regarding a man claimed by two men, each claiming he is his son, that in the pre-Islamic age. Umar called the mother of the claimed lad, saying: I remind you by Him who guided you to Islam: of which of them is he? She said: No, by Him who guided me to Islam, I know not of which of them he is. This one came to me first of the night, and this one came to me last of the night; I know not of which of them he is. Umar called four physiognomists and called for a plain, spreading it. He commanded the two claiming men, and each trod with a foot; he commanded the claimed, and he trod with a foot. Then he showed them the physiognomists, saying: Look; when you ascertain, do not speak until I ask you. The physiognomists looked, saying: We ascertained. Then he separated them, asking them one by one. They agreed, i.e., they followed, all four testifying this is of these two. Umar said: How strange what these say! I knew the bitch is impregnated by numerous dogs, and did not know women do that before this. I see what you see. Go, for they are both your fathers. So what we mentioned indicated Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, only needed the physiognomists for impossibility to be negated from the claim, not for other than that. The proof that the school of Umar was not to judge with the saying of the physiognomist in lineage nor in other:"
"5626","4783 - What al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us. And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us; al-Muzani said: From Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, and Ali said: He said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abu Yazid, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to an elder of Banu Zuhrah of our house's people. I went with the elder to Umar while he was in al-Hijr, asking him about offspring of pre-Islamic births. He said: The woman in the pre-Islamic age, when her husband divorced her or died from her, married without waiting period. The man said: As for the sperm, it is of so-and-so; as for the child, he is upon the bed of so-and-so. Umar said: He spoke truth, but the decree of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the child for the bed. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see Umar did not regard what that asked man said regarding the sperm, which is what the physiognomist is asked with upon what they say therein, returning the ruling to what contradicts it of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed. What confirms that also: The people of Islam did not differ that a man denying the child of his wife born to him upon his bed, saying: He is not of me, while his mother says: He is of him, that mutual cursing is between them, he is negated from him and returned to his mother; and that had his mother brought a group of physiognomists testifying for her with her truth, that the child is of him, that their saying therein is as no saying; and that the ruling of mutual cursing by which his lineage is negated thereby from him stands upon its state; and that the saying of the physiognomists therein is not as testimony of a just proof upon him that he is his son - judged with it, with mutual cursing not applied with it. In that is what indicated the sayings of the physiognomists in what we mentioned are no proof; rather the sayings of the physiognomists were applied in the pre-Islamic age in the like of this meaning in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negated, returning rulings to contrary thereof which the people of Islam are upon."
"5627","4784 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah informed him that marriage was in the pre-Islamic age upon four modes: A marriage among them the marriage of people today: a man proposes to a man his daughter, dowering her, then marrying her. Another marriage: a man would say to his wife when pure of her menses: Send to so-and-so and seek impregnation from him. Her husband secludes from her, never touching her until her pregnancy from that man sought impregnation from becomes clear. When her pregnancy becomes clear, her husband touches her if he wishes. He only does that desiring excellence of the child. This marriage was the marriage of seeking impregnation. Another marriage: a group below ten gather, entering upon the woman, all having intercourse with her. When she conceives and gives birth, and nights pass after she gives birth, she sends to them; no man can refuse until they gather with her. She says to them: You knew what was of her affair, and I gave birth; he is your child, O so-and-so, naming whom she loves of them by his name. Her child attaches to him, unable to refuse. A fourth marriage: many people gather, entering upon the woman, she refusing none who comes to her. They are the prostitutes, who used to erect banners over their doors; whoever wanted them entered upon them. When one of them conceives and gives birth, they are gathered for her and the physiognomists are called for them, attaching her child to whom they see, called his son, unable to refuse that. When Allah, mighty and majestic, sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the truth, He demolished all the marriage of the people of the pre-Islamic age except the marriage of the people of Islam today. He said: So in this hadith is the negation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the saying of the physiognomist in what was applied therein of what we mentioned in this hadith, and the return of the rulings of lineages to the beds, not to other than them. The people of knowledge differed in this chapter. As for Abu Hanifah and al-Thawri and the rest of the people of Kufah, they do not apply for the physiognomist a saying in anything. As for Malik, he used to apply the sayings of the physiognomists regarding slavewomen, not applying it regarding freewomen. In his negating its application regarding freewomen is what necessitates negating its application regarding slavewomen. As for al-Shafii, he used to apply it regarding freewomen and slavewomen altogether. In what preceded our mentioning in this chapter of what the matter became clear therein regarding the sayings of the physiognomists, with what we mentioned therein of what necessitates its negation in all things. And Allah we ask for success."
"5628","753 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the killer in war, who kills him of the enemy: Does he deserve thereby his spoils or not? 4785 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad, freedman of Abu Qatadah, from Abu Qatadah ibn Ribi, that he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Hunayn. When we met, the Muslims had a rout. He said: I saw a man of the polytheists overpowering a man of the Muslims. I circled for him until I came to him from behind, striking him with the sword upon his shoulder-band a blow, cutting the armor. He turned upon me, embracing me an embrace in which I found the smell of death. Then death overtook him, and he released me. I met Umar ibn al-Khattab, saying: How are the people? He said: The affair of Allah, mighty and majestic. Then the people returned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a slain with proof for him over him, his spoils are his. I stood, saying: Who testifies for me? Then I sat. Then he said that the second time, then said that the third time, and I stood. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with you, O Abu Qatadah? I related the story to him. A man of the people said: He spoke truth, O Messenger of Allah; the spoils of that slain are with me, so compensate him therefrom, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: No, by Allah then, he shall not aim at a lion of Allah's lions fighting for Allah and for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), giving you his spoils. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke truth, so give it to him. Abu Qatadah said: So he gave it to me. I sold the armor, buying with it a garden among Banu Salamah. It is the first wealth I acquired in Islam. Someone said: In this hadith is proof of the deserving by the killer of the spoils of whom he killed of the enemy, whether the Imam said before that: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his, or did not say it; for in this hadith of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever kills a slain with proof for him over him, his spoils are his. This indicates a killing preceding that saying. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: It is possible that saying was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a saying that preceded from him before that: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. He said what he said in this hadith to know who are the killers, paying to them the spoils of their slain. So we considered in that: Was therein reported anything indicating it or not? We found."
"5629","4786 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both saying: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi informed us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abu Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: On the day of Hunayn, Hawazin Bakr came upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with camels and sheep and women and children. The Muslims were defeated that day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began saying: O company of Muhajirun, I am Allah's servant and His Messenger; O company of Ansar, I am Allah's servant and His Messenger. Allah defeated the polytheists without thrusting with spear or striking with sword. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that day: Whoever kills a polytheist, his spoils are his. Abu Talhah killed twenty that day, taking their spoils. Abu Qatadah said: O Messenger of Allah, I struck a man upon the shoulder-band, dislodged from him, upon him an armor of his; see who took the armor. A man stood, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I took it, so give it to me and compensate him therefrom. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never asked a thing but he gave it or was silent. Umar stood, saying: No, by Allah, Allah, mighty and majestic, shall not bestow upon a lion of His lions, then give it to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umar spoke truth. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith was what indicated the precedence of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. For this saying was only at the dispersal of people and their scattering from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and at his need to their return to him. He said that urging them upon killing the polytheists and upon their return to him. In the hadith of Abu Qatadah: Whoever kills a slain with proof for him over him, his spoils are his, after they returned to him. That indicated his second saying in the hadith of Abu Qatadah was only for his first saying in the hadith of Anas ibn Malik. In that is what indicated one who kills a slain in war does not deserve his spoils when the Imam had not said before that: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. As Abu Hanifah and his companions, and Malik and his companions, say in that, not as one differing with them therein says. And Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask for success."
"5630","754 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the spoils of al-Madadi, companion of Awf, which Khalid ibn al-Walid paid him some of, withholding its rest, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to hand its rest to him, then commanded him not to do that. 4787 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Awf; al-Walid said: Thawr narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr, from Awf, that a Madadi accompanied them in the expedition of Mutah, and a Byzantine would charge the Muslims, cutting through them. That Madadi was subtle with him, sitting for him under a rock. When he passed by him, he hamstrung his horse; the Byzantine fell on his back, and he overcame him with the sword, killing him. He came with his horse and saddle and bridle and sword and belt, his weapon gilded with gold and jewels, to Khalid ibn al-Walid. Khalid took from him a portion, granting him its rest. I said: O Khalid, what is this? Do you not know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gives the killer all the spoils? He said: Yes, but I deemed it much. I said: By Allah, I shall surely inform it against you with the Messenger of Allah. Awf said: When we came to the Messenger of Allah, I informed him his news. He called him, commanding him to pay to the Madadi the rest of his spoils. Khalid turned to do so. I said: How do you see, O Khalid? Did I not fulfill for you what I promised you? The Messenger of Allah angered, saying: O Khalid, do not give him. Turning to me, he said: Will you leave my commanders? For you is the choice of their affair, upon them its turbidity. So in this hadith, Khalid ibn al-Walid paid to the Madadi some of the spoils of his slain, withholding from him its rest after his knowledge that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give the killer the spoils of whom he killed. So we considered that, holding in our view it is possible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not interfere with the killers regarding the spoils of their slain, not by their obligatoriness to the killers, but by his liberality with them to them, not by an obligation to them therein. The proof of that: what."
"5631","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, that al-Bara ibn Malik, brother of Anas ibn Malik, dueled Marzuban al-Zarah, stabbing him a thrust, breaking the saddle-bow, reaching him and killing him. His spoils were valued thirty thousand. When we prayed Fajr, Umar came to us in the morning, saying to Abu Talhah: We used not to take fifths of spoils, and the spoils of al-Bara reached wealth; I see but taking its fifth. We valued it thirty thousand, paying Umar six thousand. Abu Jafar said: This, with the presence of Umar and Abu Talhah and Anas ibn Malik, of what was from the Messenger of Allah on the day of Hunayn of his saying: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. In that is what negates there be therein a fifth. Umar sought taking the fifth from the spoils of al-Bara. That indicated they used to leave the fifths of spoils, not by obligatoriness upon them to leave them, but liberality from them with them to the killers of their holders. Since that was so regarding the fifths of spoils, so was it regarding their rest. Of that was what he may withhold thereof, and of it was what he may be liberal with. The carrying out by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), before the saying of Awf and after his saying, upon what he carried the matter out upon, with what he might carry it out upon. In that is what indicated the spoils of the slain are not deserved except with a preceding saying of the Imam: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. That is what may not be withheld in any state. And Allah we ask for success."
"5632","755 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was from him regarding the spoils of Abu Jahl and whom he granted it to of people, and in what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan adduced as proof of what he mentioned that what was reported in that necessitates what he said therein. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said: Had an army of Muslims entered the land of war with a commander over them, and the commander said: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his, and a man of the Muslims struck a man of the polytheists, felling him, and another cut off his head: the spoils are for the one who felled him, though he did not kill him. If he felled him striking him a blow with which he can bear up and return with speech or other, the spoils are for the one who cut off his head. He said: It reached us the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Badr: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his. So Ibn Afra struck Abu Jahl, wounding him grievously, and Ibn Masud killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his spoils for Ibn Masud. Likewise if the one who felled him struck him a blow from whose like one does not live, known its end is death, though he may live a day and two and three, and less and more, yet the other cut off his head: the spoils are for the one who cut off his head. If the first struck him scattering his belly's contents or cutting his jugulars, though in him is something of spirit, then the other cut off his head: the spoils are for the one who felled him, none for the one who cut off his head; for of this only remained the like of the movement at death. So we considered what Muhammad said in this: what he said of the chapter of jurisprudence was as he said therein. What he said regarding Abu Jahl was an error from him, for it is not known from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever kills a slain, his spoils are his, except on the day of Hunayn, not before that of the day of Badr, nor after. Matters ran in spoils upon what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. One arguing for Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in that argued. 4788 - With what Ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted him the sword of Abu Jahl on the day of Badr. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: What is in this hadith is but the granting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ibn Masud of the sword of Abu Jahl, not other than it of his spoils. In that is what indicated there had not preceded from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that day a saying necessitating the spoils of the killer. Had that been so, he would have paid the spoils of Abu Jahl in its entirety to his killer."
"5633","Of what was reported regarding Abu Jahl sounder than what we mentioned and firmer in chain. 4789 - What Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I was standing on the day of Badr between two lads young in age; I wished I were between stouter than them. One of them nudged me, saying: O uncle, do you know Abu Jahl? I said: What need have you of him, O son of my brother? He said: I was informed he reviles the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By Him in whose hand is my soul, had I seen him, my black would not part his black until the sooner of us dies. I marveled at that. The other nudged me, saying the like. I had hardly looked at Abu Jahl moving among people, saying: Do you not see this your companion you ask about? They rushed to him, striking him with their swords until they killed him. Then they came to the Messenger of Allah informing him. He said: Which of you killed him? Each said: I killed him. He said: Did you wipe your swords? They said: No. He looked at the two swords, saying: You both killed him. He decreed his spoils to Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh. The two men are Muadh ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh and Muadh ibn Afra. So in this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed the spoils to one of two men who had informed the Messenger of Allah before that that each had killed him. In that is what indicated there was no deserver in particular for his spoils; rather his spoils were referred to what the Messenger of Allah sees therein of liberality with it to one who kills him, and other than that. The proof of this also is paying some of his spoils to Ibn Masud, withholding its rest, and paying the rest of his spoils after what he granted thereof to Ibn Masud, to Muadh ibn Amr, to the exclusion of Muadh ibn Afra. In this is what indicated the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not preceded that day what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan mentioned he had preceded, of the saying that day; and that was only of what he was liberal with to whom he wished to be liberal with to him, and what he withheld from others than him of what he withheld thereof, of his killing Abu Jahl, not for what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said of what we mentioned from him. And Allah we ask for success."
"5634","756 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the inviolability of the game of Madinah and the obligatory upon its violator therein. 4790 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad; and Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, both saying: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abu Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, made Makkah unlawful the day He created the heavens and the earth and the sun and the moon, placing it between these two mountains. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for me but an hour of day. Its herbage is not cut, its trees are not lopped, none picks up its lost item but one who announces. Al-Abbas said: Except idhkhir, for the people of Makkah cannot do without it for their houses and graves. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is that the unlawfulness of Makkah was by Allah making it unlawful the day He created the heavens and the earth and the sun and the moon, placing it between the two mountains between which He placed it."
"5635","4791 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam and Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq narrated to us; al-Rabi and Bahr saying: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, and Muhammad saying: My father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us, from al-Layth, from Abu Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, made Makkah unlawful, and people did not make it unlawful. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not shed blood therein, nor lop a tree therein. If one taking license takes license, saying: It was lawful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Allah made it lawful for me and did not make it lawful for people; He only made it lawful for me an hour."
"5636","4792 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Abu Dhib, he said: Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Kabi saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5637","4793 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abu Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: When Allah, mighty and majestic, opened Makkah for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Hudhayl killed a man of Banu Layth for a slain of theirs in the pre-Islamic age. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, saying: Allah withheld killing from the people of Makkah, empowering over them His Messenger and the believers. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me. It was only made lawful for me two hours of day. After this hour of mine it is unlawful: its trees are not lopped, its thorns are not cut, its fallen is not picked up but by one who announces."
"5638","4794 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Allah, mighty and majestic, withheld the elephant from the people of Makkah."
"5639","4795 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal and Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon al-Hajun, then said: By Allah, you are the best of Allah's land and the most beloved of Allah's land to Allah, mighty and majestic. You were not lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me. You were made lawful for me but an hour of day. After this hour of yours you are unlawful till the Day of Resurrection."
"5640","4796 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in these reports is that Makkah is unlawful, and it was not lawful for anyone before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The obligatory upon one violating the inviolability of its game is the obligatory upon the killer of game in ihram, as Allah mentioned in His Book by His saying: Do not kill game while in ihram; whoever of you kills it deliberately, recompense like what he killed of cattle, the verse. And what the people of knowledge all agreed upon regarding one who does that in the inviolability of Makkah while lawful, of the obligatoriness of the like thereof upon him, except what they differed over of fasting therein, and of the saying of some: fasting does not suffice; among those who said that among them were Abu Hanifah and his companions; and of the saying of others: fasting suffices therein as it suffices in killing in ihram; among those who held that among them was Malik ibn Anas. It is the saying in our view therein, and Allah knows best. Then we looked into what Allah, mighty and majestic, informed us in His Book of what was from Ibrahim His prophet, peace be upon him, therein, of his saying: My Lord, make this city secure, and of his saying: My Lord, make this a secure city. That was not of the unlawfulness that was from Allah, mighty and majestic, in anything, as the usury the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade was not of the usury Allah, mighty and majestic, forbade in His Book in anything; for the usury Allah, mighty and majestic, forbade in His Book is in deferment, while what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade was in excess. What Ibrahim, peace be upon him, supplicated for the people of Makkah was the security by which they are distinguished from the rest of the people of lands besides Makkah. That was indicated by His saying, mighty and majestic: Did they not see We made a secure sanctuary while people are snatched around them? That was in our view, and Allah knows best, for what was of the supplication of Ibrahim, peace be upon him, in the two verses we recited. Then we looked at what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding his making Madinah unlawful, how it was."
"5641","4797 - We found Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abd Allah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim made Makkah unlawful and supplicated for them; I made Madinah unlawful and supplicated for them with the like of what Ibrahim supplicated for the people of Makkah: that He bless them in their sa and mudd. In this hadith was what indicated to us that what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding Madinah is like what was from Ibrahim, peace be upon him, regarding Makkah: the security of its people therein, and their being therein unlike those around them of people in other than it. Except that we found in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5642","4798 - What Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim, peace be upon him, made the House of Allah unlawful and secured it; I made Madinah unlawful between its two lava tracts: its thorn-trees are not cut, its game is not hunted. In this hadith was the unlawfulness of what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah: its thorn-trees not cut, its game not hunted. So it is possible that is an addition the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) added in his Madinah over what was from Ibrahim, peace be upon him, in Makkah, and his supplicating Allah, mighty and majestic, thereby, and His answering him therein. Then we considered: Is the ruling of what its inviolability is violated between the two lava tracts of Madinah, of game and thorn-trees, as is violated in the inviolability of Makkah thereof, and the obligatory thereby upon its violator?"
"5643","4799 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Jafar al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, and he is Ibn Sad ibn Abu Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, from Amir ibn Sad, that Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, rode to his palace at al-Aqiq, finding a lad cutting trees or beating them, and took his spoils. When he returned, the lad's people came to him, speaking to him to return what he took from their lad. He said: Allah forbid I return anything the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted me. He refused to return to them. Abu Jafar said: Thus Ibrahim narrated to us with these words."
"5644","4800 - And Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both saying: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Yala ibn Hakim narrating from Sulayman ibn Abu Abd Allah, he said: I witnessed Sad ibn Abu Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, when people came to him regarding a slave of theirs whose spoils Sad took, seeing him hunting in the sanctuary of Madinah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made unlawful. They spoke to him to return their spoils to them, but he refused, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when delimiting the sanctuary of Madinah, said: Whom you find hunting in any of these bounds, whoever finds him, his spoils are his. I shall not return to you a morsel Allah's Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed me. But if you wish I compensate you its place, I shall do. The wording is Yazid's."
"5645","4801 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Yala ibn Hakim, from Sulayman ibn Abu Abd Allah, that Sad ibn Abu Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, seized a slave hunting in the sanctuary of Madinah which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made unlawful, stripping his clothes. His masters came to Sad, speaking to him. Sad said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made this sanctuary unlawful, saying: Whoever seizes one hunting therein anything, to whoever seizes him are his spoils. I shall not return to you a morsel the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed me. But if you wish, I give you its price. So in this was what indicated to us the obligatory in violating game and thorn-trees between the two lava tracts of Madinah is other than the obligatory in violating them in the inviolability of Makkah; for the obligatory in violating them in the inviolability of Makkah is what we mentioned in this chapter in that, while the obligatory in violating its inviolability of Madinah is what we mentioned in these two hadiths. Then we found the jurists of the cities upon whom fatwa turns, from whom knowledge is taken in the two sanctuaries and in the rest of lands besides them, unanimous that taking the spoils of one violating the inviolability of game and thorn-trees at Madinah is not applied. Thereby we understood their consensus upon leaving what is in these two hadiths was for their standing upon its abrogation; for they are trusted over what they reported and over what they said. One who leaves what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said or judged with is outside this rank, his saying unacceptable, his narration inapplicable. Allah forbid, mighty and majestic, they be so. Rather their leaving that was in our view, and Allah knows best, upon the like of their leaving other than it of what was reported regarding violating sanctuaries, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Like that is what was reported from him regarding the withholder of zakat: We take it and half his wealth, a resolve of our Lord's resolves, mighty and majestic. What was reported from him, peace be upon him, regarding the mountain stray: therein is double indemnity and exemplary lashes. What was reported from him regarding one who falls upon his wife's slavegirl coercing her: she is freed upon him and upon him is her like; if that from him to her while she obeys him, she was his, and upon her was her like to his wife. So he imposed a corrupt slavegirl, placing in her place an uncorrupt slavegirl, freeing upon him when he fell upon her coercing her. Like that, and Allah knows best, is what was reported of spoils in what we mentioned: it is possible it was while rulings therein were so, then abrogated by the abrogation of its forms we mentioned in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"5646","757 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what the people of knowledge differed over the manner of testimonies in rights with judges, with what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Arim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid testified a testimony with Yazid ibn Abu Muslim. He said: Do you testify with the testimony of Allah, mighty and majestic? He said: I testify my own testimony. He repeated to him twice, each time saying: I testify my own testimony. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz related to us, from Abu Ubayd, he said: Hajjaj, meaning Ibn Muhammad, narrated to us, from Shuabah, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, that Yazid ibn Abu Muslim said to him: Do you testify with the testimony of Allah? He said: No, but I testify my testimony. Hajjaj said: Shuabah said: I testified with Sawwar, who said to me: Do you testify with the testimony of Allah, mighty and majestic? I said: No. I narrated to him this hadith, and he said: By your life, with your testimony. Abu Jafar said: So in this account, from Sawwar, his seeking testimony from Shuabah over what he attended to testify with it with him, that his testimony over that with him be with the testimony of Allah, mighty and majestic, over what he testifies with it. This saying, we found the jurists of the cities all upon contrary thereof: the testimony sought therein is the testimony of witnesses over their own testimonies, not over the testimony of Allah thereby over whom they testify against. Like that was reported from Shurayh."
"5647","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub and Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin: A man testified with Shurayh a testimony, saying: I testify with the testimony of Allah. Shurayh said: Do not say the testimony of Allah, for Allah testifies but upon truth; rather testify with your testimony. Then we considered: Was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that anything indicating what is applied therein or not?"
"5648","4802 - We found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abu Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Umarah ibn Khuzaymah al-Ansari narrated to me that his uncle narrated to him, being of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a horse from a bedouin, following him to pay him the price of his horse. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened walking, the bedouin lagging. Men began confronting the bedouin, bargaining him for the horse, unaware the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought it, until some of them raised the bedouin in bidding over the price of the horse the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought it with. The bedouin called the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: If you are buying this horse, buy it; otherwise I sell it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood when he heard the bedouin's call, saying: Did I not buy it from you? The bedouin said: No, by Allah, I did not sell you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, I bought it from you. People began resorting to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the bedouin while they disputed. The bedouin kept saying: Bring a witness testifying I sold you. A man of the Muslims passed, and the bedouin said: Woe to you, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would only say truth, until Khuzaymah came, listening to the dispute of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the dispute of the bedouin while he says: Bring a witness testifying to you I sold you. Khuzaymah said: I testify you bought him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Khuzaymah, saying: With what do you testify? He said: By believing you, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the testimony of Khuzaymah the testimony of two men. He said: So in this hadith, of the testifying of Khuzaymah against the bedouin for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), at his need to testimony for him over what the bedouin denied him thereof, by testifying for him upon his selling him, not upon testifying for him with the testimony of Allah, mighty and majestic, upon his selling him: thereby he deserved the honor and rank Allah singled him with. That indicated all testimonies with judges over rights are likewise, not upon what Sawwar held therein. Then we found what is higher than this: what Allah mentioned in His Book in the verse of mutual cursing, of His saying, mighty and majestic: The testimony of one of them is four testimonies by Allah. He did not say the testimony of one of them is with the testimony of Allah, mighty and majestic, with what he testifies with in mutual cursing. In that is proof of the manner of testimonies over rights that they are as we mentioned, not upon what Sawwar charged Shuabah in his testifying with him. In that is a meaning that must be stood upon: Allah, mighty and majestic, knows the realities of things His creation does not know. It is possible a man testifies for a man over an obligatory right for him upon him, then he is absolved to him thereof, Allah knowing that thereof, hidden to creatures. It is broad for one who knew the obligatoriness of the right at first to testify with its obligatoriness to its claimant against the claimed, while Allah testifies therein with contrary thereof of what He hid from His creation. In what we mentioned is what indicated what we described of what we clarified in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"5649","758 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding women's wearing gold, of permitting and forbidding. 4803 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi and Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, both saying: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from his father, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw upon her two bracelets of gold. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you of better than this? Were you to remove these and make two bracelets of silver, then yellow them with saffron, they would be good. Abu Jafar said: One impugning impugned the chain of this hadith, saying: Its basis is from Ibn Shihab, with neither Urwah nor Aishah therein. He mentioned in that."
"5650","4804 - What Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the like of it, mentioning therein neither Urwah nor Aishah. Abu Jafar said: That did not in our view necessitate what he said, for Ibn Wahb is not above Bakr ibn Mudar to be judged for him over him therein; rather Bakr preserved therein what Ibn Wahb did not preserve. Both, praise be to Allah, are proof, though with the precedence he has in age and narration. We found this hadith from the narration of other than Amr, from Ibn Shihab, agreeing with Bakr upon what he reported upon it."
"5651","4805 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: My mother adorned me with a necklace and nails and a silver bracelet. One day I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Had I two bracelets of gold. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were you to smear upon your bracelets saffron, it would resemble gold. This impugner said: Abu Hariz is not of those by whose narration judgment is passed in the like of what you mentioned upon disagreement therein. Our answer to him concerning that, by the accord of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help: Though as he mentioned, Mamar ibn Rashid, whose narration is not below the narration of Amr, agreed with Bakr and his father upon that."
"5652","4806 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, or from Amrah, from Aishah, thus he said, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw in Aishah's hands two twisted bracelets of gold. He said: Cast them from you and make two bracelets of silver, yellowing them with saffron. Thereby judgment for Bakr over Ibn Wahb became obligatory in what we mentioned their disagreement therein of the chain of the hadith in whose chain they differed."
"5653","4807 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from a sister of Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Woe to you, O company of women, have you not in silver what to adorn with, that you adorn with gold? No woman of you adorns with gold but she is punished with it on the Day of Resurrection."
"5654","4808 - al-Husayn ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from a sister of Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then she mentioned the like of it. So we considered the hadith of Aishah with which we began mentioning in this chapter: Was reported what indicated its abrogation or not?"
"5655","4809 - We found Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ata, from Aishah, that she used to adorn her sister's daughters with gold, while Umm Salamah disliked that and denounced it. So in Aishah's permitting the adorning of her sister's daughters with gold, after her hearing from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned from her in this chapter, was that that was not from her but after her standing upon its lawfulness for them and their likes, after its unlawfulness upon them and their likes. Thereby was established the abrogation of what she had known of the preventing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he prevented thereof. Then we considered the hadith of Mansur which Sufyan and Sharik reported from him."
"5656","4810 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur. And we found Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from his wife, from the sister of Hudhayfa, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Sufyan and Sharik, from Mansur, both of which we mentioned from him in this chapter."
"5657","4811 - And we found Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la informed us, he said: al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur narrating, from Rib'i, from his wife, from the sister of Hudhayfa, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned the like of it. He said: So this hadith is defective due to the defectiveness of its isnad, because its isnad goes back to the wife of Rib'i, who is unknown, and one like her cannot be adduced as proof in this chapter."
"5658","4812 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Sallam, from Abu Asma' al-Rahabi, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The daughter of Hubayra came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a gold ring (fatakh) in her hand, or large heavy rings, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began striking her hand. Then she went to Fatima, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and complained to her about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done to her. Thawban said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Fatima while I was with him, and she had taken a gold chain from her neck, and she said: This was gifted to me by Abu Hasan (Ali). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while the chain was in her hand, and he said: O Fatima, would it please you that people say: Fatima, daughter of Muhammad, with a chain of fire in her hand. Then he left without sitting. So Fatima took the chain and bought a slave boy with it and freed him. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Praise be to Allah who saved Fatima from the Fire. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith was among the best of what was narrated in this chapter on the prohibition of women wearing gold, except that it may have been abrogated by what we mentioned of that which abrogated the hadith of A'isha which we narrated in this chapter."
"5659","4813 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu al-Jahm, from Abu Zayd, from Abu Hurayra, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a woman came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, a neck collar of gold? He said: A collar of fire. She said: O Messenger of Allah, a bracelet of gold? He said: A bracelet of fire. She said: Two earrings of gold? He said: Two earrings of fire. She was wearing two gold bracelets, so she threw them away, and said: O Messenger of Allah, when a woman does not adorn herself for her husband, she becomes unattractive in his eyes. He said: What prevents one of you from making two earrings of silver and then coloring them yellow with saffron. We examined this hadith and found in its isnad an unknown man, not known who he is, namely Abu Zayd mentioned in it, so one like him cannot be adduced as proof in this chapter."
"5660","4814 - Malik ibn Yahya Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, that is Ibn Abi Kathir, from Mahmud ibn Amr, that Asma' bint Yazid narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any woman who adorns herself with a necklace of gold, the like of it of fire will be placed on her neck on the Day of Resurrection, and any woman who puts a gold earring in her ear, the like of it will be placed in her ear on the Day of Resurrection. This hadith also has in its isnad Mahmud ibn Amr, who is unknown. Then an objector, among those who argue for repelling what is in this chapter on the prohibition of gold for women to wear, said: It has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he permitted that for them."
"5661","4815 - He mentioned what al-Rabi' al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Sa'ba, from a man of Hamdan called Aflah, from Ibn Zurayr, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took silk in his right hand and took gold in his left hand, then said: These two are forbidden for the males of my umma."
"5662","4816 - And what Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi al-Sa'ba al-Qurashi, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, from Abd Allah ibn Zurayr, he said: I heard Ali, peace be upon him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with gold in one hand and silk in the other, and said: These two are forbidden for the males of my umma, and lawful for its females. Our answer to him regarding that, by Allah's guidance and help, was: this hadith has a defective isnad, because Ibn Lahi'a said in it: from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, while al-Layth opposed him, and he is more accurate in narration than him, saying in it: from a man of Hamdan called Aflah; and this Aflah is unknown, and he is not Abu Ali al-Hamdani, for Abu Ali is Thumama ibn Shufi. And Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated this hadith from Yazid ibn Abi Habib and agreed with al-Layth in it, opposing Ibn Lahi'a."
"5663","4817 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi al-Sa'ba, from Abu Aflah, from Abd Allah ibn Zurayr al-Ghafiqi, from Ali, peace be upon him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Layth, and he did not mention in it: and lawful for its females, just as al-Layth did not mention it. Then we examined whether there is in this chapter anything that corroborates what is in this hadith of Ibn Lahi'a from another route."
"5664","4818 - We found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us. 4819 - And we found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ibrahim ibn Munqidh had narrated to us, they said: al-Muqri' narrated to us, then they all agreed saying: from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi', from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Silk and gold are lawful for the females of my umma, forbidden for its males. Then we examined whether we find on this meaning anything stronger than this hadith."
"5665","4820 - We found Ibn Abi Imran, Muhammad ibn Khuzayma, Ibn Abi Dawud, Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman, and Abu Zur'a al-Dimashqi had narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn Zayd ibn Arqam narrated to me, he said: My paternal aunt Unaysa bint Zayd ibn Arqam narrated to me, from her father Zayd ibn Arqam, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. And Ali added in his hadith, she said: A man said to him: You say this while this Commander of the Believers forbids it. She said: I had in my hand two gold bracelets (qalban), so he said: Put them down, and he mounted his donkey and left, then returned and said: Put them back on, for I asked him, and he said: There is no harm in it. Then we examined whether we find in this chapter anything stronger than this."
"5666","4821 - We found Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Thawban and Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayya, he said: I heard Maslama ibn Mukhallad saying to Uqba ibn Amir: Stand and narrate to the people what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Uqba stood and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies about me, let him take his seat in Hell. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Silk and gold are forbidden for the males of my umma, lawful for their females. This hadith was among the best in this chapter."
"5667","Except that we found Ibn Wahb had contradicted Yahya in this hadith from Amr, as Yunus and Bahr narrated to us; Yunus said: Ibn Wahb informed us, and Bahr said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, in both of their hadiths: that Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayya al-Lakhmi narrated to him, he said: I heard Maslama ibn Mukhallad delivering a sermon saying: O people, do you not have in coarse cotton (asb) and linen what suffices you instead of wearing silk; and among you is a man who reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Stand, O Uqba. So Uqba ibn Amir stood and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever wears silk in this world will be deprived of wearing it in the Hereafter. So Ibn Wahb and Yahya differed over Amr in this hadith: Ibn Wahb narrated it in a way entailing prohibition of wearing silk, while Yahya narrated it in a way permitting wearing it for females; Allah knows best the truth of it. As for the hadith of al-Hasan ibn Thawban, there was no disagreement in it that it entails permitting women to wear silk. Then we examined whether anything else besides these reports was narrated in this chapter."
"5668","4823 - We found Muhammad ibn Khuzayma had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Silk and gold are lawful for the females of my umma, forbidden for its males."
"5669","4824 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Dirhami narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Aruba, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. This was among the best of what was narrated in this chapter, except that some people mentioned that Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Umari narrated this hadith from Nafi', saying in it: from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, from a man, from Abu Musa; and that Yunus ibn Yazid, in the hadith of al-Qasim ibn Mabrur, narrated it from Nafi' likewise. Yet Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Umari is not one with whom one like those we mentioned can be countered, for Yunus, though above Abd Allah ibn Umar, is not one with whom Ubayd Allah and Ayyub can be countered. Then we examined whether anything was narrated permitting women to wear silk besides what we mentioned."
"5670","4825 - We found Bakkar and Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu Awn al-Thaqafi, he said: I heard Abu Salih al-Hanafi saying: I heard Ali, peace be upon him, saying: A striped silk robe (hulla siyara') of silk was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent it to me; I wore it and saw dislike on his face, so I tore it into headscarves (khumur) among my women."
"5671","4826 - We found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Hunayn narrated to him, that his father narrated to him, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed me with a striped silk robe (hulla siyara'), so I went out in it. He said to me: O Ali, I did not clothe you with it for you to wear it. So I returned to Fatima and gave her its edge, as if she was folding it with me, then I tore it. She said: May your hands be dusty, O son of Abu Talib, what have you brought? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to wear it, so wear it yourself and clothe your kinswomen with it."
"5672","4827 - We found Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, he said: I heard Mujahid narrating, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: I heard Ali saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a robe of silk, so he sent it to me and I wore it, and I saw dislike on his face, so he commanded me and I tore it into headscarves among the women."
"5673","4828 - We found Yazid and Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, they said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi' narrating, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw Utarid al-Tamimi offering for sale in the market a striped silk robe (hulla siyara'), so Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you bought it for the Arab delegations when they come to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share. Then afterwards the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought striped silk robes, so he sent a robe to Umar and a robe to Usama, and gave Ali a robe, commanding him to tear it into headscarves among his women. He said: Usama went out in his robe, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him with a look by which he knew he disliked what he had done, and said: I did not send it to you to wear it; I sent it to you only to tear it into headscarves among your women."
"5674","4829 - We found Rawh had narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a striped silk robe on Utarid and disliked it for him and forbade him from it. Then he clothed Umar with the like of it, so he said: O Messenger of Allah, you said about the robe of Utarid what you said, yet you clothe me with this? He said: I did not clothe you with it for you to wear it; I gave it to you only to clothe women with it. So what we narrated was the succession of reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting women to wear silk; thus what was narrated from him on that must not be set against what was narrated from him contradicting it which did not come as it came, nor did reports successively establish it as reports successively established its opposite. We shall bring the examination of that after this chapter of this book of ours, if Allah wills; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5675","759 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on women wearing silk, both prohibition and permission. 4830 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, I passed by Utarid, or by Labid, while he was displaying a silk robe; if only you bought it for Friday and for delegations. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share in the Hereafter."
"5676","4831 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it, except that he did not mention Utarid nor Labid."
"5677","4832 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Amr informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it, and he mentioned that the man was Utarid or Labid."
"5678","4833 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni Abu Qurra narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamza narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Humran narrated to me, he said: Mu'awiya performed Hajj and invited a group of the Ansar in the Ka'ba, and said: I adjure you by Allah, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid silk garments? They said: O Allah, yes. He said: And I bear witness."
"5679","4834 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Shu'ayb, that is Ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi, informed me, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb, that is Ibn Ishaq, narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Shaykh narrated to me, he said: Himman narrated to me, thus he said, he said: Mu'awiya performed Hajj, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5680","4835 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Nusayr ibn al-Faraj informed me, he said: Umara ibn Bishr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to me, he said: Himman narrated to me, thus he said, he said: Mu'awiya performed Hajj, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5681","4836 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyad informed us, from Uqba, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to me, he said: Ibn Himman narrated to me, thus he said, he said: Mu'awiya performed Hajj, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: These reports contain the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition of wearing silk absolutely; it is possible that women were included in it along with men, since this doctrine was narrated from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, which we shall mention hereafter in this book of ours, if Allah wills. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also indicates this meaning."
"5682","4837 - As Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu Ushshana al-Ma'afiri, that he narrated to him that he heard Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani reporting: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid his family gold ornaments and silk, saying: If you love the ornaments and silk of Paradise, do not wear them in this world. This hadith supported what Ibn al-Zubayr held, and how he construed the first reports as including women with men in the prohibition mentioned in them. This view was, in our opinion, correct by analogy, for we saw that using gold and silver vessels was forbidden because they are the vessels of the people of Paradise, so men and women were equal in that; and silk is the clothing of the people of Paradise, as Allah informed us in His Book saying: And their clothing therein will be silk. Men were forbidden it, so by analogy women should be forbidden it and be like men in it, just as they are regarding gold and silver vessels. That is the analogy in this chapter, but most reports contradict that, and we have mentioned of them what suffices in the chapter before this one. And it has been narrated about Umm Kulthum, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), regarding her wearing thereof."
"5683","4838 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamza informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that he saw on Umm Kulthum, the daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a striped silk cloak (burd harir siyara')."
"5684","4839 - And what Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamza informed us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it."
"5685","4840 - And what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Awza'i and Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it."
"5686","4841 - And what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it."
"5687","4842 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman and Haywa ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami narrated to us, they said: Baqiyya narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: This indicates that among the household of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were those who wore silk; if that was in his lifetime, it contradicts the hadith of Uqba which we mentioned in this chapter, and if it was after him, it proves its abrogation; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5688","760 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which Ibn al-Zubayr construed from his forbidding wearing silk in this world, that whoever wears it therein will not enter Paradise: is it as he construed it or not? 4843 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dhubyana narrated to me, he said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr delivering a sermon saying: O people, do not clothe your women in silk, for I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter. Ibn al-Zubayr said: And I say, whoever does not wear it in the Hereafter will not enter Paradise, for Allah, blessed and exalted, said: And their clothing therein will be silk."
"5689","4844 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Abu Dhubyana Khalifa ibn Ka'b narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it. So Ibn al-Zubayr's interpretation implies that whoever wears it in this world will not enter Paradise. We examined whether what he construed from it is as he construed it or not."
"5690","4845 - We found Bakkar ibn Qutayba had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Qatada, from Dawud al-Sarraj, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, even if he enters Paradise; the people of Paradise will wear it, but he will not wear it."
"5691","4846 - We found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Uthman, that is Ibn Abi Safwan, informed us, he said: Yahya, that is Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Dawud - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb said: Thus said Muhammad ibn Uthman, and the correct is Dawud - from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith Shu'ba agrees with Hisham on the text of this hadith."
"5692","4847 - We found Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Dawud al-Sarraj, from Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter. He did not mention anything else of what is in the preceding hadith."
"5693","4848 - We found Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Faraj informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Dawud al-Sarraj, from Abu Sa'id, the like of it, from his saying. Shu'ba said: And Hisham informed me, and he was more attached to him than I, that he used to raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5694","4849 - We found Ahmad had narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'id, that is Abu Qudama, informed us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham informed us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatada, from Dawud al-Sarraj, from Abu Sa'id, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter; even if he enters Paradise, the people of Paradise will wear it but he will not wear it. Abu Ja'far said: By that we established that Ibn al-Zubayr's interpretation which he gave to what we mentioned was not in reality as he interpreted it, due to what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contradicting it. We examined Dawud al-Sarraj, who he is and his condition in narration, and found al-Bukhari mentioned him, noting he is from Thaqif, without mentioning anything by which he criticizes others in their narration; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5695","761 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his prayer over al-Najashi in Medina: was that while al-Najashi was then in Abyssinia or in Medina? 4850 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, from Yahya, that is Ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother al-Najashi has died, so pray over him. He said: We thought the bier had come; he said: So we lined up and prayed over him, though he had died in Abyssinia, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him when he entered Medina. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held regarding al-Najashi: that he was carried to Medina by Allah's subtle power on the day he died, so that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him as he prays over one who died with him in Medina. That repels using this hadith as proof by one who permits prayer over the absent dead outright. What Allah did in that by His subtle power is like what He did for His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Quraysh belied him after he told them he was taken by night to Bayt al-Maqdis, then returned to his house that same night."
"5696","4851 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn Ashras narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz, that is al-Majishun, narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, that is al-Hashimi, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw myself in al-Hijr while Quraysh were questioning me about my night journey; they asked me about things of Bayt al-Maqdis which I had not retained, and I was distressed as never before; then Allah raised it before me so I looked at it, and whatever they asked me about, I informed them."
"5697","4852 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said, Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When Quraysh belied me, I stood in al-Hijr, and Allah displayed Bayt al-Maqdis before me, and I began informing them of its furnishings while looking at it."
"5698","4853 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutayba ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Then an objector said: The interpretation of this hadith which you narrated from Imran is impossible, for in it the bier came, as they thought, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his prayer over him was when he entered Medina; yet a bier has no coming of its own, and al-Najashi had no entering, for entering belongs to the living, not the dead. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, was: this and the like is said of the dead as it is said of the living, for they say: the bier has arrived, meaning: the bier has been brought; and much like this is in their speech, even said in Allah's Book: Did the people of the towns feel secure from Our punishment coming to them at night while they slept; or did the people of the towns feel secure from Our punishment coming to them in the forenoon while they played. So He attributed coming to the punishment, though punishment does not come by itself but is brought. Likewise His saying: And Allah presents the example of a town that was safe and secure, its provision coming to it abundantly from every place. The coming of provision to it is by the coming of those who bring it. Likewise is the coming of the bier to what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held of its coming to him, and al-Najashi's entering Medina at the time he entered it is by the doing of other than the bier and other than al-Najashi. By Allah's praise, it is removed that this hadith contains impossibility as this claimant alleged; and in this hadith is what repels any proof for one who holds prayer over the absent dead. Among those who did not hold prayer over the absent dead were Abu Hanifa and Malik and their companions; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5699","762 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on affirming interdiction (hajr) over the spendthrift regarding his wealth, and negating interdiction from him. 4854 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar. And al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that a man mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he is deceived in sales, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: When you trade, say: No cheating (la khilaba). So whenever the man sold, he would say: No cheating."
"5700","4855 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, that he heard Ibn Umar saying, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5701","4856 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to me, from Haywa, from Ibn Ajlan, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that a man was heavy-tongued; when he traded with people they cheated him in sale, so he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: When you trade with anyone, say: Take, and no cheating. Abu Ja'far said: In this was that man's informing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or another's informing, that he is deceived in sales, yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not interdict him nor withhold his hand from his wealth on that account. Then an objector said: In that is proof negating interdiction over sane adults, and among those who held that was Abu Hanifa, preceded in it by Muhammad ibn Sirin."
"5702","As Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, that he did not consider interdiction anything."
"5703","And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, that he did not recognize interdiction nor regard it as anything. So among the proof against one who held this view, adducing for it what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned his adducing it in this chapter, is: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give that man free sale except with his stipulating in it that there be no cheating; and in that is proof that the sale he permitted him is not like the sale of others who are not deceived in sale. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a townsman selling for a Bedouin, saying: Leave people, Allah provides for some of them through others."
"5704","4857 - Yunus narrated it to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this which was narrated of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting that man to sell while stipulating no cheating, proves his sale is a sale referred to the consideration of whoever is in charge over him: if there is cheating in it, he annuls it, and if there is no cheating, he upholds it; and in that is proof of the hand being placed over him, not lifted from him."
"5705","4858 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Habban ibn Munqidh had a head wound reaching the brain membrane (ma'muma), so his tongue became heavy, and he used to be deceived in sale. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted him, in whatever he buys, the option for three days, and said to him: Say: No cheating. Ibn Umar said: I heard him saying: No khidhaba, no khidhaba (mispronouncing). So in this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted Habban, who is this man mentioned in these reports, the option of three days in what he buys, for his sale to be considered and either upheld or rejected, as we narrated before in his story in this chapter; and that is interdiction over him in his wealth, not free disposal therein."
"5706","4859 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Hammad al-Ma'ni informed us, he said: Abd al-A'la, that is Ibn Abd al-A'la, narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatada, from Anas, that a man was weak in mind and used to trade, and his family came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, interdict him. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and forbade him, but he said: O Prophet of Allah, I cannot refrain from trading. He said: When you trade, say: No cheating. He said: So in this hadith, Habban's family asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to interdict him, and he did not rebuke them for saying that, and he commanded him with the like of what is in the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Umar in his story; and in that is proof of interdiction over one like him in his wealth, and that his hand is not free therein except in what whoever is in charge over him permits. Then we found the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his Rightly-Guided Caliphs and others among them, affirming interdiction over whoever deserves it; among that."
"5707","What Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sama'a informed us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf saying. And what Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi informed us, he said: Abu Yusuf informed us, then both agreed saying, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, that Abd Allah ibn Ja'far came to al-Zubayr and said: I made a purchase, and Ali, peace be upon him, wants to interdict me. Al-Zubayr said: I am your partner in the purchase. Then Ali came to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him to interdict Abd Allah ibn Ja'far. Al-Zubayr said: I am his partner in this purchase. Uthman said: How can I interdict a man whose partner is al-Zubayr? So in this hadith, Ali, peace be upon him, attempted to interdict Abd Allah ibn Ja'far, and when al-Zubayr learned of that, he asked Abd Allah ibn Ja'far to make him partner in that purchase for which Ali attempted interdiction, to lift from himself what he feared from Uthman therein. Uthman's learning of that and his arguing against Ali with al-Zubayr's partnership with Abd Allah ibn Ja'far therein proves that had it not been for al-Zubayr's partnership with him therein, he would have interdicted him. Abd Allah ibn Ja'far feared that for himself from Uthman interdicting him on its account; and all that was in the presence of those of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides them who attended them, and they did not rebuke them nor oppose them therein, proving their concurrence with them thereon."
"5708","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, that Najda wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him: When does an orphan's orphanhood end? Ibn Abbas wrote to him: You wrote asking me when an orphan's orphanhood ends? By my life, a man may grow a beard while still weak in taking for himself and weak in giving from it; when he takes for himself soundly as people take, his orphanhood has ended. So this Ibn Abbas too did what concurred with those we mentioned before him among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in affirming interdiction."
"5709","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, that A'isha learned that Ibn al-Zubayr had learned she was selling some of her real estate, and said: She must stop, or I shall interdict her. She said: Did he say it?! By Allah, I shall never speak to him again."
"5710","Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shabur narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, that he narrated to him, he said: al-Tufayl ibn al-Harith narrated to me, and he was A'isha's brother through her mother, a man of Azd Shanu'a, that it reached Ibn al-Zubayr that A'isha was selling some of her houses, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5711","al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Awf ibn al-Harith ibn al-Tufayl, he being the son of A'isha's maternal brother, that A'isha narrated to him that Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5712","Fahd and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith is what is in it from Ibn al-Zubayr and what is in it from A'isha, with no denial therein from her of interdiction, nor her saying: can anyone be interdicted for his disposal of his wealth like what reached Ibn al-Zubayr that she does with her wealth? How then may anyone depart from the sayings of those we mentioned to what contradicts it? Then an objector said: We found on negating interdiction what is stronger than this, namely Allah's saying: O you who believe, when you contract a debt for a fixed term. Then He said after that: And if he who owes the debt is foolish or weak or unable to dictate himself, let his guardian dictate justly. He mentioned at the start of the passage contracting debts with one whom He described at its end as possibly foolish or weak; and in that is proof of the validity of his sale in his foolishness. Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and help, was: foolishness (safah) may be in wasting wealth, and may be in other than that with no wasting of wealth along with it; so it is in Arab speech: they say, so-and-so was foolish with his wealth, so-and-so was foolish in his religion. Among it is Allah's saying: And who turns away from the religion of Ibrahim except one who fools himself. Abu Ja'far said: I heard Wallad al-Nahwi saying: al-Masadiri narrated to me, from Abu Ubayda Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna, he said: He fooled himself means he destroyed and ruined it; and that may be from one who has in managing his wealth what one whose soundness in religion is undisputed does not have. al-Kisa'i said: The fool is one who knows the truth yet turns from it stubbornly, and he recited: Shall we believe as the fools believed? Indeed they are the fools. He said: meaning those who knew the matter yet stubbornly opposed it. And it is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as our narration of it preceded in this book of ours on pride, that it is one who rejects the truth; and in that is proof that what is meant thereby is one who has knowledge yet stubbornly opposes it and clings to its opposite. So in that is proof that the foolishness mentioned in the verse we recited is not foolishness of corrupting wealth, but other kinds of foolishness. One objector said: This verse which we interpreted is more indicative in the Qur'an for applying interdiction - and he is al-Shafi'i - he said: for in it is: let his guardian dictate justly. So among our proof against him in repelling his interpreting it of debt-contracting at its start by one described at its end with foolishness, and in that what repels what he said. If an objector says: who is his guardian meant at the end of this verse? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, is: he is the guardian of the religion owed by him; and in the verse is proof of this, namely His saying: Let him fear Allah his Lord and not diminish anything from it. Had his guardian been the one in charge over him as this objector held, he would not be addressed with this address, for he gains nothing for himself by diminishing; rather he was warned of that lest he reduce for the creditor part of what is owed him. In what we mentioned is clear proof of the corruption of that interpretation. Yet the doctrine on interdiction is to apply it and judge by it, and to preserve wealth for its owner when feared for from him. Abu Hanifa entered into part of this, saying: I withhold him after his majority from his wealth until he completes twenty-five years, yet I do not annul his disposals therein; and this is a saying whose corruption is obscure to no one, for if he withholds him from his wealth to preserve it for him from wasting it where it must not be wasted, then the very disposals in which its waste lies are what he preserved the wealth for him on account of; and if with preserving it for him he does not prevent him from wasting it himself, there is no meaning to preserving it for him. And he says along with this, on what one deserving interdiction over his wealth does before the judge interdicts him, what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad differed on: Abu Yusuf upheld it from him, while Muhammad ibn al-Hasan annulled it; so he considered his conditions, not the judge's ruling over him. We hold that Muhammad's saying therein is the worthier of the two in our view, for interdiction is only for a reason on whose account the judge interdicts one in whom that reason lies, so by his interdicting him he lightens him by his being in it before his interdicting him; and that is the doctrine of Malik ibn Anas therein; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5713","763 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever reaches Arafat has reached Hajj. 4860 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mur al-Di'li, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at Arafat, then some people of Najd came and asked him about Hajj. He said: Hajj is the day of Arafat; whoever reaches Jam' (Muzdalifa) before the Fajr prayer has reached Hajj. The days of Mina are three days of Tashriq; whoever hastens in two days, no sin on him, and whoever delays, no sin on him. Then he seated a man behind him who announced that."
"5714","4861 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mur, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention the Najdis asking him nor his seating the man behind. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you oppose it? For you say: whoever reaches the standing at Arafat before dawn still has remnants of Hajj upon him, among them standing at Muzdalifa, stoning the Jamarat, shaving, the Tawaf al-Ziyara which is the most binding, such that if he returned to his land without doing it he is ordered to return to Mecca to do it, remaining in his ihram sanctity; among them Tawaf al-Sadr though not like it in obligation; and the things we mentioned in this objection are expiated by blood, and one abandoning them need not return to Mecca for them as he must for abandoning the Tawaf of the Day of Sacrifice. How then is one in this state one who has reached Hajj? Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: standing at Arafat is such that after it Hajj is not missed, while missing it misses Hajj; and since Hajj may be missed by missing the standing at Arafat, with after it remaining causes of Hajj, it is permissible to say: whoever reaches the standing at Arafat has reached Hajj, for he has set out as one whose standing there misses Hajj. This is Arabic speech with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed Arabs who grasp his intent from it and understand its meaning therein. In what we mentioned is sound proof negating impossibility therein. Like this is what he addressed them with besides Hajj, namely his saying: Whoever reaches a rak'a of prayer has reached the prayer. Not meaning he is like one who prayed it all and need not pray the rest, but that he has reached of its reward what one who entered it from its start reached; and those he addressed understood his intent thereby, may Allah be pleased with them, for his language is their language, as He said: We sent no messenger except in his people's tongue to make clear to them. Since what he addressed them with made his intent clear to them, they needed no addition therein. As the Qur'an came with adjacent things in this meaning like this, among them: And if there were a Qur'an by which mountains were moved or the earth cut or the dead spoken to, nay, to Allah belongs the affair entirely. Needing nothing besides that over which language scholars differed, what is it? Some said: it is they would disbelieve in it; some said: it would be this Qur'an; Allah knows best His intent thereby. Likewise His saying: And were it not for Allah's bounty upon you and His mercy, and that Allah is Relenting, Wise. Needing no mention of what would be were it not for His bounty and mercy, for their understanding the intent thereby. In what we mentioned is uncovering of the meaning in what we narrated in this chapter; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5715","764 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The worthiest conditions you fulfill are those by which you made private parts lawful. 4862 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurra ibn Abi Khalifa al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salama al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worthiest conditions you fulfill are those by which you made private parts lawful."
"5716","4863 - Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salama al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muhammad, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Abu al-Khayr narrated to him, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, without mentioning in his isnad between Ibn Jurayj and Yazid ibn Abi Habib anyone. Abu Ja'far said: We examined whether Ibn Jurayj heard it from Yazid or took it from another from him."
"5717","4864 - We found Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'id ibn Ayyub narrated to me, thus he dictated to us, though it is really Ibn Abi Ayyub, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Abu al-Khayr narrated to him, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The worthiest conditions to be fulfilled are those by which you made private parts lawful. By that we established that Ibn Jurayj took this hadith from Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, from Yazid. Then we examined the text of this hadith to establish its intent, if Allah wills. We found Allah said in His Book: And give women their dowries graciously. And He said: And live with them in kindness. And He said: But if you dislike them, perhaps you dislike a thing. Urging them to retain them for what He knew for them therein of good in their doing that. Then He said: And if you gave one of them a heap of gold, take nothing from it; would you take it slanderously and in manifest sin. So He made their taking it from them where they ought not take it slander and manifest sin. Then He said: And how can you take it when some of you went into others and they took from you a solemn covenant. The going-in mentioned in this verse is intercourse between them, and the covenant mentioned therein is the contract in which their private parts were made lawful to those who married them. And Allah said upon His Messenger's tongue (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5718","4865 - What Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Azib ibn Shabib ibn Gharqada Abu Gharqad narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqada, from Sulayman ibn Amr, he being Ibn al-Ahwas al-Azdi, from Amr ibn al-Ahwas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivered a sermon in the Farewell Hajj, saying in his sermon: Beware, fear Allah regarding women, for they are captives with you; you took them by Allah's trust and made their private parts lawful by Allah's word. You have a right over them and they have a right over you. Among your rights over them is that they admit none into your houses except by your permission, nor let those you dislike tread your beds; if they do, abandon them in beds and strike them a non-severe striking. If they obey you, seek no way against them. Among their rights over you are their provision and clothing in kindness. Abu Ja'far said: The marriage contract entails these mentioned things for wives over husbands by the marriage contracts they conclude among them; they are thus stipulated by Allah for wives over husbands, hence worthiest to be fulfilled, for what humans stipulate for one another obliges whoever stipulated it to fulfill it to whom he stipulated it for himself; and when that is so for what some stipulate for others, what Allah stipulated for some over others is worthier to fulfill than anything else some stipulate for others. Especially what in violating its sanctity are the punishments made of penalties and hudud, some involving loss of lives. What is such is reasonably among things lifting that - namely punishment - with which its permissibility comes; and Allah described what He made its cause saying: And He placed between you love and mercy. What brings love and mercy, with their high rank, is opposite to what counters it of punishment by penalty and the rest we mentioned; and worthiest for people of minds is choosing what we mentioned of praiseworthy things over their opposites of blameworthy things; and with Allah is guidance."
"5719","765 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports some people narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on rejecting the testimony of one punished (mahdud) in Islam. 4866 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiya al-Fazari narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad al-Shami, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Urwa, he said: A'isha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The testimony of a traitor, male or female, is not permitted, nor of one flogged in a hadd, nor of one bearing rancor (ghimr) toward his brother, nor of one tried with false testimony, nor of the dependent (qani') living with a household for their sake, nor of one suspected regarding clientage or kinship. We examined this hadith and found in it the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying that the testimony of one flogged in a hadd is not permitted. We found al-Awza'i used to hold this doctrine, even saying of one flogged for wine: his testimony is not accepted even if he repents."
"5720","As Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari permitted us, from Mahmud ibn Khalid, from Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid, he said: I heard al-Awza'i saying: The testimony of one punished in Islam is not permitted, nor of one known for false testimony, nor of one suspected in clientage or kinship, nor a traitor, male or female, nor one bearing rancor against his brother, nor an adversary, nor a dubious one. al-Awza'i's wordings in this narration were the wordings of this hadith, except what is at its end of mentioning adversary and dubious. By that we established that he took this saying of his from that hadith, either from Yazid who narrated it from him Marwan, or from one higher than him above Yazid, namely al-Zuhri. We found for his saying that the testimony of one flogged in a hadd is not permitted no concurring one among scholars except al-Hasan ibn Salih ibn Hayy, for we found from him, as Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ru'asi mentioned from him, that he used to say: When a judge strikes a man in a hadd, his testimony is never permitted, even if he repents. This saying is opposed by the jurists of the cities besides them. Then we examined what they and the city jurists differed on of this meaning. We found things Allah forbade and threatened with severe punishments, of zina and theft; and punishments therein are expiations for their committers, among them cutting thieves' hands, and among them establishing the zina hadd on virgin fornicators, namely flogging, and on the married among them, namely stoning. We found scholars do not differ on accepting the testimony of those cut for thefts when they repent, nor on accepting the testimony of virgin fornicator punished ones when they repent; and in that is proof that other punished ones besides zina and theft are likewise. Except what Allah's Book excluded thereof in the qadhf hadd by His saying: And those who accuse chaste women then bring not four witnesses, flog them eighty lashes and never accept their testimony, and they are the defiantly disobedient. So He distinguished them from others, imposed fisq as their description, and followed that with: Except those who repent thereafter and reform, for Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. Scholars differed on accepting their testimony after repentance from what this was their ruling: some said it leaves them by repentance and they return to testimony acceptance; some said the fisq on which is the threat leaves them, but their testimony is never accepted. Among those holding the first view were most Hijazis; among those holding the second, some Hijazis and many besides them. As for the city jurists around whom fatwa revolves, like Malik and other Hijazis, they accept their testimony after repentance; so too al-Shafi'i said herein. As for Abu Hanifa, al-Thawri and their companions, they never accept it, making their ruling in rejecting it from them after repentance like their ruling in rejecting it before repentance. The Hijazis and those accepting their testimony after repentance clung to what they narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab of what he said to Abu Bakra after punishing him in what was from him regarding al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba."
"5721","As Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Abu Bakra: If you repent I shall accept your testimony, or repent and your testimony will be accepted. He said: We examined this hadith and found there entered in its isnad what prevents it being proof for one who adduces it against his opponent."
"5722","As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyayna, he said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: The Iraqis claim the qadhif's testimony is not permitted; I bear witness that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Abu Bakra: Repent and your testimony will be accepted, or if you repent I shall accept your testimony. He said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrating it thus many times, then I heard him saying: I doubted it. Al-Zuhri said: He informed me; when I stood I asked, and Umar ibn Qays who attended the gathering with me said to me: It is Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib. I said to Sufyan: Did you doubt it when he told you it is Sa'id? He said: No, except that doubt had entered me. Abu Ja'far said: The Umar mentioned in this hadith, by whom Sufyan sought corroboration therein, is Umar ibn Qays, who with hadith scholars is not firm therein; and when that is so, what one who doubted a hadith has confirmed by one who doubted does not remove his doubt therein. Then we found this hadith narrated from al-Zuhri by one of trustworthy, accepted narration, namely al-Layth ibn Sa'd."
"5723","As Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me that it reached him that Umar ibn al-Khattab sought Abu Bakra's repentance for his qadhf of al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba, but he refused to repent, claiming what he said was true, persisting therein, so no testimony was permitted for him. They also clung therein."
"5724","With what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn and Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Four testified against al-Mughira; Ziyad held back, so Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged the three and sought their repentance; two repented while Abu Bakra refused to repent. Their testimony was accepted when they repented, while Abu Bakra's testimony was not accepted because he refused to repent, though he was like a worn-out hide in worship. Those clinging to the first hadith said: this hadith has no defect, and none may abandon ruling by it. Among the proof of their opponents against them, by Allah's guidance and help, is that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib did not take this from Umar by hearing from him, but took it from him as a report (balaghan), for Sa'id, though he saw Umar, has no sound hearing of this from him. Proof the hadith was not strong with Sa'id is that he held contrary to its content."
"5725","As Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Qatada narrated to us, from al-Hasan and Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that they said: The qadhif when he repents, his repentance is between him and his Lord; his testimony is not accepted."
"5726","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad and text the like of it. That proves the first view was with Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib to abandon accepting the qadhif's testimony even if he repents; and we understood that what he narrated from him from Umar was not sound with him, for it is inconceivable with Umar's majesty and his high rank with him that he would say this saying, especially in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without their rebuking him or opposing him therein, then he abandons it for its opposite. One holding acceptance of the qadhif's testimony after repentance said: This saying is narrated from Ata', Tawus and Mujahid."
"5727","And he mentioned what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ulayya narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Najih, on the qadhif when he repents, he said: His testimony is accepted. He said: All of us say it: Ata', Tawus and Mujahid. And another mentioned regarding that."
"5728","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata': And never accept their testimony. He said: When he repents his testimony is accepted. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: Those grander than them opposed them therein, namely Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib; and concurred with him thereon one like him among those who judged for the Rightly-Guided Caliphs, namely Shurayh."
"5729","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Shaybani narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, he said: His testimony is not permitted when he repents, meaning the qadhif; his repentance is between him and his Lord. Opposed them therein one like them or above them, namely al-Hasan al-Basri. When they differed therein this divergence, we examined what they differed on and found they do not differ on qadhf: that it does not prevent accepting the qadhif's testimony before he is punished therein. Do you not see that if a man alone testifies against a man of zina, then testifies another testimony while outwardly just in his testimony, saying: I testified against him only truthfully, his testimony is accepted; yet when he is punished therein, then comes testifying another testimony while persisting in that testimony of his, his testimony is rejected, though the hadd established on him is purification for him if he was lying in his testimony. Since testimony is not rejected by what made him a qadhif outwardly, but rejected by the punishment established on him therein, namely the hadd he was punished with, and repentance if from him after that is only from the saying that was from him in the testimony he testified, and his testimony was not rejected by that saying but by other than it, namely flogging; and flogging admits no repentance, repentance being only in what preceded it of the testimony in which he was a qadhif, which did not annul testimony; rather what annulled testimony is the hadd after it. Testimony after flogging and before flogging is one in meaning; since it does not prevent testimony acceptance, and what prevents testimony acceptance is other than it, done to the witness; and his repentance is only from his own acts and sayings, not from what was done to him; rejecting his testimony after it remains on its ruling before it, for what rejected his testimony admits no repentance therein, repentance being only in other than it. In what we mentioned is sound proof affirming the saying of those holding rejection of testimony after repentance among those we mentioned; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5730","766 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports on the mother of Abd Allah ibn Shaddad from him, and from genealogists: who is she among the believing sisters? What was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereon. 4867 - Ali ibn Zayd al-Fara'idi narrated to us, he said: Abda ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Ya'qub and Abu Fazara, they said: Abd Allah ibn Shaddad narrated to us that the daughter of Hamza freed a freedman of hers; the freedman died leaving her and leaving his daughter. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her half and gave the daughter of Hamza half. Then he said, meaning Abd Allah ibn Shaddad: Do you know what is between me and her? She is my sister through my mother; our mother was Asma' bint Umays al-Khath'amiyya. Mus'ab al-Zubayri, whose place in genealogy is his place therein, used to say thereon, as Harun al-Asqalani permitted us, from al-Ala'i from him, he said: Abd Allah ibn Shaddad is a freedman of Banu Layth, his mother is Asma' bint Umays, and he was the brother of Hamza's daughter through her mother. We examined what was narrated thereon from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to establish thereby the truth therein, if Allah wills."
"5731","4868 - We found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Uqba, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believing sisters are four: the daughter of al-Harith, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Umm al-Fadl, daughter of al-Harith, mother of Ibn Abbas; Salma bint al-Harith, wife of Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib; and their maternal sister Asma' bint Umays al-Khath'amiyya."
"5732","4869 - We found Rawh ibn al-Faraj had narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith, of the believing sisters, the one with Hamza was Salma bint al-Harith, not Asma' bint Umays. What confirms it is Salma, not Asma', is what we narrated earlier in this book of ours on the dispute of Ja'far and Ali, sons of Abu Talib, and Zayd ibn Haritha, may Allah be pleased with them, before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over Hamza's daughter, and Ja'far's saying therein to Ali: I have of kinship the like of what you have, and her maternal aunt is with me, meaning Asma' bint Umays; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged her for Ja'far to be with her maternal aunt Asma'. In that is proof that Abd Allah ibn Shaddad was the son of Salma bint al-Harith, not the son of Asma' bint Umays. This is among the subtle inferences in such matters; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5733","767 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he relates from his Lord, that He said: Every deed of Adam's son is for him except fasting; it is Mine, and I reward for it. 4870 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed me that he heard Abu Hurayra, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every deed of Adam's son is for him except fasting; it is Mine, and I reward for it. By Him in whose hand is Muhammad's soul, the breath of the fasting one's mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk. We examined this hadith and found fasting has a meaning we find in no other devotional acts, among them prayer: a man may perform it intending thereby other than Allah, showing people his prayer while hiding his defect from them; so too his charity and his Hajj. Fasting is unlike that, for no one can see it from him as those acts are seen from their doers; rather Allah alone is singled out with its knowledge from him and awareness of it besides others. What He alone of creation is singled out with is His; what He knows, then informs His creation of who it is from, upon what we mentioned of what He had associates in - exalted is He - while what we mentioned of fasting is what He attains, outside it. So fasting was attributed to Allah in what we mentioned, while other described acts were not attributed to Him, since he may perform them; and it differed from Him in what Allah alone is singled out with of fasting and what others share with Him of besides it; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5734","768 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Abu Burda ibn Niyar about his sacrifice which he slaughtered: Repeat another in its place; and of his saying to him when he said to him: I have a jadha'a better than a musinna, he said to him: Slaughter it, but it will not suffice anyone after you. 4871 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Iyami, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi narrating, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us on the day of sacrifice to al-Baqi', began with two rak'as of prayer, then turned to us with his face and said: The first of our rites on this day is to begin with prayer, then return and slaughter. Whoever does that has conformed to our Sunna; whoever slaughtered before that, it is but meat he hastened for his family, not of the rite in anything. My maternal uncle stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I slaughtered, and I have a jadha'a better than a musinna. He said: Slaughter it, but it will not suffice, or avail, anyone after you."
"5735","4872 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Wahban ibn Uthman al-Baghdadiyyan narrated to us, they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Zubayd, Mansur, Dawud, Ibn Awn and Mujalid narrated to me, from al-Sha'bi - and this is Zubayd's hadith - he said: I heard al-Sha'bi here narrating from al-Bara' at a pillar in the mosque; had I been near it I would have told you its place; then he mentioned the like of it."
"5736","4873 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amir al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the Day of Sacrifice as a preacher, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Let none slaughter until he prays. My maternal uncle stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a day in which meat is disliked to be scarce, and I slaughtered my sacrifice and fed my family and neighbors. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You have done it, so repeat another slaughter. He said: I have a young milk-fed she-kid (anaq laban) better than two meat sheep. He said: It is the best of your two sacrifices; no jadha'a will suffice anyone after you."
"5737","4874 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"5738","4875 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, he said: Zubayd narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara', then he mentioned the like of it."
"5739","4876 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, from Firas, from Amir, from al-Bara', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"5740","4877 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Dalani, from Amir, from al-Bara', from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: The jadha'a meant in this hadith is the jadha'a of goats, not the jadha'a of sheep. Scholars differed on sacrifice: some among them said it is obligatory on those able, among them Abu Hanifa; most scholars besides him held it is commanded, encouraged, not obligatory. What those holding its obligation adduced was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to Abu Burda: No jadha'a will suffice anyone after you. He said: Sufficiency is only from an obligation. Among his opponent's proof against him therein is that the obligation on whose account this saying came is that Abu Burda, having slaughtered his sacrifice which he had made obligatory before its slaughter time, consuming it wherein it was, it became his, thus its replacement became due upon him; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him what he said on account of his consuming an obligation he owed Allah by his making it obligatory. We examined what they said therein to establish the truth, if Allah wills. We found things obligatory by Allah's making them obligatory, when servants make them obligatory on themselves, their making them obligatory has no meaning. Do you not see that one who makes obligatory on himself one of the five prayers, or the fasting of Ramadan, or the Hajj of Islam while able to reach it, nothing binds him thereby, being like one who did not make it obligatory. If sacrifice were obligatory by Allah's making it obligatory, servants' making it obligatory on themselves would be meaningless; and if not obligatory by Allah's making it obligatory, whoever made it obligatory on himself, it became obligatory on him by his making it obligatory. Yet if Allah made sacrifice obligatory, He did not make it obligatory in a specific sheep, cow or camel; so when a man places what is due upon him thereof by Allah's obligation in something specific thereof, it may become due as he made it due. We examined that and saw what he made due in something specific thereof, had it perished by death or otherwise, what Allah made due upon him would not lapse, for He did not make it due upon him in what perished thereof. And since that is so, we understood that what he made due, if Allah made sacrifice obligatory, is other than what he made due; its perishing and remaining are one in meaning. This is the ruling of sacrifice if Allah made it obligatory. Then we examined its ruling if Allah did not make it obligatory. We found scholars do not differ that whoever made it obligatory, it became due upon him; and whoever consumes it before executing it wherein he made it due owes its value until he spends it wherein it must be spent, of its replacement. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not consider the value of what Abu Burda slaughtered, binding him with it for Allah. By that we understood: what he bound him with, for what he bound him therewith, is for other than what he made due on himself, but for what Allah made due upon him. By that the obligation of sacrifice upon those able is established. What Abu Hanifa adduced therein is among the best adduced in such; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5741","769 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his sending those he sent to fight those he sent them to fight without a command (imra) with which he had placed them in charge. 4878 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, he said: al-Mujalid ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqa, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Medina, Juhayna came to him and said: You have settled among us, so make a firm pact for us that we may trust you and you trust us; so he made a pact for them even if they had not submitted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us in Rajab, ordering us to raid a clan of Kinana next to Juhayna. We raided them, but they were many, so we sought refuge with Juhayna and they protected us, saying: Why do you fight in the sacred month? We said: We fight only those who expelled us from the sacred land in the sacred month. Some of us said to others: What do you think? They said: Let us go to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him; others said: No, let us stay here. I said with some people with me: No, let us go to this caravan of Quraysh and cut it off. So we went to the caravan - booty then being whoever took something it was his - while our companions went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He stood angry, red-faced, and said: You went together and came divided; those before you were destroyed only by division. I shall send over you a man not the best of you, the most patient of you in hunger and thirst. So he sent over us Abd Allah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi, and he was the first commander in Islam. So in this hadith is proof that army had no commander. Then an objector said: How do you accept this when you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He mentioned what we mentioned earlier in this book from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you over you. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: Sa'd's hadith was earlier; among those sent in what they were sent for was their disagreement. So by Allah's disliking disagreement, He directed His Prophet's affairs thereafter contrary to it, appointing commanders over his armies so matters return to one word, obedience to whom is due by those with him without departing from his word. That is strengthened by what He revealed in His Book: And do not dispute, lest you fail and your strength depart; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5742","770 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports of what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the sacred months: raiding his enemies therein or leaving that until they pass. 4879 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not raid in the sacred month - Abu al-Walid thinks he said - unless he was raided; when it came he stood until it passed."
"5743","4880 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, he said: al-Hadrami narrated to me, from Abu al-Sawwar, from Jundub ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a group and placed over them Abu Ubayda, or Ubayda ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with them. When he set out to go, he wept longing for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sat down, and he sent Abd Allah ibn Jahsh, writing him a letter, ordering him not to read it until he reaches such-and-such place, saying: Compel none of your companions to march. When he reached the place, he read the letter and said: I seek Allah's refuge; hearing and obedience to Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He told them the news and read the letter to them; two men among them turned back while the rest went on. They met Ibn al-Hadrami and killed him, not knowing whether that day was of Rajab or of Jumada. The polytheists said to the Muslims: You killed in the sacred month. So Allah revealed: They ask you about the sacred month, fighting therein; say: Fighting therein is grave. The polytheists said: If there is no sin, there is no reward for them; so Allah revealed: Indeed those who believed and emigrated and strove in Allah's way, to the end of the verse."
"5744","4881 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Raqqashi narrated to us, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard my father, he said: al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Abu al-Sawwar, Abu al-Sawwar narrating to him, from Jundub ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali, then he mentioned the like of it exactly. Then an objector said: In this hadith is prohibition of fighting in the sacred month for one not fought, while you narrate from more than one of the predecessors contrary to that, and follow them thereon."
"5745","And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhrama ibn Bukayr informed me, from his father, from Ibn al-Musayyib - and I sought his fatwa: may Muslims fight disbelievers in the sacred month? Ibn al-Musayyib said: Yes; Bukayr said: Sulayman ibn Yasar said that too. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: that ruling is abrogated by what was revealed in Surat Bara'a."
"5746","As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiya ibn Salih narrated to me, from Ali ibn Abi Talha, from Ibn Abbas, on His saying: Freedom from Allah and His Messenger to those polytheists you covenanted with; travel in the land four months. He said: Allah set for those who covenanted His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) four months to travel therein wherever they wished, and set for those with no covenant the passing of the sacred months, from the Day of Sacrifice to the end of Muharram, fifty nights: But if they repent and establish prayer and give zakat, let them go. When the sacred months passed, He ordered him to put the sword to those he covenanted if they did not enter Islam, annulling what he named for them of covenant and pact, removing the appointed time and the first condition. Then He said: Except those you covenanted at the Sacred Mosque, meaning Mecca's people: So long as they are upright to you, be upright to them; Allah loves the God-fearing. And His saying: If they prevail over you they observe toward you neither kinship nor pact. His saying illan is kinship, and pact is dhimma. When Bara'a was revealed, covenants were broken, and he fought the polytheists wherever he found them, lying for them in every ambush, until they entered Islam; none of the Arabs sheltered thereby after Bara'a. So this hadith proves all covenants were cut by what we recited in Surat Bara'a, and fighting became lawful in all time. It led us to accept Ali ibn Abi Talha's narration from Ibn Abbas though he did not meet him, for in reality it is from him from Mujahid and Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas. Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Qadi narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Fahd saying: I heard Ahmad ibn Hanbal saying in Egypt: Mu'awiya ibn Salih's book on interpretation - if a man entered Egypt, wrote it, then left with it, I would not see his legs gone in vain; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5747","771 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one who prays with gathered-up hair (ma'qus). 4882 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz, that is Ibn Abi Rawwad, informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Musa informed me, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me that he saw Abu Rafi', freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), pass by al-Hasan ibn Ali, peace be upon them, praying with his braid stuck into his nape; so Abu Rafi' undid it. Hasan turned to him angrily, so Abu Rafi' said: Attend to your prayer and do not be angry, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: That is the devil's share (kifl), meaning the devil's seat, that is the insertion of his braid. Abu Ja'far said: We did not know who this Imran ibn Musa is until I heard Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Razi saying: I heard Salih ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, he said: Ali ibn Abd Allah, that is Ibn al-Madini, narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyayna asking about Isma'il ibn Umayya and Ayyub ibn Musa which is older. He said: Ayyub was black-headed and bearded, while Isma'il dyed; it was said to Sufyan: Did they die in one year? He said: No, Ayyub died before these came, meaning the Musawwida. It was said to Sufyan: And Imran ibn Musa? He said: He was the eldest of them, and we did not see he had any hadith. He said: By that we knew this Imran is the brother of Ayyub ibn Musa. Then we examined what is mentioned in this hadith from Sa'id al-Maqburi of his seeing Abu Rafi' do to al-Hasan ibn Ali what he mentions he did to him. We found it very remote, for Abu Rafi' died long ago, his death being in Ali's time, peace be upon him, and Ali was his executor over his wealth and children."
"5748","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that Ali, peace be upon him, paid zakat on the wealth of Abu Rafi's sons and handed it to them, but they found it short. They said: We found it short. He said: Do you want me to have wealth on which I pay no zakat. By that we established: al-Maqburi cannot have seen of Abu Rafi' what he relates in this hadith that he saw of him. For al-Maqburi's death, as Muhammad ibn Sa'd, al-Waqidi's companion, mentioned from al-Waqidi in the book al-Tabaqat, was at the start of Hisham's caliphate in the year 125; between that and Ali ibn Abi Talib's death, peace be upon him, are eighty-five years. Abu Rafi's death was before that by what Allah willed. We found on forbidding a man praying with gathered hair nothing but what was narrated from Ali, peace be upon him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5749","4883 - As Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ali, I love for you what I love for myself and dislike for you what I dislike for myself; do not pray with your hair gathered (aqis), for it is the devil's share."
"5750","4884 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"5751","4885 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he did not say in his hadith: for it is the devil's share. Abu Ja'far said: This is among what the praying one must not do in his prayer: he should let his hair loose when prostrating with his prostration; likewise he does with his garments. We know no disagreement among scholars therein; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5752","772 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: This wealth is sweet and green. 4886 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Khawla, he said: We came to her to ask her about a hadith she heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she had been under Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, then a man of Banu Zurayq married her after him. Her husband came while we were with her and said: What brought you? We said: We came to her to ask her about a hadith she heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said to her: Look what you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for lying about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not like other lying. She said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having visited his uncle to console him, saying: This wealth is green and sweet; whoever takes it rightfully is blessed therein. Many a one plunging into what his soul desires of Allah's and His Messenger's wealth shall have the Fire on the Day of Resurrection. We examined this hadith and found it of Isma'il ibn Umayya's hadith from al-Maqburi, affirming his taking it from Khawla by hearing from her; and the one who narrated it from him is Muslim ibn Khalid. Then we found Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar contradicted Muslim in this hadith's isnad, stating it is from Isma'il, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayra, not from Khawla."
"5753","4887 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This wealth is green and sweet; whoever takes it rightfully is blessed therein. Many a one plunging into Allah's and His Messenger's wealth (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what his soul desires shall have the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"5754","4888 - And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Dawud al-Attar narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. We examined Muslim's narration of this hadith from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Khawla: is it in reality as he narrated it from her."
"5755","4889 - We found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam had narrated to us; al-Rabi' said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed us; and Muhammad said: My father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth informed us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us; then both agreed saying: from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Ubayd Abu al-Walid, he said: I heard Khawla bint Qays ibn Qahd, she having been under Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: This wealth is sweet and green; whoever attains it rightfully is blessed therein. Many a one plunging into what his soul wills of Allah's and His Messenger's wealth shall have nothing on the Day of Resurrection but the Fire. By that we established that Sa'id al-Maqburi did not hear this hadith from Khawla, but heard it from Ubayd Abu al-Walid from her. This Ubayd is the one called Sanuta, as Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari mentioned."
"5756","4890 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Ubayd Sanuta, from Khawla bint Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith."
"5757","4891 - And as al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb ibn Hayyan al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Ubayd Sanuta, from Khawla bint Qays, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Then we examined what is in this hadith of Khawla's mention: is it as in this hadith or not?"
"5758","4892 - We found Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la had narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Haywa ibn Shurayh, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed me that he heard al-Nu'man ibn Abi Ayyash al-Ansari saying: He heard Khawla bint Thamir saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: This wealth is green and sweet; many a one plunging into Allah's and His Messenger's wealth (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without right shall have the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"5759","4893 - We found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi had narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'a narrated to us, he said: Haywa informed us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith Khawla is attributed to Thamir. It is possible that Qays ibn Qahd, to whom she was attributed in what we narrated before this, was nicknamed Thamir; so some narrated her hadith by her father's real name, and some narrated it by the nickname he was called. Then we examined his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): This wealth is green and sweet. He mentioned wealth, masculine, with what feminines are described with, saying khadira hulwa, not khadiran hulwan. That in our view, Allah knows best, refers wealth to this world, since wealth exists only therein. He emphasized that with what Arabs emphasize things they emphasize with: when they intend that they use therein such in good and evil alike; they say in good: so-and-so is allama, so-and-so is nassaba; and say in evil: so-and-so is humaza, so-and-so is lumaza; in things of this kind, what we mentioned thereof suffices; and from Allah we ask guidance. This hadith is also narrated from Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, from the Messenger of Allah."
"5760","4894 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Ma'bad al-Juhani, from Mu'awiya, that he would hardly narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything, yet he would not omit these words every Friday, narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. This wealth is sweet and green; whoever takes it rightfully, Allah blesses it for him. Beware of excessive praise, for it is slaughter."
"5761","773 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what decides for some reciters over others in their disagreement in reciting min ladunni, with doubling and lightening. 4895 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Hamza al-Zayyat, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he mentioned anyone and supplicated for him, began with himself. One day he said: Allah's mercy upon us and upon Musa; had he remained with his companion, he would have seen wonders. But he said: If I ask you about anything after it, keep me no company; you have attained from me (min ladunni) - doubled."
"5762","4896 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida, from Hamza al-Zayyat, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: If I ask you about anything after it, keep me no company; you have attained from me an excuse (min ladunni udhran) - doubled."
"5763","4897 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Umayya ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting: You have attained from me an excuse (qad balaghta min ladunni udhran) - with doubled nun. He said: This is among what we know no opponent of its narrator therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he narrated from him thereon. As for the reciters' disagreement therein."
"5764","And as Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, al-A'mash said: min ladunni - doubled; Hamza likewise; Abu Amr likewise; Asim: ladunni with kasra on nun, jazm on dal, giving it a hint of damma, and nasb on lam in the sura; min ladunhu likewise; and Nafi' has min ladunni lightened. In what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us, from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam in his book on recitations, he said: His saying: You have attained from me an excuse. Nafi' and others of Medina's people recited it with fath on lam and lightening of nun with damm on dal: laduni. So too Asim recited it, except he gave lam a hint of damma with jazm on dal: ladni. As for al-A'mash, Abu Amr, Hamza and al-Kisa'i, they doubled the nun with fath on lam and damm on dal: ladunni. Abu Ubayd said: So is the recitation with us, and it is the high language; the nun was doubled to preserve its sukun, it being originally sakin, like their saying in min and an. Do you not see the nun of them is originally sakin, as your saying min fulan and anka; when you annex to yourself you say minni and anni, adding a second nun to preserve the sukun therein. Had you said mini and ani lightened, the sukun would go and the nun turn to kasra; hence they said minni and anni with doubling, so ladunni. Abu Ja'far said: Among what came in the Qur'an on the plural nun in ladun: Had We intended to take a pastime We would have taken it from Us (min ladunna); Did We not establish for them a safe sanctuary to which fruits of everything are brought as provision from Us (min ladunna); and affection from Us (min ladunna) and purity. In their consensus on what we mentioned is proof that the worthiest recitations in what we mentioned their disagreement therein is what al-A'mash, Hamza and Abu Amr recited upon what we mentioned from them therein, especially strengthened by what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein agreeing with what they recited thereon; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5765","774 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, of his saying to Abu Barza when he sought his permission to kill the man he sought his permission to kill: It belonged to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and in that, what is it? Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abd Allah ibn Mutarrif ibn al-Shikhkhir, that he narrated to them, from Abu Barza al-Aslami, he said: We were with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, in his work; he became angry at a Muslim man, his anger intensifying greatly. When I saw that, I said: O successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shall I strike his neck? When I mentioned killing, he turned from that talk entirely. When we dispersed, he sent for me thereafter and said: O Abu Barza, what did you say? I had forgotten what I said. I said: Remind me. He said: Do you not remember the day you said such-and-such; would you have done it? I said: Yes, by Allah, had you ordered me I would have done it. He said: Woe to you, that by Allah belongs to none after Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5766","Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abd Allah ibn Mutarrif, from Abu Barza al-Aslami, he said: One day I was with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, and his anger intensified against a Muslim man. I said: O successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shall I strike his neck? He left me without speaking to me. Then I met him after days and he mentioned what I said. He said: I said: What did I say? He said: Do you remember the day you were with me and my anger intensified against a Muslim man, and you said: O successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), shall I strike his neck? I said: Now, if you order me I shall do it. Abu Bakr said: That belongs to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is Abu Bakr's saying to Abu Barza what is therein of what we mentioned therein. It is possible he meant, that is Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, by his saying it belonged to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that none kills anyone for his anger against him. And it is possible it means none is killed except by the order of one who orders his killing, until the ordered one knows his deserving it; and those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are not obeyed therein as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was obeyed therein, for he is trusted in his acts and sayings, and his sayings and acts are referred to Allah, belief in them due, matters executed thereon, while others therein are unlike him. Then we found this hadith narrated in other wordings."
"5767","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, he said: I heard Abu Sawwar narrating, from Abu Barza, he said: I came upon Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, while he had spoken harshly to a man, and the man answered him back. I said: Shall I not strike his neck? He rebuked me and said: It belongs to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Yet we found this hadith differed over us on who is between Amr ibn Murra and Abu Barza in its isnad: Shu'ba said therein: from Amr, I heard Abu Sawwar narrating from Abu Barza; while al-A'mash said: from Amr ibn Murra, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu Barza."
"5768","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiya narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Amr ibn Murra, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu Barza, he said: Abu Bakr became enraged at a man, so I said: Who is he, O successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Why? I said: To strike his neck if you order me that. He said: Would you do it? I said: Yes. He said: By Allah, the gravity of my word I said removed his anger; then he said: It belonged to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we found its narrators from al-A'mash, from Amr, differing therein too: Abu Mu'awiya says therein: from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, while Hafs ibn Ghiyath says therein: from Abu al-Bakhtari."
"5769","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Murra narrated to us, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from Abu Barza, he said: I saw Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of it. And Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad agreed with Hafs on what he narrated from him."
"5770","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: Abu Barza al-Aslami narrated to me, he said: I reached Abu Bakr, then he mentioned the like of it. We found this hadith also from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa's narration, from Amr ibn Murra, agreeing with Shu'ba thereon."
"5771","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd, that is Ibn Abi Unaysa, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abu Sawwar, from Abu Barza al-Aslami, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry at a man, we never saw anger severer than his that day. Abu Barza said to him: O successor of the Messenger of Allah, order me and I shall strike his neck. He said: It was as if a fire was extinguished. He said: Then Abu Barza left; then Abu Bakr sent for him and said: May your mother lose you, what did you say? He said: I said: By Allah, if you order me to kill him I shall kill him. He said: May your mother lose you, O Abu Barza, it belonged to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: It is possible that what belonged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereof is killing one whose way is the way mentioned in these reports, and that belongs to none after him. Then we found this hadith also came in other wordings, with meanings besides the meanings we mentioned before, among them."
"5772","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Tawba al-Anbari, from Abu Sawwar, from Abu Barza: A man reviled Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, so I said: Shall I not strike his neck, O successor of the Messenger of Allah? He said: No, this belongs to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in this hadith is that man's reviling Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr's saying to Abu Barza when he sought his permission to kill him for that: This belongs to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). That meaning differed from the meanings mentioned in what we narrated before of these reports. It was reasonable: whoever reviles the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a disbeliever, lawful in blood, unlike whoever reviles others. So the meaning intended thereby in this hadith of Abu Barza of the Messenger of Allah's particularity with what he was singled out, besides people who undertake affairs after him, became confused to us. Then we found scholars differed on this and its likes of what rulers order others among people: may the ordered ones comply, or may they not? Some said: compliance is open to the ordered to do by their rulers' orders and by orders of others vested therewith. Among those saying that: Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad."
"5773","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Ya'qub, from Abu Hanifa, without disagreement he mentioned from them therein. Yet Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said afterwards in his Nawadir which Muhammad ibn Sama'a related from him, and we took them from Ibn Abi Imran as discussed with us therefrom, that he said: The ordered one may not do that until the one ordering him therewith is just with him, and until another just one besides him testifies with him thereon against the one ordered therein therewith in other than zina; and in zina it does not avail him until three men testify with him against the one ordered therein therewith, of its deserving upon him upon what he ordered him therein with what he ordered him therein. We know for scholars in this chapter no saying besides these two sayings. What we mentioned from the holders of the first of them, they meant thereby the just among the two commanders, not others; for whoever departs from the justice by which he deserved authority over what he undertakes to its opposite is removed from authority thereover, deposed therefrom, no longer its governor. The second of these two sayings has by analogy no meaning, for the one ordered with what we mentioned has no hearing testimony from a witness thereby, since he is no judge to hear that by what is his of hearing what he applies in his judgments. By that the corruption of this saying is manifest, and the first saying stands, since in this chapter are only these two sayings; when one is negated the other stands. Then we examined: was anything narrated in this chapter besides the hadith of Abu Barza we mentioned or not?"
"5774","4898 - We found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Alqama ibn Mujazziz al-Mudliji over an army; he sent a detachment and placed over them Abd Allah ibn Hudhafa al-Sahmi, a man in whom was jesting. Before them was a kindled fire; he said to his companions: Is not my obedience obligatory upon you? They said: Yes. He said: Plunge into this fire. A man stood, gathering himself to enter it; then he laughed and said: I was only jesting. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he laughed and said: Had they done this, do not obey them in disobedience to Allah."
"5775","4899 - We found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: Alqama ibn Muhazziz, with ha'. Abu Ja'far said: It was reasonable that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed Abd Allah ibn Hudhafa over what he placed him over, that was for them to obey him in what he orders them of what is his to order them; hence those among them who intended to throw themselves into the fire when he ordered them that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do not obey them in disobedience to Allah. By that he excluded their ordering them to disobey Allah from what he placed over them of obeying those he placed over them. In that is proof of the first of the two sayings we mentioned in this chapter. By that is manifest: the meaning of Abu Bakr's saying, may Allah be pleased with him, that it belonged to none after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is that he meant thereby: none may order killing anyone for reviling reviled by others than him, by which the like applies to whoever reviles the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and others therein; for whoever reviles the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a disbeliever, his killing due upon his umma, ordered therewith or not. Whoever reviles others among rulers after him deserves therefor discipline, like discipline; as for other than that of what his exit from Islam to disbelief entails, no; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5776","775 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever kills deliberately, retaliation (qawad) is his due. 4900 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Kathir, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed in blind tumult (immiyya) and throwing among them with stone, whip or stick, his blood-money is that of error; whoever kills deliberately, retaliation seizes his hand. Whoever stands between him and it, upon him is Allah's curse, the angels' and all people's; Allah accepts from him neither sarf nor adl."
"5777","What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus, the like of it, without mentioning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor Ibn Abbas. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: Sufyan used to narrate it thus latterly, while before that he narrated it as Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated it. Even had they differed, Sulayman is acceptable in narration, firm therein, among those whose solitary narration would be accepted; and when so, his addition over others in a hadith is accepted over them. Then we examined the meaning of his saying: retaliation seizes his hand. That in our view, Allah knows best, means the due for the slain's guardian likewise is retaliation, nothing else. An objector said: You narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this meaning contrary to what you mentioned. And he mentioned what."
"5778","4901 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salama narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayra narrated to me, he said: When Allah granted His Messenger Mecca's conquest, Hudhayl killed a man of Banu Layth for a slain one of theirs in Jahiliyya. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and preached, saying in his sermon: Whoever has a slain one, he has the better of two views: either to kill, or to take blood-money."
"5779","4902 - And what Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the slain's guardian choice between the two mentioned things, while in the hadith you narrated before he gave him one thing, namely retaliation; this is severe discrepancy. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: there is no discrepancy as imagined. In the first hadith we narrated from Ibn Abbas is mention of the due, namely retaliation; and in Abu Hurayra's hadith we narrated after: the slain's guardian may kill, which is the retaliation in Ibn Abbas's hadith. That in our view, Allah knows best, refers to the killer's paying blood-money to the slain's guardian and the guardian's accepting it from him; that is in the meaning of settlement from blood for the blood-money paid to him. This objector said: Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i narrated this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating contrary to what you mentioned. And he mentioned what."
"5780","4903 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya, that is Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, he said: Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu Shurayh al-Ka'bi saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his sermon on the day of Mecca's conquest: You, company of Khuza'a, killed this slain one of Hudhayl, and I shall pay his blood-money. Whoever has a slain one after my saying, his family are between two choices: to take the blood-money, or to kill. He said: In this hadith is the slain's guardian's taking blood-money from the killer, without clarifying that is by his paying it to them. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: that in this hadith is no contradiction to Abu Hurayra's hadith we narrated before; for in Abu Hurayra's hadith is paying from the killer, and in Abu Shurayh's hadith is the guardian's taking from the killer. Correcting both is upon paying from the killer per Abu Hurayra's hadith, and taking by the guardian thereof per Abu Shurayh's hadith. This is a question scholars differed on: some say this saying we mentioned, correcting both hadiths thereon, the doctrine of Hijazis and Iraqis alike; others say the slain's guardian may take blood-money from the killer whether he wills or not, adducing what this interpreter interpreted this hadith upon. Among those holding that: al-Shafi'i. They said: the killer must save his life; when he does not do his duty he is seized therewith, though unwilling. Our answer to whoever adduced that: the killer must save his life as mentioned, and must save it by blood-money and other wealth he owns, until thereby he preserves his blood. All agreed: had the slain's guardian sought the killer's house or slave to take it from him, lifting retaliation from him, upon the killer between him and his Lord is to do that, yet he is not compelled thereon if he refuses. What else of his wealth is likewise: he is not compelled to save his life therewith, nor seized thereof unwillingly. This objector said: Why then was mentioning this needed? It was said to him: Because the law among Banu Isra'il in deliberate killing was retaliation, nothing else; retaliation was due upon the killer, none deflecting it from him. Allah lightened for this umma by what He revealed in His Book thereon."
"5781","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Retaliation was among Banu Isra'il with no blood-money among them. So Allah said to this umma: Prescribed for you is retaliation in slain ones, the free for the free, to His saying: But whoever is pardoned by his brother anything. Pardoning is accepting blood-money in deliberate killing: That is lightening from your Lord, from what was prescribed upon those before you. So in this hadith from Ibn Abbas is his informing of the meaning on whose account the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached on the day of Mecca's conquest what he preached of permitting taking blood-money in deliberate blood, for that was forbidden upon those before his umma and not of their religion's laws; Allah made it of his law and what He bound his umma with, so he preached it to people that they may know it."
"5782","Hammad ibn Salama narrated this hadith from Amr, opposing Ibn Uyayna in its isnad and falling short in some wordings. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Hammad, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas: Prescribed for you is retaliation in slain ones, the free for the free, to the end of the verse. He said: Retaliation was prescribed upon Banu Isra'il, and you were permitted blood-money: But whoever is pardoned by his brother anything, then following with kindness and payment to him with goodness; that is lightening from your Lord. He said: From what was prescribed upon Banu Isra'il in what returned to permission; it was not taken from whom it is taken except with his willing it. In what we mentioned is sufficiency and proof, and that there is no contradiction in anything we narrated in this chapter; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5783","4904 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from al-Harith ibn Fudayl, from Sufyan ibn Abi al-Awja', from Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever suffers blood or wound - meaning by khabl wounds - his guardian has choice among one of three: to pardon, to retaliate, or to take blood-money. If he refuses the fourth, seize his hand. If he accepts one of them then transgresses after that, his is the Fire, abiding therein forever."
"5784","4905 - And what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad, that is Ibn al-Awwam, narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: al-Harith ibn Fudayl informed me, from Sufyan ibn Abi al-Awja', from Abu Shurayh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the slain's guardian has choice between pardoning, retaliating or taking blood-money. It was reasonable therein: his pardoning admits no taking blood-money with it, as his taking blood-money admits no pardoning with it; by that this saying too is void. Then we tripled with what al-Awza'i said of obliging the guardian to take blood-money from the killer whether he wills or not, after our standing on what is in the verse we recited, namely that Allah only prescribed upon us in our slain retaliation, nothing else. It was reasonable that the right Allah made for him not shift to other than it except with the consent of both on whom it shifts thereby and who holds the blood. By that this saying too is void. Nothing remains in this chapter but the saying we mentioned therein from the first group, namely retaliation, and that it not shift to other than it except with the consent of both the killer and who holds the blood; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5785","777 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his prayer over the grave of one over whose grave he prayed without attending his burial. 4906 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Qudama al-Missisi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over a grave after three days. Scholars differed on praying over the grave of one buried without prayer over him: a group among them said prayer is over his grave until three days pass, not exceeding more; holding the dead after that leaves the state of one prayed over. Among those holding that: Abu Hanifa and his companions. In this hadith we narrated from Ibn Abbas is what refutes that; what they said thereof is timing, and timing is taken only by divine designation. Moreover we saw many buried dead exhumed after a period exceeding the long period, in a state had they been in it outside graves prayer over them would be due; by that this saying's corruption is established. We found drowned dead brought out after days exceeding this time prayed over; when so for the drowned, others of the dead are likewise, so long as their bodies exist. When their bodies are gone by decay or otherwise, becoming nonexistent, it is reasonable they not be prayed over. This is the saying with us in this chapter; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5786","778 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his prayer over Uhud's slain eight years after their killing. 4907 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Ibn Lahi'a informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Abu al-Khayr informed him that he heard Uqba ibn Amir saying: The last sermon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached to us: he prayed over Uhud's martyrs, then ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: I am your forerunner (farut), and I am a witness over you."
"5787","4908 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and prayed over Uhud's people as his prayer over the dead. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Uhud's slain eight years after their killing. It is possible that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) because martyrs' Sunna before that was no prayer over them; then Allah made prayer over them of their Sunna, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over them for that. Then an objector said: How do you accept this when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed over them at their killing? And he mentioned what."
"5788","4909 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on Uhud's day, would have ten placed before him; he would pray over them and over Hamza, then the ten would be lifted while Hamza remained placed; then ten would be placed and he would pray over them with Hamza among them."
"5789","4910 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered on Uhud's day regarding the slain; he began praying over them, placing nine with Hamza, magnifying over them seven takbirs, then they would be lifted leaving Hamza; then nine would be brought and he would magnify over them seven until he finished them. The objector said: In this hadith he had prayed over them at their killing; and when that is so of one who prayed before that over the buried, he may repeat prayer over him. In its permissibility for him is permissibility for others to pray over him too. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: What was narrated of this hadith from Ibn Abbas was opposed therein by Jabir and Anas ibn Malik. As."
"5790","4911 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, that Jabir ibn Abd Allah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Uhud's slain buried in their blood, neither prayed over them nor were they washed."
"5791","4912 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me that Ibn Shihab narrated to him that Anas ibn Malik narrated to him: Uhud's martyrs were not washed; buried in their blood, no prayer over them. Abu Ja'far said: So Jabir and Anas report the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not prayed over them at their killing. It is possible he had not prayed over them, while others prayed over them by his order. We examined whether anything was narrated therein or not."
"5792","4913 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on Uhud's day, passed by Hamza, peace be upon him, mutilated; he said: Were Safiyya not to grieve, I would have left him until Allah gathers him from birds' bellies and beasts. He shrouded him in a striped cloak (namira); when his head was covered his feet showed, when his feet were covered his head showed; so he covered his head. He prayed over none of the martyrs besides him, saying: I am a witness over you today. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed over none of Uhud's martyrs besides Hamza. His praying over Hamza and leaving others may have been for what occupied him that day of what befell him in his face and the helmet's crushing on his head. As."
"5793","4914 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim and Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi informed me, from Abu Hazim - Sa'id said in his hadith: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd, and Ibn Abi Hazim said from Sahl - that he was asked about the Messenger of Allah's face on Uhud's day, with what it was treated. Sahl said: The helmet broke on his head, his front tooth broke, his face was wounded. Fatima would wash him, Ali, peace be upon him, pouring water with the shield (mijann). When Fatima saw water only increased blood, she took a piece of mat, burnt it and stuck it on his wound, and the blood stopped. Ibn Abi Hazim's and Sa'id's wording in this hadith differ, meaning one."
"5794","4915 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah's wrath intensified against people who bloodied the Messenger of Allah's face, crushed the helmet on him and broke his front tooth."
"5795","4916 - And as Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had his front tooth broken on Uhud's day and his face gashed; he began wiping blood from his face, saying: How shall people prosper who gashed their Prophet's face, broke his front tooth, while he calls them to Allah. So Allah revealed: You have nothing of the affair. Abu Ja'far said: It is possible he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left prayer over them for what occupied him of the pain of what befell him, except Hamza, whom he singled out with prayer for his place with him. Then an objector said: The hadith you mentioned of the Messenger of Allah's singling Hamza with prayer over him is narrated contrary to what Uthman ibn Umar, whom you mentioned that therein in his hadith from him, narrated from him. And he mentioned what."
"5796","4917 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Hamza was shrouded in a striped cloak (namira); when they spread it over his head his feet emerged, when over his feet his head emerged. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to bring it forward over his head and place idhkhir grass on his feet. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were Safiyya not to grieve, we would have left Hamza unburied until gathered from birds' bellies and beasts. Ibn Wahb did not mention therein the Messenger of Allah's prayer over Hamza. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: Ibn Wahb, though he did not mention that, Uthman ibn Umar added over him from Usama what is in his hadith of affirming prayer over him; both, by Allah's praise, trustworthy, firm, acceptable in narration. Whoever adds, being such, over another an addition in a hadith both narrated, his addition is accepted. Then an objector said: You mentioned in the chapter before this chapter that when the dead vanishes by decay or otherwise, becoming nonexistent, no prayer over his grave; yet in Uqba's hadith you narrated: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over Uhud's slain eight years after their killing. This hadith is proof against you for what you mentioned thereof, for the dead decay in less than that period. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, was: Uhud's martyrs, the Messenger of Allah knew they had not decayed, remaining, for what Allah revealed to him on them of His saying: Think not those killed in Allah's way dead; nay, alive with their Lord, provided for. So he prayed over them for that. It is narrated of their existing in the states Allah mentioned in this verse after multiples of this period of time."
"5797","What Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: When Mu'awiya wanted to run the spring by the martyrs' graves in Medina, he ordered a crier who called: Whoever has a dead one, let him come to him. Jabir said: I went to my father; we brought them out supple, bending. A pick struck a man's finger among them and it burst with blood. So in this hadith is proof of their bodies' remaining after the period in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over them. Thus we say: whoever is known to remain bodily after a period, however long, in his grave, prayer over his grave is permitted if he was not prayed over before burial, following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein; and from Allah we ask guidance."
"5798","779 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by which those holding free sale of the mudabbar argue. 4918 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, that is al-Jazari, from Ata', from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was approached by a man who had made tadbir of a slave boy of his, then needed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Charity is only from surplus wealth; begin with those you support."
"5799","4919 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata', from Jabir, that a man freed his slave after his death (dumur), then his master needed, so he ordered him sold; he was sold for eight hundred dirhams. He said: Spend it on your dependents, for charity is only from surplus wealth; begin with those you support. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his permitting this slave's mudabbar to sell him. This hadith is narrated from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah from other than these two routes."
"5800","4920 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Ata', from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that a man of the Ansar freed a slave boy of his after his death (dumur), then needed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy him from me? Nu'aym ibn Abd Allah bought him for eight hundred dirhams, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed it to him."
"5801","4921 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Amr, from Hassan ibn Ibrahim, from Ibrahim al-Sa'igh, from Ata: Jabir ibn Abd Allah reported that a man at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) owned a slave and freed him in that manner; it reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Who will buy him from me? So he sold him and gave the price to his owner."
"5802","4922 - As Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti, from Shuayb ibn Ishaq, from al-Awzai, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah: Jabir ibn Abd Allah said a man owned a slave and granted him freedom after his death while owning little else, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold the slave, gave him the price and said: You need its price more, and Allah is Rich. Abu Jafar said: this shows the Prophet himself sold that mudabbar, possibly because of the owner's condition that restricted owners with little wealth from grants like tadbir, and Mujahid also narrated it from Jabir."
"5803","4923 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: a man of Banu Udhrah in Madinah freed his Coptic slave called Abu al-Mudhakkir contingent on his own death, then mentioned his need to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered him to sell him; he sold him for eight hundred dirhams to Nuaym al-Nahham."
"5804","4924 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn Adi, from al-Muharibi, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: a man of Banu Udhrah owned a slave and freed him contingent on his death while in need, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is in need let him begin with himself, then ordered him and he sold him to Nuaym ibn Abd Allah for eight hundred dirhams."
"5805","4925 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: an Ansari man called Abu Mudhakkar freed a slave of his contingent on death while owning nothing else, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for him, sold him to Nuaym ibn Abd Allah al-Nahham for eight hundred dirhams, returned the price to him and said: Only one with surplus frees; otherwise he falls back on himself."
"5806","4926 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from al-Husayn ibn Muhammad, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir likewise, except he said: Jabir said: a Coptic slave called Yaqub who died the previous year. Abu Jafar said: this shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself sold that slave, possibly for the reason mentioned under Atas narration from Jabir. Then we looked for others narrating it from Jabir, and found Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir also narrated it from Jabir."
"5807","4927 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from al-Muqaddami, from Said ibn Salamah - Abu Jafar said he is Ibn Abi al-Husam - from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: a man freed a slave of his while owning nothing else, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned him to slavery, sold him and gave him the price. Then we looked: did anyone else narrate it from Jabir?"
"5808","4928-4929 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Umar, from Sufyan; and as Ahmad narrated to us, from Musaddad, from Hammad ibn Zayd, both from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: a man freed a slave of his contingent on his death while owning nothing else, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy him from me? Nuaym ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Nahham bought him, a Coptic slave who died the previous year, for eight hundred dirhams. This matches the previous hadiths and bears the same possible meaning. Then we looked for others narrating it from Jabir."
"5809","4930 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Abu Hudhayfah, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir: an Ansari man called Abu Fatimah freed a slave contingent on his death; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked: Does he have any other wealth? They said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will buy him from me? Nuaym ibn al-Nahham, the in-law of Umar ibn al-Khattab, bought him for eight hundred dirhams, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spend it on yourself, then if surplus on your family, then on relatives, then here and there, right and left."
"5810","4931 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir: an Ansari man freed a slave contingent on his death - Amr thought Zuhayr said he was called Abu Madhkur - owning nothing else; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked: Did you free your slave contingent on your death? He said: Yes. He said: Who will buy him from me? Al-Nahham bought him for eight hundred dirhams and he gave it to him, saying: Spend on yourself, then if anything remains on your family, then on kin, then like this and like this."
"5811","4932 - As Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, from Ibn Lahiah and al-Layth, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, then he mentioned the like of it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5812","4933 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, from Azrah ibn Thabit, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold a mudabbar for eight hundred dirhams, gave the price to his master and said: When one of you is poor let him begin with himself. Abu Jafar said: in the narrations of Sufyan, Zuhayr, al-Layth and Ibn Lahiah the Prophet uncovered the owners condition, asking whether he had other wealth, and sold him only upon learning he had none, showing the ruling differs when he has other wealth; and Atas view was likewise."
"5813","As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, from Asbat ibn Muhammad, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, about a man who freed a slave girl contingent on death: may he have intercourse with her? He said: Yes. May he sell her? He said: No, unless he needs her price. The Shaykh said: whoever permits selling her without need for its price, this hadith is proof against him. And it is narrated from Ata, from Jabir, that what was sold of that mudabbar was only his service, not his person."
"5814","4934 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Tarif al-Kufi, from Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the sale of the mudabbars service. So what he ordered sold was his service, not his person. An objector said: can a hiring be called a sale? Our answer: this is permissible, calling hire sale, as narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5815","4935 - As Yazid narrated to us, from Abu Asim, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling bare land for ploughing, where a man sells his land; the Messenger forbade that."
"5816","4936 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, from Sulaym ibn Hayyan, from Said ibn Mina, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has surplus water or surplus land, let him cultivate it and not sell it. Sulaym said: I asked him: meaning rental? He said: Yes. So here hire is called sale, just as the sale of the mudabbars service may be hire called sale. And we found Jabir did not take this hadith from the Messenger but from another man, unknown whether a Companion or not, which blocks using it as proof."
"5817","4937 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Muhammad ibn Jafar Ghundar, from Shubah, from Amr: I heard Jabir, from a man of his people, that he freed a slave of his contingent on death, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for him and sold him. Then we found this story also narrated as occurring after the masters death."
"5818","4938 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani, from Sharik, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ata and Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir: a man granted a slave tadbir then died in debt, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold him for the debt."
"5819","4939 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from Abu Nuaym, from Sharik, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"5820","4940 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Khalaf ibn Hisham, from Sharik, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: a relative by marriage of Umar ibn al-Khattab died in debt owning a mudabbar, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sold him for the debt. Thus this sale was after the masters death, for debt; Malik and other Medinans say the mudabbar is sold after the masters death for his debt but the master may not sell him in life. If the hadith is as in Shariks narration, it does not permit selling the mudabbar in the masters life. Moreover there is severe inconsistency here, greater than that objected to in the hadith of Barwa, so those who forbid selling the mudabbar in the masters life have wider grounds. And we found from Jabir, the narrator, what shows his view was that the mudabbar is not sold."
"5821","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Hammad, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abi al-Zubayr: he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say about children of a mudabbara, when her master dies: he sees them only as free, her child from her as a limb of her. So Jabir shows tadbir acts on the person in the masters life, unlike a bequest of emancipation, supported by the Prophets saying charity is only from surplus wealth. And we found from Uthman ibn Affan and Abd Allah ibn Umar what bars selling the mudabbar."
"5822","As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn al-Layth, from al-Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abi al-Nadr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yaqub, client of al-Huraqah of Juhaynah: the master of my grandmother married her to a slave of his, then freed her contingent on death; she bore children before and after the tadbir, then her master died, and she disputed to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), who ruled: children born before tadbir are slaves, children born after tadbir are freed with her emancipation."
"5823","As Fahd narrated to us, from Abu Nuaym, from Sufyan, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: the child of the mudabbara has her status."
"5824","As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Nuaym, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: the woman freed contingent on death, her child has her status, freed with her freedom and enslaved with her slavery. This from Uthman and Ibn Umar matches Jabirs view. The jurists of the cities holding the bar on selling the mudabbara include Abu Hanifah, Ibn Abi Layla, al-Thawri, and the Hijazi imams like Malik and his companions."
"5825","780 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his imposing the zina hadd on the one confessing it, while the woman denied it. 4941 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Asad ibn Musa, from Muslim ibn Khalid, from Abu Hazim: Sahl ibn Sad, Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reported that a man of Aslam came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said he committed zina with a named woman; the Prophet sent for the woman, asked her about what he said, she denied, so he punished him and left her. Al-Rabi and Nasr narrated it without anyone between Muslim ibn Khalid and Abu Hazim."
"5826","4942 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Muslim ibn Khalid, from Abbad ibn Ishaq, from Abi Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad: a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: So-and-so committed zina with me; he sent for him, asked him, he denied, so he stoned the woman. Ibn Abi Dawud inserts Abbad ibn Ishaq between Muslim and Abu Hazim. This hadith imposes the zina hadd on the confessor, man or woman. Scholars differ: some say the confessor is punished as a zani while the denier bears nothing, among them Abu Yusuf. Others say the confessor is not punished when the denier can claim qadhf against him, for he cannot bear both hadds together: if truthful he is a zani due hadd for zina with no qadhf hadd, if lying he is a qadif due hadd for qadhf with no zina hadd; among them Abu Hanifah. His opponents cited this hadith against him, so we examined it."
"5827","4943 - As Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, from Abi al-Walid al-Tayalisi, from Abu Awanah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Maiz ibn Malik: Is what reached me about you true? He asked: What reached you about me? He said: That you went to a slave girl of such family; so he confessed against himself four times, and he ordered him stoned."
"5828","4944 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Abi al-Walid al-Tayalisi, from Aban ibn Yazid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Yazid ibn Nuaym ibn Hazzal - and Hazzal had urged Maiz to seek stoning -: his family had a slave girl herding sheep, Maiz fell upon her, Hazzal seized him, schemed and said: Go to the Messenger of Allah and tell him what you did, perhaps Quran will come down about you; so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him, and when the stones hurt him he ran, a man met him with a camels jawbone, struck and felled him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hazzal, had you covered him with your garment it would be better for you. Abu Jafar said: these two hadiths show the confessor before the Prophet was the man, as in al-Rabi and Nasrs hadith, not the woman as in Ibn Abi Dawuds hadith, and he was of Aslam, Maiz ibn Malik, without dispute. They also show the woman he accused was a slave girl with no hadd due to her for his accusing her, and so Abu Hanifah says: when the accused woman is a slave girl who denies, the confessor is punished for zina; he is spared only when she is free and due qadhf hadd from him, making him a liar whose testimony lapses."
"5829","781 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about killing: is there quasi-intentional killing as the Kufans say, or no quasi-intentional as the Hijazis say. 4945 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi, from Hushaym, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah ibn Jawshan, from Uqbah ibn Aws al-Sadusi, from a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed on the conquest of Makkah: intentional-looking accidental killing with whip, stick and stone carries an aggravated blood-money of one hundred camels, forty of them pregnant with their young. This shows no qisas in it. People differ: one group says killing is two kinds, accidental and intentional, with no third - the Hijazi view. Another says three: intentional with qisas, accidental with money on the clan, and quasi-intentional with this mentioned money; the Kufans differ over killing with a heavy lethal stone, some saying quasi-intentional with aggravated money and no qisas, among them Abu Hanifah, others saying qisas by sword, holding the stone in this hadith to be one that does not kill."
"5830","4946 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi, from Shubah, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the victim of accidental quasi-intentional killing with whip or stick, one hundred camels, forty pregnant with their young. Ayyub did not mention Uqbah ibn Aws in this hadith; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated it from Ayyub differing from Shubah."
"5831","4947 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ibrahim, from Yunus ibn Muhammad, from Hammad, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed on the conquest, without mentioning any other link in its chain. Then we sought who the Companion narrator in Khalids narration was."
"5832","4948 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi, from Hammad, from Khalid, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah, from Uqbah ibn Aws, from Abd Allah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the victim of accidental quasi-intentional killing with whip and stick, one hundred camels, forty pregnant with their young. Then we looked: did anyone besides Hushaym narrate it from Khalid?"
"5833","4949 - As Ahmad narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Masud, from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah, from Yaqub ibn Aws - not Uqbah - from a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the hadith."
"5834","4950 - As Ahmad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Bazi, from Yazid ibn Zuray, from Khalid, from al-Qasim ibn Rabiah, from Yaqub ibn Aws: a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned it. Bishr and Yazid did not mention the stone, only whip and stick. What Abu Yusuf and Muhammad held was preferable to us over Abu Hanifahs by analogy: killing intentionally with a sword entails qisas with the sin of killing, and killing with a heavy lethal stone bears the sin of killing, while killing with whip and stick that do not ordinarily kill bears no sin of killing; so what carries the sin of killing carries qisas, what does not carries aggravated money. The Kufans differ over the aggravated money: Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf said one hundred camels, twenty-five bint makhad, twenty-five bint labun, twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadhaah; Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said thirty hiqqah, thirty jadhaah, forty pregnant, and the latter was preferable to us for agreeing with the Prophetic hadith."
"5835","4951-4952 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi; and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari and Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi, both together from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik: his aunt al-Rubayyi slapped a slave girl and broke her front tooth; they sought pardon and refused, sought compensation and refused, insisting only on qisas; they disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered qisas, so Anas ibn al-Nadr said: Will al-Rubayyis tooth be broken? No, by Him who sent you with truth, it shall not be broken. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, the Book of Allah is qisas; then the people agreed and pardoned, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among Allahs servants are those who, if they swear upon Allah, He fulfills them. A slap that in a life would carry no qisas, the Messenger imposed qisas for below a life. So these two hadiths support the Kufans: a life may be intentional with qisas, accidental with accidental money, or quasi-intentional with quasi-intentional money, while below a life there is only accidental and intentional with no quasi-intentional."
"5836","782 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his ordering Ali ibn Abi Talib to kill the Copt who visited Mariyah, mother of Ibrahim son of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4953 - As Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah narrated to us, from Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi, from Ahmad ibn Dawud, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi al-Kufi, from Yunus ibn Bukayr, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, from his father, from his grandfather Ali ibn Abi Talib: people maligned Mariyah over a Copt visiting her, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told me: Go, if you find him with her kill him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I be in your command like a heated skewer proceeding without restraint, or does the witness see what the absent does not? He said: The witness sees what the absent does not. So I girt my sword, went, found him leaving her with a water jar on his neck; when he saw me draw my sword he dropped the jar, fled, climbed a palm, fell on his back halfway, his garment uncovered him, and I saw he was castrated, smooth, with nothing of what Allah created for men; so I sheathed my sword and said: Stay. He said: Good; I am a Nabatean man, she is a Nabatean woman, wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I gather firewood and fetch sweet water for her. Then I returned and told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who approved leaving him. Abu Jafar explained: the Prophets command was based on outward suspicion permitting it, then the inquiry revealed innocence, showing the witness judges by what he sees and the absent defers, and showing vindication of Mariyah."
"5837","783 - Chapter refuting what Abu Bahriyah narrated from Umar about Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died angry with him. 4954 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Ahmad ibn Shabib ibn Said, from his father, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab: Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan reported from Abu Bahriyah that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to a gathering with Uthman, Ali, Talhah, al-Zubayr and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with them), and said to them: Each of you aspires to rule after me; they were silent; he repeated; al-Zubayr said: Yes, deeming himself fit. He said: Shall I tell you about yourselves? Al-Zubayr said: Tell us though we were silent. He said: As for you Zubayr, you are a believer when pleased, a disbeliever when angry, one day a devil and one day a man; if your devil-day comes, who will be caliph? As for you Talhah, by Allah the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died angry with you. As for you Ali, you are firm and jesting. As for you Abd al-Rahman, by Allah you are worthy of the good Allah gave you, and among you is a man whose faith if divided over an army would encompass them. Al-Zubaydi narrated this from al-Zuhri inserting Amr ibn al-Harith al-Fahmi between al-Zuhri and Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan."
"5838","4955 - As Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiyah al-Utbi narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Zubaydi, from Amr ibn al-Harith al-Himyari al-Himsi, from Abd Allah ibn Salim al-Zubaydi, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn al-Harith al-Fahmi, scribe of Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan reported from Abu Bahriyah al-Kindi: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came upon a gathering with Uthman ibn Affan and Ali ibn Abi Talib, then he mentioned this hadith, adding after would-encompass-them, meaning Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him). This weighed heavily on our hearts about Talhah given his eminence, companionship until the Prophets death in finest companionship, and inclusion in Allahs verse: Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged to you under the tree. How could the Messenger be angry with one Allah declared pleased with? That is impossible to sound minds. Moreover Abu Bahriyah did not mention presence with Umar nor hearing it from him; even had he claimed hearing, he is unknown, not of the trusted scholars taken from, let alone without claimed hearing. Then we looked for what else is narrated from Umar about Talhah."
"5839","As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, from Said ibn Dawud al-Zanbari, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar: Abd Allah ibn Umar said: the group entered upon Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) before his affliction - Uthman, Ali, Abd al-Rahman, al-Zubayr and Sad (may Allah be pleased with them) - and he said: I have considered your affair of the people; I find no discord among people unless from you, and if discord it is among you; the affair is to six: Abd al-Rahman, Uthman, Ali, Sad, al-Zubayr and Talhah - Talhah being absent in al-Sarah over properties of his - then your people will appoint one of you three, Uthman, Ali and Abd al-Rahman; if you Abd al-Rahman hold anything of peoples affair, do not impose Banu Abi father on peoples necks; if you Uthman, do not impose Banu Abi Muayt; if you Ali, do not impose Banu Hashim."
"5840","As Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Salih al-Makhzumi al-Madani narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Uwaysi, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Umar likewise. This hadith has Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) naming Talhah among those to whom he assigned the caliphate; impossible had the Messenger died angry with him. Abd Allah ibn Umar is just, firm, trusted in narration, unlike Abu Bahriyah. Also Aslam, client of Umar, narrated from Umar about Talhah contradicting Abu Bahriyah."
"5841","4956 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Shuja ibn Ashras, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed: I saw in sleep a red cock pecking me at my waist three pecks; I asked Asma bint Umays to interpret; she said: A non-Arab man will kill you. I reckon my death sudden, and I call you to witness: if I perish without appointing, the affair is to those with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died pleased: Uthman, Ali, Talhah, al-Zubayr, Sad and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf. Among them also Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi."
"5842","4957 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sahl ibn Bakkar, from Abu Awanah, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn Maymun: when Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed - and I was present - he was asked: Appoint a successor. He said: I find none worthier than those with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died pleased; he named Ali, Uthman, Talhah, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Sad (may Allah be pleased with them). Among them also Madan ibn Abi Talhah al-Yamuri."
"5843","4958 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan ibn Abi Talhah al-Yamuri: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stood, praised Allah, mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), then said: People, I saw in a dream as if a red cock pecked me once or twice - Said doubted - and I see it only as my term approaching; some order me to appoint, but Allah would not waste His religion, His caliphate, nor what He sent His Prophet with; if I am hastened, consultation is among these six with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died pleased - whichever you pledge, hear and obey: Ali, Uthman, Talhah, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Sad ibn Malik Abi Waqqas. I know some will impugn this affair, though I fought them with this hand for Islam; if they do, they are Allahs enemies, disbelieving, astray."
"5844","4959 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Muadh ibn Faddalah, from Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan ibn Abi Talhah likewise, except he did not name the six but said: if I am hastened, the caliphate is among these six with whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died pleased. So Aslam client of Umar, Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi and Madan ibn Abi Talhah al-Yamuri - imams in knowledge, just, trusted, accepted - narrate from Umar, with hearing and witnessing, the opposite of Abu Bahriyahs narration. How may a man of reason or religion cling to the narration of an unknown non-scholar with no known meeting with Umar, against these? Even about one below Talhah this would be rejected, let alone Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) with his eminence, rank, place in Allahs religion, the proof of his standing with the Messenger, and the testimony of these just imams that Umar deemed him fit for caliphate and that the Messenger died pleased with him."
"5845","784 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his leaving prayer over one who killed himself. 4960 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Mansur, from Israil, Sharik and Zuhayr, from Simak, from Jabir ibn Samurah: a man slaughtered himself with a broad arrowhead, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray over him."
"5846","4961 - As Ismail ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us, from Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus, from Zuhayr, from Simak, from Jabir ibn Samurah: a man fell ill and there was wailing over him; his neighbour came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: He has died. He said: How do you know? He said: I saw him. He said: He has not died. He returned, there was wailing, he came saying he died; he said: He has not died. He returned, wailed, his wife said: Go tell the Messenger; the man said: O Allah curse him, then went, saw him slaughtered with blades, told the Messenger he died; he asked: How do you know? He said: I saw him slaughter himself with his blades. He asked: Did you see him? He said: Yes. He said: Then I shall not pray over him. This shows the Prophet left prayer over him for killing himself. Scholars differ: one group says one like him is prayed over, among them Ibrahim al-Nakhai, Abu Hanifah and his companions. Another says he is not prayed over, citing this hadith. We found the leaving was from the Messenger alone, not all people; possibly he left it for his blameworthy deed, as of his law not to pray over debtors and others, while people still pray over them; so no proof against praying over him."
"5847","785 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likening the five prayers, in Allahs erasing sins of performers through them, to washing with water cleansing bodily dirt. 4962 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri, from Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab, from his uncle Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Ubayd Allah al-Zuhri, from Salih ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah: Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas told him he heard Aban ibn Uthman say: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: What if a flowing river stood at one of your courtyards, bathing in it five times daily, what dirt would remain? He said: Nothing. He said: Prayers remove sins as water removes dirt."
"5848","4963 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The parable of the prescribed prayers is like a flowing river at one of your doors, bathing in it five times daily."
"5849","4964 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Hammad, from Abu Awanah, from Sulayman - that is al-Amash - then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"5850","4965 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah: he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: What if a river stood at one of your doors, bathing in it five times daily, would any dirt remain? They said: None. He said: That is the parable of the five prayers; Allah erases sins through them."
"5851","4966 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf; and as Yunus narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr, both from al-Layth, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"5852","4967 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd, from al-Amash, from Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayrah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The parable of these five prayers is like a flowing river at one of your doors, bathing in it five times daily, what dirt remains? These reports show Allah erases forgivable sins of performers through the five prayers, likened to water washing dirt five times daily, proving likening things to their likes is applied, and the rulings for destroyed property by likes or value."
"5853","786 - Chapter on the problem of the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer about bit when asked. 4968 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Wahb, that Malik ibn Anas told him. 4969 - And as Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, from Yunus ibn Yazid, both from Ibn Shihab, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about bit and said: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful."
"5854","4970 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Isa ibn al-Tabba, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about bit, then he mentioned the like."
"5855","4971 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Abi Salamah, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful. Abu Jafar said: this answer bears two possibilities: that a drink whose much intoxicates is unlawful only when intoxicating, or that when its much intoxicates its little and much are unlawful in themselves. So we looked: did anyone besides Aishah narrate his answer to this question?"
"5856","4972 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Shubah, from Said ibn Abi Burdah: I heard my father report from Abu Musa that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Muadh and Abu Musa to Yemen, Abu Musa told him: a drink is made in our land from honey called bit, and from barley called mizr; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful. This answer bears the same possibilities as Aishahs hadith. So we looked: is there any other narration of his answer besides these two?"
"5857","4973 - As Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Muhammad, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi Burdah, from his father: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen and I said: O Prophet of Allah, there is a drink made of barley and wheat called mizr and bit; what do we drink? He said: Drink, but do not drink an intoxicant - or he said: do not become intoxicated."
"5858","4974 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Raja al-Ghudani, from Israil ibn Yunus, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi Burdah, from his father: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Muadh to Yemen, and I said: you sent us to a land whose people drink much; he said: Drink both, but do not drink an intoxicant."
"5859","4975 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, from Asad ibn Musa, from al-Fudayl ibn Marzuq, from Abi Ishaq, from Abi Burdah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Said ibn Abi Burdahs narration from his father from Abu Musa bears the same possibilities as Aishahs hadith, but Abi Ishaqs narration via Sharik, Israil and al-Fudayl clarifies it, with his permitting drink and forbidding every intoxicant. So we understood: the intoxicant meant is what intoxicates of those drinks, not what does not intoxicate. Then we looked: did this hadith from Abu Burdah gain any addition from others besides Abi Ishaq and his son Said, returning its meaning to Abi Ishaqs hadith?"
"5860","4976 - As Fahd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad ibn Shaddad, from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Said ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa al-Ashari: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Muadh ibn Jabal to Yemen and I said: O Messenger of Allah, give us ruling on two drinks we made in Yemen: bit from honey steeped until strong, and mizr from barley and millet steeped until strong. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), given comprehensive speech, said: Unlawful is every intoxicant that intoxicates away from prayer. This addition returns its meaning to Abi Ishaqs hadith, clarifying Shubahs narration that the unlawful intoxicant is what intoxicates away from prayer, not what does not; permitting drinking what does not intoxicate though its much intoxicates. Then we looked: did anyone besides Abi Ishaq and Said narrate it from Abu Burdah?"
"5861","4977 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, from Sayyar Abi al-Hakam, from Abi Burdah, from Abu Musa: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Yemenis make a drink from honey and mizr from millet and barley; he said: I forbid you every intoxicant."
"5862","4978 - As Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan al-Basri narrated to us, from Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, from al-Huraysh ibn Sulaym al-Kufi, from Talhah al-Iyami, from Abi Burdah, from Abu Musa: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful. These two hadiths forbid every intoxicant; reconciling them with Zayd ibn Abi Unaysahs hadith - every intoxicant that intoxicates away from prayer - restricts it to what intoxicates away from prayer, so all reports agree without contradiction. Then we looked: did anyone besides his son Abu Burdah narrate it from Abu Musa?"
"5863","4979 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Abbas, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from al-Ajlah ibn Abd Allah, from Abi Bakr ibn Abi Musa al-Ashari, from his father: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen and I asked: O Messenger of Allah, there are drinks there, what do I drink and leave? He asked: What are they? I said: Bit and mizr. He asked: What is bit? I said: Bit from honey, mizr from millet, strong until intoxicating. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not drink an intoxicant, for I have forbidden every intoxicant."
"5864","4980 - As Fahd narrated to us, from Nuaym ibn Hammad, from Ibn al-Mubarak, then he mentioned the like with its chain. Reconciling this with the preceding hadiths: the intoxicant meant is what intoxicates away from prayer, as with Aishahs hadith in this chapter; permitting drinking what does not intoxicate of these drinks and forbidding what intoxicates thereof."
"5865","787 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from Ibn Abbas about what is unlawful of every drink, is it intoxication or the intoxicant? As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn, from Misar ibn Kidam, from Abi Awn al-Thaqafi, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, from Ibn Abbas: wine is unlawful in itself, and intoxication from every drink."
"5866","As Fahd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Misar, from Abi Awn, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad: Ibn Abbas said: wine is unlawful in itself, its little and much, and intoxication from every drink."
"5867","As Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Kindi narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Waki, from Misar, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"5868","As Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Aslami narrated to us, from Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, from Abi Awn, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn Abbas the like."
"5869","As Ahmad ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Hanifah, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"5870","As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from al-Firyabi, from Sufyan, from Abi Awn, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like."
"5871","As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Ali, from Surayj ibn Yunus, from Hushaym, from Ibn Shubrumah, from a trustworthy man, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn Abbas, then the like. Abu Jafar said: the narrators agree the trustworthy man Ibn Shubrumah meant is Abu Awn al-Thaqafi. So this hadith from Abi Awn via Misar, Abu Hanifah, Ibn Shubrumah and al-Thawri returns to mentioning the intoxicant of every drink; and Shubah narrated it from Misar with this chain saying: and the intoxicant of every drink."
"5872","4981 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam; and as al-Husayn ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Misar, from Abi Awn, from Abd Allah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn Abbas: wine is unlawful in itself, its little and much, and the intoxicant of every drink - Ahmad ibn Shuayb said Ahmad ibn Abd Allah did not mention its little and much. What Waki, Abu Nuaym and Jarir narrated from Misar is preferable to Shubahs differing narration, for three are more retentive than one, others narrated it from Abi Awn as those three did, and Shubah, despite eminence, narrated from memory without jurisprudence, altering wording by supposed meaning, as he did in Miqdam ibn Madikaribs hadith on the maternal uncles inheritance and similar cases mentioned earlier in this book."
"5873","4982 - As Ibn Abi Imran and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Jad, from Shubah, from Ismail ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade saffron. Ibn Abi Imran added: Ali said: then I met Ismail and told him Shubah narrated it from him thus, and he said: I did not narrate it so; I narrated that the Prophet forbade a man perfuming with saffron. Ibn Abi Imran said: they differ, for forbidding a man to use saffron covers only men, while forbidding saffron covers men and women. Abu Jafar said: all other companions of Abd al-Aziz narrated it as forbidding a man to use saffron. Then we considered Ibn Abbass hadith: on Shubahs wording it bears what we mentioned in the first chapter, and on the others wording likewise, as mentioned in the first chapter."
"5874","788 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Banu Hisham ibn al-Mughirah sought his permission to marry their daughter to Ali ibn Abi Talib, and what he did. 4983-4985 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi, via Abd Allah ibn Wahb and Shuayb ibn al-Layth, and as Ibn Abi Dawud via Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus, all from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Banu Hisham ibn al-Mughirah sought permission to marry their daughter to Ali ibn Abi Talib; I do not permit, I do not permit, I do not permit, unless Ibn Abi Talib divorces my daughter and marries theirs, for she is part of me; what troubles her troubles me, what harms her harms me. Abu Jafar said: possibly this was for a proposal Ali had made to them, or that they proposed Ali to themselves without his prior proposal. So we looked for other narrations clarifying the true meaning."
"5875","4986 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from his father: I heard al-Numan ibn Rashid report from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah: Ali proposed the daughter of Abu Jahl; Fatimah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: your people say you do not anger for your daughters, and Ali has proposed Abu Jahls daughter; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fatimah is part of me, and I dislike what distresses her; and he mentioned Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi praising him, saying: the daughter of Allahs Prophet and the daughter of Allahs enemy are not joined."
"5876","4987 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khali, from Bishr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah: Ali ibn Abi Talib proposed Abu Jahls daughter while Fatimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with him; when Fatimah heard she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: your people say you do not anger for your daughters, and here Ali is marrying Abu Jahls daughter. Al-Miswar said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and, after the testimony, said: I married Abu al-As and he told me truthfully; Fatimah daughter of Muhammad is part of me, and I only fear they will tempt her; by Allah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the daughter of Allahs enemy shall never join; so Ali dropped the proposal."
"5877","4988 - As Ahmad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri, from his uncle, from his father, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Halhalah al-Duali, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali ibn Husayn: when they returned from Yazid ibn Muawiyah after Husayn ibn Alis killing, al-Miswar ibn Makhramah met him asking any need and whether to give him the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sword lest people overpower him for it; then he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib proposed Abu Jahls daughter over Fatimah, and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) address the people - I was then near puberty - saying: Fatimah is from me, I fear she will be tempted in her religion; then he mentioned a son-in-law of Banu Abd Shams praising his companionship: he told me truthfully and kept his promise; I do not forbid lawful nor permit unlawful, but by Allah the daughter of the Messenger of Allah and the daughter of Allahs enemy shall never join in one place."
"5878","4989 - As Abd al-Rahman ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ala ibn Zibriq al-Zubaydi, from Amr ibn al-Harith al-Himyari, from Abd Allah ibn Salim, from al-Zubaydi, from Muhammad ibn Muslim: Ali ibn Husayn reported that when they returned from al-Taff as captives to Yazid ibn Muawiyah, then came to Madinah, al-Miswar ibn Makhramah al-Zuhri came to him, then the like with its chain, except without: I do not forbid lawful nor permit unlawful. These reports show Ali had proposed that woman; possibly he did so not seeing how the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take it, and when he learned he abandoned it, choosing what pleased the Messenger, for which he is praised. An objector said: the Prophet praised Abu al-As for leaving what Ali did with his daughter; does that rank Abu al-As above Ali in his heart? Our answer: what Abu al-As left differed from Alis case, for Abu al-As remained with the Prophets daughter upon Islam while his people opposed, then left Makkah to Madinah abandoning kin and wealth for her, unlike one proposing another wife alongside Fatimah; moreover the Prophets displeasure was only lest Fatimah be tempted in religion by joining with Allahs enemys daughter, not hatred of lawful marriage; and Alis prompt abandonment shows his merit."
"5879","789 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what suffices disputants over the believing slave, whether one who affirms faith but neither fasts nor prays suffices. Abu Jafar said: reported from al-Hasan al-Basri and Ibrahim al-Nakhai. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Abu Dawud, from Abi Hurrah: I heard al-Hasan say: a believing slave suffices only one who fasts and prays; a non-believing slave may be a child. And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih, from Abu Shihab, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim: in expiation for killing only a slave who fasted and prayed is valid, while for zihar and oaths one who never fasted nor prayed suffices. Abu Jafar said: later jurists said one affirming faith suffices for the believing slave, even without fasting or prayer, or one deemed believer by parents faith; the judge between them is what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5880","4990 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abu Dawud; and as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, from Asad, both from al-Masudi, from Awn ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Abu Hurayrah: a man brought the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a non-Arab slave girl who could not speak clearly, saying: I owe a believing slave; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her: Where is Allah? She pointed to heaven. He asked: Who am I? She pointed to heaven. The Messenger said: Free her - al-Masudi once said: free her, for she is a believer - such is Bakkars wording. Al-Rabis wording: he asked her: Who am I? She pointed to him and to heaven, meaning: you are Allahs Messenger. He said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"5881","4991 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abi Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, my mother vowed to free a believing slave, and here is a black slave girl; the Messenger of Allah asked her: Where is Allah? She said: In heaven. He asked: Who am I? She said: You are Allahs Messenger. He said: Free her, for she is a believer."
"5882","4992 - As Yunus narrated to us, that Ibn Wahb told him Malik told him; and as al-Muzani narrated to us, from al-Shafii, from Malik, from Hilal ibn Usamah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Umar ibn al-Hakam: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, a slave girl of mine herded my sheep; I came, a sheep was missing, I asked her, she said: a wolf ate it; I was angered, being a son of Adam, and slapped her face; I owe a slave - shall I free her? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her: Where is Allah? She said: In heaven. He asked: Who am I? She said: You are Allahs Messenger. He said: Free her. Al-Muzani said: al-Shafii said: Malik named this man Umar ibn al-Hakam, but he is Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam."
"5883","4993 - As Yunus told us, from Bishr ibn Bakr, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hilal ibn Abi Maymunah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami: I had a slave girl herding my sheep near Uhud and al-Jawwaniyah; I found a wolf had taken a sheep; being a son of Adam I anger as they anger, so I struck her; I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him, and he deemed it grave. I said: Shall I free her? He said: Call her to me. I called her. He asked: Where is Allah? She said: In heaven. He asked: Who am I? She said: You are Allahs Messenger. He said: She is a believer, so free her."
"5884","4994 - As Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, from al-Awzai, from Yahya, from Hilal ibn Abi Umayyah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. These narrations prove the later of the two views mentioned in this chapter, that affirming faith suffices without fasting or prayer."
"5885","790 - Chapter on the problem of rulings between disputants over selling wala or gifting it, with what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: Maymunah gifted the wala of Sulayman ibn Yasar to Ibn Abbas. An objector said: Maymunah and Ibn Abbas permitted gifting wala; against whom did you oppose them? Our answer: we opposed them for what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contradicting them, to which had it been cited to them they would have returned."
"5886","4995 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abu Dawud, from Shubah and Malik ibn Anas, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling wala and gifting it. Shubah said: I asked him: Did you hear it from Ibn Umar? He said: Yes; I asked his son and his son asked him about it."
"5887","4996 - As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri, from Warqa, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah and Shubah, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"5888","4997 - As Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"5889","4998 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah, from Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"5890","4999 - As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah, from Sufyan and Ibn Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"5891","5000 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Musaddad, from Yahya ibn Said, from Sufyan, Shubah and Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar: I heard Ibn Umar say, then he mentioned the like from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5892","5001 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Ismail ibn Jafar, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"5893","5002 - As Fahd narrated to us, from Abu Nuaym, from Hasan ibn Salih: I heard Abd Allah ibn Dinar say: I heard Ibn Umar say, then he mentioned the like from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5894","5003 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, from Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus al-Kufi: I heard Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like. Abu Jafar said: this sunnah is narrated from the Messenger only through this channel, with nothing contradicting it, so it must be followed; the jurists agree against what is narrated from Ibn Abbas and Maymunah. Analogy also requires it, for wala owed by manumission is like lineage to a child: as one may not gift a childs lineage, so one may not gift a clients wala."
"5895","791 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what mosques are unfit for and what they are for. 5004 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamani, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These mosques are unfit for anything of this - meaning urine and filth; they are only for Allahs remembrance, prayer and Qurans recitation - or as the Messenger said. An objector said: you narrated that when he observed itikaf a tent was pitched for him and for his wives with him, and you mentioned that earlier in this book, which uses the mosque for other than stated; our answer: there is no contradiction, for itikaf occasions Allahs remembrance within the stated meaning, and residents need shelter from cold and heat, privacy for the Mothers of the Believers, and food and drink without which bodies cannot subsist, without cutting people off from prayer in the rest of the mosque."
"5896","5005 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani, from Ali ibn Abis al-Mulai, from Abi Fazarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abi Layla: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) observed itikaf in the last ten of Ramadan in a dome of palm leaves. The objector said: this occupies the mosque for other than built; and it contradicts Anass hadith. Our answer: itikaf is a cause of Allahs remembrance, entering Anass meaning; the residents need what shields them and sustains them, and the Mothers of the Believers need screening from men unlawful to look at them; nor did it cut people from the rest of the mosque."
"5897","5006 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, from Abd Allah ibn Numayr, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah: Sad ibn Muadh (may Allah be pleased with him) was wounded on the Trench day; a Qurayshi man called Hibban ibn al-Ariqah shot him in the forearm vein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pitched a tent for him in the mosque to visit him from near."
"5898","5007 - As Ahmad ibn Masud al-Khayyat al-Maqdisi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Isa ibn al-Tabba, from Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pitched a tent for Sad ibn Muadh in the mosque to visit him closely. The objector said: this adds beyond the itikaf case. Our answer: the Prophet may have meant for Sad increase with his Lord from prayers in his mosque - a prayer therein worth a thousand - completing for him what his injury would cut off, alongside nearness for visiting and observing him; agreeing with the first hadith."
"5899","792 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his leading the prophets the night he was taken to Bayt al-Maqdis, whether all prophets or some. 5008 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Shayban ibn Farrukh; and as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Minhal, each from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Abi Hamzah - Ibn Khuzaymah said: al-Awar - from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Alqamah, from Ibn Masud, on the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) riding al-Buraq to Bayt al-Maqdis: then we went to Bayt al-Maqdis, I tied the mount to the ring the prophets tie with, entered the mosque, and the prophets Allah named in His Book and those He did not name honoured me, and I prayed with them except these: Isa, Musa and Ibrahim. This hadith excepts those three; while Anas ibn Malik narrates he led all without exception."
"5900","5009 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Wahb, from Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hashim ibn Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas, from Anas ibn Malik: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Bayt al-Maqdis the night he was taken there, Adam and the prophets below him were raised for him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led them."
"5901","5010 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Minhal, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was brought al-Buraq, a white mount between donkey and mule, placing its hoof at its sights end; I rode until Bayt al-Maqdis, tied it to the prophets ring, entered and prayed, then left. In his first hadith his prayer there was leading the prophets; in his second only praying without mentioning leading; and Abu Hurayrah narrates what agrees with Anass first hadith."
"5902","5011 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Shuja ibn Ashras, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Majishun, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, on the Isra to Bayt al-Maqdis: I saw myself among prophets - Musa standing praying, a curly lean man as of Shanuah; Isa ibn Maryam standing praying, most resembling Urwah ibn Masud al-Thaqafi; Ibrahim standing praying, most resembling your companion, meaning himself - then prayer came and I led them; when finished one said: O Muhammad, this is Malik, keeper of Fire, greeting you; I turned and he greeted with peace. So Anass and Abu Hurayrahs hadiths affirm his leading all prophets that night, while Ibn Masuds excepts three; we looked for the source of the difference, for Ibn Masuds hadith as narrated has an addition we omitted that we must mention here."
"5903","5012 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Shayban ibn Farrukh; and as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Minhal, both from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Abi Hamzah, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Alqamah, from Ibn Masud: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken to Bayt al-Maqdis, he said: I came upon a man standing praying; I asked: Who is with you Jibril? He said: Your brother Muhammad; he welcomed and blessed, saying: Ask ease for your nation. I asked: Who is this? He said: Your brother Isa. Then we came upon another likewise: your brother Musa. Then we saw lamps and light; I asked, he said: This is the tree of your father Ibrahim; draw near; we drew near and he blessed and welcomed me; then we went to Bayt al-Maqdis. Abu Jafar said: herein is his meeting those three excepted prophets before Bayt al-Maqdis; possibly Ibn Masuds exception in the first hadith was for that, supposing they had not prayed with him since met before Bayt al-Maqdis; but meeting on the way does not bar their later joining at Bayt al-Maqdis."
"5904","5013 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Minhal, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Sulayman al-Taymi and Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I came upon Musa at the red dune, standing praying in his grave. Anas thus knew the Prophet passed Musa praying in his grave on the way, yet held he joined Bayt al-Maqdis and the Prophet led him there with the rest. And Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman denied the Prophet prayed that night in Bayt al-Maqdis."
"5905","5014 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Minhal, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was brought al-Buraq, a tall white mount placing its hoof at its sights end; he and Jibril did not leave its back until Bayt al-Maqdis; heavens gates opened and he saw Paradise and Fire. Hudhayfah said: He did not pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. I said: Rather he prayed. Hudhayfah asked: What is your name bald one? I know your face not your name. I said: Zirr ibn Hubaysh. He asked: How do you know he prayed there? I said: Allah says: Glory to Him who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque. He asked: Do you find he prayed? I said: No. He said: Had he prayed there you would pray there as in the Sacred Mosque. It was said: He tied the mount to the prophets ring. Hudhayfah said: Would he fear it leaving when Allah brought it to him? Abu Jafar said: what Ibn Masud, Anas and Abu Hurayrah affirm of his praying there is preferable to Hudhayfahs denial, for affirmation outweighs denial; Hudhayfahs claim that praying there would oblige the nation to go pray there is void, nor does Allahs subjugating a mount remove need to tie it, as we tie our subjugated mounts."
"5906","As Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, from Jarir ibn Hazim; and as Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, from his father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from al-Amash, from Marur ibn Suwayd al-Asadi: I attended the season with Commander of the Believers Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him); returning to Madinah he led us in dawn prayer, reciting: Did you not see how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant, and: For the protection of Quraysh; then he saw people going aside and asked: Where are they going? They said: To a mosque here where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed. He said: Those before you perished by such, tracing their prophets آثار and taking them as churches and synagogues; whoever is overtaken by prayer in any of these mosques where the Messenger prayed, let him pray therein, but let him not make for them."
"5907","As Fahd narrated to us, from Umar ibn Hafs, from his father, from al-Amash, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd the like. So Umars hadith shows the mosques where the Messenger prayed do not oblige the nation to visit or pray therein because he did; likewise his prayer at Bayt al-Maqdis in the hadiths of Ibn Masud, Anas and Abu Hurayrah does not oblige going there or praying therein; clearer, no mosque is greater after the Sacred Mosque than the Prophets mosque for reward, yet visiting it and praying therein is not written obligatory as for the Sacred Mosque. This shows Umars rank in knowledge above others. As for Hudhayfahs denial that the Messenger tied al-Buraq that night, the Messengers affirmed report outweighs Hudhayfahs denial; and not every subjugated thing obeys without effort, as Allah subjugated mounts yet we labour in riding them."
"5908","793 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from Said ibn Jubayr about where: And ask those We sent before you of Our messengers, came down, with what is narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 5015 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, from Musaddad and Sahl ibn Bakkar, from Abu Awanah, from Abi Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr: And ask those We sent before you of Our messengers - he said: he met the messengers the night he was taken. This shows this verse came down neither in Makkah nor Madinah, for the Messenger was taken from Makkah to a place he knew only by reaching it and gathering there with the prophets, whom he led as in the previous chapter, and they are those he was ordered to ask, for he led them nowhere else; proving Said ibn Jubayrs narration."
"5909","794 - Chapter on the problem of what is meant by Allahs saying: If you are in doubt about what We sent down to you. As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, from al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad al-Basri, from Umar ibn Yunus, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Abu Zumayl: a man told Ibn Abbas: something falls in my mind which I would rather fall from heaven than utter. Ibn Abbas asked: Of doubt you mean? He said: Yes. He said: Does anyone escape that? Allah said to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If you are in doubt about what We sent down to you. No Companion is known to interpret this verse besides this hadith from Ibn Abbas. As for Followers, as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Said ibn Mansur, from Hushaym, from Abi Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr and Mansur from al-Hasan: they said of this verse: he did not doubt, and we did not doubt."
"5910","As Ahmad ibn Ali ibn Musab Abu al-Abbas al-Baghdadi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Hashim ibn Mushkan, from Hushaym, from Abi Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr the like."
"5911","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Salim, from Hushaym, from Abi Bishr, from Said and Mansur from al-Hasan the like."
"5912","As Ahmad narrated to us, from Musaddad and Sahl ibn Bakkar, from Abu Awanah, from Abi Bishr, from Said ibn Jubayr the like. As for linguists, al-Kisai and al-Farra both said: His saying If you are in doubt is not reporting he is in doubt, like a man telling his son: if you are my son do so, without doubting he is his son. Better than that, others said the addressee apparently the Prophet but meant are others, the doubters, meaning: if you are in doubt from others about what We sent down; among them Abu Ubaydah Mamar ibn al-Muthanna, like Allahs: Until when you are in the ship - meaning Nuh - then He clarified with: and they sail with them with a fair wind, showing others than him and his nation meant. They said those asked in: then ask those who read the Book before you, are prior believers of the People of the Book like Abd Allah ibn Salam and his likes. It occurred to me the meant ones may be those he met at Bayt al-Maqdis of prophets sent before him, and we ask Allah for success."
"5913","5016 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn Buhlul al-Kufi, from Abd Allah ibn Idris, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Thawr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), on the two wives who backed one another against the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his giving his wives choice after: I sat saying: O Prophet of Allah, you are Allahs Prophet, His chosen, His choice of creation as I see - lying on a mat of palm fibre with a leather pillow stuffed with fibre under his head, so this hadith mentions - while Khosrow and Caesar are on gold couches and silk brocade; he sat saying: O Umar, do you doubt? I said: No, by Him who sent you with truth, I am certain of Allah about you, you are His Prophet and chosen; but I marvelled at what is withheld from you of the world and spread for them. He said: They are people whose good things are hastened in worldly life, while ours are deferred to the Hereafter."
"5914","5017 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, then the like with its chain, except he said: Are you in doubt O son of al-Khattab? Those are people whose good things are hastened in worldly life. I said: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for me. Since Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) denied doubt of himself by oath before the Messenger, with the Messenger not rebutting him, denial is stronger for the Messenger, and likewise for his likes among the Companions; so the doubters meant are others besides the Messenger, Umar and his companions - those doubting him whose Islam, if any, is not as the Companions, or who disbelieve and never entered his law; and we found no better interpretation of this verse than what we chose here."
"5915","794 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him), his arguing by his charity of Ruma well while barred from drinking it, his addition to the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mosque while barred from praying in it. 5018 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan, from al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman, from his father, from Abi Nadrah, from Abi Said client of Abi Usayd al-Ansari: word reached Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Egyptian delegation approached, so he went to meet them, then the hadith at length until his coming before people saying: I adjure you by Allah, do you know I bought Ruma from my wealth for such to fetch sweet water, making my rope as any Muslims rope? They said: O Allah yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know any barred from drinking it but me, so I break fast only on sea water? They were silent. Then one day he looked upon them saying: I adjure you by Allah, do you know I bought land from my wealth for such and added it to the mosque? They said: O Allah yes. He said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know any barred from praying in it but me? They were silent."
"5916","5019 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Said ibn Amir, from Yahya ibn Abi al-Hajjaj, from Abi Masud al-Jurayri, from Thumamah ibn Hazn al-Qushayri: I witnessed the house when Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) looked upon them saying: Bring me these two companions of yours who incited you against me; they were brought as two camels or two donkeys; Uthman looked upon them saying: I adjure you by Allah and Islam, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah with no sweet water but Ruma well, and said: Who will buy Ruma well, his bucket with Muslims buckets, for better for him in Paradise? So I bought it from my own wealth? While today you bar me from drinking it so I drink sea water. They said: O Allah yes. He said: I adjure you, do you know the mosque straitened its people, and the Messenger said: Who will buy such familys plot for better in Paradise? So I bought it from my wealth and added it to the mosque? While you bar me from praying two rakahs therein. They said: O Allah yes. He said: I adjure you, do you know I equipped the army of hardship from my wealth? They said: O Allah yes. He said: I adjure you, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on Thabir of Makkah with Abu Bakr, Umar and me, the mountain shook till stones fell to the foot, and he stamped saying: Be still Thabir, upon you only a Prophet, a truthful one and two martyrs? They said: O Allah yes. He said: Allah is Greatest - and likewise in the hadith of Abu Said client of Abi Usayd - then Uthman said: By Lord of the Kabah, I am guiltless of what they accuse; the people had pledged him upon that, then broke it, and Allah says of breakers what He says."
"5917","795 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding Umar ibn al-Khattab returning to his charity, whether by all means so unfit for him in any way, or specific means. 5020 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, from Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Muqri, from Ali ibn Mushir, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): I gave a horse in Allahs path - and when we gave in Allahs path we brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) giving it to him to place as Allah showed him - so I brought my horse to him and he gave it to a Companion; I found him selling it in the market and wanted to buy it, so I told the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Do not buy it, nor return to anything of your charity."
"5918","5021 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, from al-Shafii, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: Umar ibn al-Khattab gave a horse in Allahs path, found it sold, wanted to buy it, asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that; he said: Do not buy it nor return to your charity."
"5919","5022 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, from al-Shafii, from Sufyan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): he saw a horse sold in the market that he had given in charity, and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I buy it? The Messenger said: Do not buy it nor anything of its offspring."
"5920","5023 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, from al-Shafii, from Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid, from Yahya ibn Said, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: Umar gave a horse in charity in the Messengers time, found it sold, told the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Do not buy it, nor come near it. Abu Jafar said: these reports forbid Umar buying his charity, as returning to it; possibly disliked by all means of owning, or only by some means; so we examined."
"5921","5024 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, from al-Layth, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar: Ibn Umar reported Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) gave a horse in Allahs path, found it sold after, wanted to buy it, then asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Do not return to your charity. Hence Ibn Umar would not buy anything he gave in charity, nor inherit it except making it charity, showing he saw returning charity by inheritance disliked to retain, until returning it to charity. Then we looked: is there from the Messenger what rebuts this view?"
"5922","5025 - As Yunus narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather: a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, I gave my mother a garden, and she died leaving no heir but me; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your charity is due, and your garden returned to you. This permits the giver owning his charity by inheritance. Reconciling: retaking charity by buying and likes - acceptance as gift or charity or other ownership - is disliked; while Allahs returning it to his ownership by inheritance from the recipient, without his retaking, is not disliked. Also narrated from the Messenger forbidding others than Umar likewise."
"5923","5026 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abi Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abd Allah ibn Amir, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him): he gave a horse in Allahs path and it covered a horse or foal, and he wanted to buy it, but was forbidden it."
"5924","5027 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from Surayj ibn al-Numan, from Hammad, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abi Uthman al-Nahdi, from Ibn Abbas: al-Zubayr gave a horse in Allahs path and found a horse sold of its progeny - meaning its grand-offspring - and was forbidden to buy it. Abu Jafar said: Sulayman and Asim differed over the man Abu Uthman reported this from, as mentioned; and the like is narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd."
"5925","5028 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Abd Allah ibn Miqal, from Usamah or Zayd: he gave a horse in Allahs path and wanted to buy its foal, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him."
"5926","5029 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Layth ibn Dawud, from Shubah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Abd Allah ibn Miqal, from Usamah ibn Zayd or Zayd ibn Harithah the like. Layth added between al-Hakam and Abd Allah ibn Miqal Yahya ibn al-Jazzar beyond Wahb. So in al-Zubayr and Usamahs hadiths, what the given horse begets is disliked like the horse itself. Thus no contradiction in this chapter from the Messenger; each meaning has its face distinct from what the unlearned deem opposing."
"5927","796 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returning the ruling of one returning to his charity to one returning to his vomit, who is he? We narrated in the previous chapter his forbidding returning to charity; possibly meaning his owning it is impermissible, or permissible with dislike; so we sought the truth. 5030 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, from al-Shafii, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: I gave a horse in Allahs path, its holder neglected it, I wanted to buy it deeming he would sell cheap; he said: Do not buy it though he give it for one dirham, nor return to your charity, for one returning to his charity is like a dog returning to its vomit."
"5928","5031 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Maryam, from Muhammad ibn Jafar al-Ansari, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab the like. This chapter likens one returning to his charity to a dog returning to its vomit; the dog is not bound by unlawful and lawful as Adams sons bound by them, including forbidding their vomit to them; the dogs returning to its vomit is returning to filth, not to what is unlawful to it; showing the givers returning is returning to filth, not to unlawful, nor that his ownership of what he gave fails as with things unlawful in themselves. Also narrated from the Messenger through another channel."
"5929","5032 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Rawh ibn Ubadah, from Awf, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The parable of one returning to his gift is as a dog that ate till sated, vomited, then returned to its vomit and ate it. Thus the Messengers intent in forbidding Umar and those with him in the previous chapter is clear."
"5930","797 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about returning in gifts, likening it to a dogs returning to its vomit. 5033 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, from Mualla ibn Asad, from Wuhayb ibn Khalid, from Abd Allah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): One returning in his gift is like a dog that vomits then returns to its vomit."
"5931","5034 - As Fahd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani, from Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak, from Khalid, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We have not an evil parable; one returning in his gift is like a dog returning to its vomit."
"5932","5035 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Abu Amir al-Aqadi, from Shubah and Hisham, from Qatadah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Abbas: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One returning in his gift is like one returning to his vomit. This hadith likens the returner without naming whether of the bound or others; while the two hadiths before name him of the unbound; showing returning in gifts is not unlawful, but filth and base character, not unlawful; supported by what is narrated from the Companions."
"5933","As Yunus narrated to us, that Malik told him, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abi Ghatfan ibn Tarif al-Murri, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever gifts a gift for kinship or as charity does not return in it; whoever gifts deeming reward is meant by it remains upon his gift, returning in it if not pleased therefrom."
"5934","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Makki ibn Ibrahim, from Hanzalah, from Salim: I heard Ibn Umar say: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: Whoever gifts a gift is worthier of it until rewarded therefrom with what pleases him. Abu Jafar said: this mentions the giver is worthier until rewarded as he pleases; the first hadith names which giver, the one deeming reward meant, not other givers."
"5935","As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq, from Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar: Whoever gifts a gift to kin, it is passed; whoever gifts to non-kin is worthier of it while not rewarded therefrom. Abu Jafar said: we know nothing else from Umar on this; and narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib on that."
"5936","As Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Shubah, from Jabir al-Jufi: I heard al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman report from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Ali: The giver is worthier of his gift while not rewarded therefrom."
"5937","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, from Shubah, from Jabir, from al-Qasim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from Ali the like. Abu Jafar said: reconciling this with Umars hadith: the giver Ali meant for obligatory return is the giver Umar meant, the reward-seeking giver; and we know nothing else from Ali in this chapter. And narrated from Abi al-Darda on that."
"5938","As Fahd narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Abi al-Darda: Gifts are three: a man gifting unasked, as charity, who may not return in his charity; a man asked who gifts, due reward - if he accepts reward for his gift that is all, and he may return in his gift while unrewarded; and a man gifting stipulating reward, a debt upon its holder in life and after death. We know nothing else from Abi al-Darda on gifts. And narrated from Fadalalah ibn Ubayd."
"5939","As Fahd narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Amir: I was with Fadalah ibn Ubayd when two men disputing over a hawk came; one said: I gifted him a hawk hoping reward from him; the other said: Yes, he gifted me a hawk unasked and unsought; Fadalah said: Return his gift to him, for only women and the worst people return in gifts. We know nothing else from Fadalah in this chapter; and in what we narrated from the Companions is proof of whom the Messenger meant thereby and the ruling of his returning."
"5940","798 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab and Sahl ibn Hunayf (may Allah be pleased with them), their ordering suspicion of opinion, with what is narrated from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As Salih ibn Hakim al-Basri narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd Allah al-Umayri, from Mubarak ibn Fadalah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar: Suspect opinion upon religion. 5036 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sabiq, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Abi Husayn, from Abi Wail: when Sahl ibn Hunayf (may Allah be pleased with him) came from Siffin we visited seeking news; he said: Suspect opinion; I saw myself on Abu Jandal day, had I been able to return the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order I would have."
"5941","5037 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal, from Abu Amir al-Aqadi, from Shubah, from al-Amash, from Abi Wail: I heard Sahl ibn Hunayf on the Camel and Siffin days say: Suspect your opinions; I saw myself on Abu Jandal day, had I been able to return the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order I would have."
"5942","5038 - As Ishaq narrated to us, from Harun, from Yala ibn Ubayd, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Siyah, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit: I came to Abu Wail hearing him say: Sahl ibn Hunayf said: People, suspect yourselves; we saw ourselves on Hudaybiyah day - the truce between us and the polytheists - had we seen fighting we would have fought. What is in Umar and Sahls hadiths: opinion may hit truth or fall short, though exercising opinion in novelties without ruling in Book, Sunnah or consensus is permitted and we are praised for striving therein though unable beyond it, as we narrated in this book on differing judges: hitting, two rewards; erring, one reward, having striven with fit tools. This is the sound peoples word; as for extremists saying his striving with fit tools judges the truth as Quran would reveal - we seek Allahs refuge from this word and its people, though, praise Allah, this doctrine scarcely needs refutation given its corruption."
"5943","799 - Chapter on the problem in what is narrated from al-Bara, that the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowing, standing, raising his head from bowing, prostrating and between prostrations were near equal. I heard Bakkar ibn Qutaybah say: when I was carried from Basra for what I was carried, arriving at the capital, the judge then Jafar ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Hashimi led us in Asr prayer, standing scarcely bowing, bowing scarcely rising, rising scarcely prostrating, prostrating scarcely rising, rising scarcely prostrating, prostrating, doing in the second prostration as the first, then sitting scarcely saluting, likewise the rest, till I feared Asrs time would leave; when finished I came to him, he asked of my affairs, I told him, then said: O judge, I feared sunset before you finished; from whom did the judge take this prayer? He said: O Abu Bakrah, glory Allah, does this escape you? I took it from the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer. I asked: Who narrated to you the Messengers prayer was thus? 5039 - Bakkar told us: what Abu Dawud narrated to us, from al-Masudi, from al-Hakam: I told Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla: I saw none longer in standing than Abu Ubaydah in prayer; he said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib say: the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowing, raising his head from bowing, prostrating and raising his head from prostration were equal. I asked him: What proof for you in this? And I mentioned to him what Abu Dawud narrated to us."
"5944","5040 - As Abu Dawud narrated to us, from al-Masudi, from Ibn Mawhab, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As - and I saw no Thaqafi better than him -: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leads people, let him lighten prayer, for among them are the old, weak and needy. Abu Bakrah excelled in arguing Jafar thereby; and in this chapter are many reports sufficed by what we mentioned from Bakkar. Abu Jafar said: one firmer than al-Masudi, Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj, narrated al-Baras hadith from al-Hakam."
"5945","5041 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from Shubah, from al-Hakam: when Matar ibn Najiyah overcame Kufah he ordered Abu Ubaydah to lead people; Abu Ubaydah lengthened bowing, and when rising lengthened standing to say: O Allah our Lord, to You praise filling heavens and earth and whatever You will beyond. I mentioned that to Ibn Abi Layla, and he told me from al-Bara ibn Azib: the Messenger of Allahs (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowing, standing, raising his head from bowing, prostrating and between prostrations were near equal. So we understood Abu Ubaydahs lengthening reported by al-Bara from the Messenger was only that measure - O Allah our Lord to You praise filling heavens and earth and whatever You will beyond - and each other posture likewise that measure, besides the sitting tashahhud the Messenger taught people; showing the opposite of Jafars supposition, and by the imams lightening in prayers he led, the Companions after him followed, clinging to his sunnah."
"5946","As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, and as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, both saying: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abu Raja al-'Utaridi, who said: I said to al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him: Why do I see you, O Companions of Muhammad, among the people with the lightest prayer? He said: We hasten ahead of the whispering (of Satan). Abu Ja'far said: By that he means what Satan whispers to them, so they were commanded to lighten the prayer in order to get ahead of that whispering so that it does not overtake them in it. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"5947","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, of his forbidding exaggeration in women's dowries, and his arguing in that from the dowries the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his wives, and from the dowries the husbands of his daughters gave his daughters. 5042 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: al-'Umari narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never gave to any of his wives nor to his daughters more than twelve uqiyyahs."
"5948","5043 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us another time, he said: al-'Umari narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, with the like of that, and he did not mention 'Umar in it."
"5949","5044 - Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-'Umari narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: I think it is from 'Umar, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Zur'ah said to us: The doubt is not from me, but it is in the hadith. So Fahd and Abu Zur'ah differed over Abu Nu'aym in this hadith as we have mentioned."
"5950","5045 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Muzani narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, from 'Umar that he addressed the people and said: Do not be extravagant in women's dowries, for if it were an honor in this world and the Hereafter, the most entitled of you to it and the worthiest would be Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his household. He did not marry any previously-married woman among his wives, nor marry off any previously-married woman among his daughters, for more than twelve uqiyyahs. Abu Ja'far said: I heard this hadith from Rawh and memorized it and wrote it down, then I found part of it had gone from my book due to a page coming loose from it, so I copied it from its original after his death, thus."
"5951","5046 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad, from Abu al-'Ajfa, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Do not be extravagant in women's dowries, for if it were an honor in this world or piety before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been the worthiest of you of it. He did not marry off any previously-married woman among his daughters, nor marry any woman among his wives, for better than twelve uqiyyahs."
"5952","5047 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub and Ibn 'Awn and Salamah ibn 'Alqamah and Hisham ibn Hassan - the hadith of some of them entered into the hadith of others - from Muhammad ibn Sirin; Salamah said: from Ibn Sirin, I was informed from Abu al-'Ajfa, and the others said: from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu al-'Ajfa, who said: 'Umar said: Behold, do not be extravagant in women's dowries, for if it were an honor in this world or piety before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been the worthiest of you of it. He did not give any woman among his wives, nor give any woman among his daughters, more than twelve uqiyyahs in dowry. Behold, one of you indeed goes to excess in his wife's dowry until there remains for her enmity in his heart, so he says: I have burdened myself for you with the water-skin's strap, or he said: the sweat of the water-skin."
"5953","5048 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us. 5049 - And Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, then both agreed and said: He said: Mansur - meaning Ibn Zadhad - informed us, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Abu al-'Ajfa al-Sulami narrated to us, who said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab addressing the people, so he praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and extolled Him, then said: Behold, do not go to excess in women's dowries, for if it were an honor in this world or piety before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been the worthiest of you of it. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith of Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb."
"5954","5050 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu al-'Ajfa, or from Ibn Abi al-'Ajfa, who said: 'Umar said, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is his forbidding the people from exceeding in dowries beyond the dowries of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he gave his wives, and the dowries which the husbands of his daughters gave his daughters. And that was from him, in our view - and Allah knows best - intending that the dowries to be referred to, for whoever among women deserves a dowry like that of his wives, upon whoever among husbands owes it, should be moderate and not excessive. And like this is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had disapproved in his time."
"5955","5051 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Abu Hadrad, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him for help regarding a dowry, so he said: How much have you given as dowry? I said: Two hundred dirhams. He said: If you were scooping from Buthan, it would not be more. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Bakkar narrated it to us."
"5956","5052 - And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated it to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi: that Abu Hadrad married a woman, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his help for her dowry, then he mentioned it. And the dowries which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to give his wives were what we have mentioned in this chapter, and the dowries of those whose dowry he did not disapprove were as given."
"5957","5053 - As 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Musa ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Our dowry when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us was ten awaq, and he joined his hands together, and that is four hundred."
"5958","5054 - And as Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas: that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, married a woman of the Ansar for the weight of a date-stone of gold, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Give a wedding feast, even if with a sheep. And it was reported from 'A'ishah regarding what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his wives as dowry."
"5959","5055 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Had informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, who said: I asked 'A'ishah about the dowry of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his wives, so she said: Twelve uqiyyahs and a nashsh. I said to her: What is a nashsh? She said: Half an uqiyyah."
"5960","5056 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: My grandfather narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and added in it: Such was the dowry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his wives and his daughters. And it was reported from others than her regarding that."
"5961","5057 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, who said: 'Amr ibn Hurayth proposed to 'Adi ibn Hatim for his daughter, so he said: I will not marry you except on my terms. Then one of his companions turned to him and said: By Allah, a woman of Quraysh is dearer to us than a woman of Tayy on her father's terms. He said: That is indeed so, then his soul refused but to propose to him, so he said: I will not marry you except on my terms. He said: I have made you judge. He said: Go, I have married her to you. So 'Amr went and spent the night without sleeping, fearing he would judge against him what he could not bear. When morning came he sent to him: Clarify to me what you have judged upon me so I may send it to you. He said: I judge upon you four hundred and eighty dirhams, the practice of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he sent it to him, and sent to him ten thousand, or twenty thousand - Hudbah doubted - and said: Prepare her with this. And it was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with the hadith of Abu Hadrad."
"5962","5058 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Abu Isma'il, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, I have married a woman - or proposed to a woman, or mentioned a woman. He said: Look at her, for in the eyes of the Ansar there is something. He said: How much have you given her as dowry? He said: Eight awaq. He said: If one of you were carving from the mountain, it would not be more. Abu Ja'far said: So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, remained upon what he was upon of what we have mentioned from him, until proof was established against him from the Book of Allah by which the proof stood against him regarding the permissibility of the highest dowries."
"5963","5059 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mujalid informed us, from al-Sha'bi, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Do not go to excess in women's dowries, for it shall not reach me that anyone has given more than what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave or was given, except that I shall put the excess of that in the public treasury. Then he came down, and a woman of Quraysh met him and said: O Commander of the Believers, is the Book of Allah more deserving to be followed, or your word? He said: Rather the Book of Allah; why is that? She said: You just forbade the people from going to excess in women's dowries, while Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says in His Book: {And you have given one of them a qintar, then do not take anything from it}. So 'Umar said: Everyone is more understanding than 'Umar, two or three times, then he returned to the pulpit and said to the people: I had forbidden you from going to excess in women's dowries, so let a man do with his wealth what he wishes. Abu Ja'far said: And this from 'Umar, after the proof stood against him, was what was obligatory upon him, and what was from him before that of looking after the people was what was obligatory upon him according to what his striving (ijtihad) led him to in it. So when the proof from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, stood against him in contrary to that, he returned to it, and commanded what we have mentioned from him - may Allah's pleasure be upon him. And this is among what indicates the soundness of what we have held regarding striving of opinion (ijtihad al-ra'y) of what our mention of it has preceded in this book of ours. Then there was from him, may Allah be pleased with him, in himself."
"5964","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from his grandfather: that 'Umar gave Umm Kulthum daughter of 'Ali forty thousand as dowry. And there preceded him in that what was given as dowry at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what exceeds the measure which 'Umar had stopped at, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his wives as dowry of what we have mentioned in this chapter."
"5965","5060 - As Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il al-Jabbuli narrated to us - and this is a man of praiseworthy narration - he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give any of his wives nor his daughters more than twelve uqiyyahs, except Umm Habibah, for the Negus married him to her and gave her four thousand as dowry, and paid it on his behalf, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give her anything. Thus al-Baghandi narrated this hadith to us from al-Jabbuli, from Ibn al-Mubarak."
"5966","And Nu'aym ibn Hammad differed with him in it. 5061 - As Fahd and Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, both saying: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Umm Habibah: that she was under 'Ubaydullah ibn Jahsh, who had journeyed to the Negus and died, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Umm Habibah while she was in the land of Abyssinia; the Negus married her to him and gave her four thousand as dowry from himself, and sent her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and all her trousseau was from the Negus, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not send her anything. And the dowries of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were four hundred dirhams. Abu Ja'far said: And in the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving off disapproval of the Negus is what indicates the permissibility of small dowries and large ones. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"5967","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: It is not lawful for the giver to take back his gift, except the father from his child. 5062 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a man to take back his gift, except the father from his child."
"5968","5063 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: It is not lawful for a man who gives a gift or grants a present then takes it back, except the father in what he gives his child. He said: And the like of one who gives a gift then takes it back is like the dog that ate until when it was full it vomited, then returned to its vomit."
"5969","5064 - Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a giver to take back his gift, except the father for his child."
"5970","5065 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Sa'id al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq - and he is al-Azraq - narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a man to give a gift then take it back, except the father in what he gives his child. And the like of one who gives a gift then takes it back is like the dog that ate until when it was full it vomited, then returned to its vomit."
"5971","5066 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Ibn Abi 'Adi narrated to us, from Husayn, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: Tawus narrated to me, from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: It is not lawful for a man to give a gift - meaning - then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Ja'far said: So we looked into this hadith: did anyone other than those mentioned report it from Husayn al-Mu'allim contrary to what those we mentioned reported from him?"
"5972","5067 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Mas'ud informed us, he said: Khalid - meaning Ibn al-Harith - narrated to us, from Husayn, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I think he said: It is not lawful - Husayn doubting in the hadith regarding 'lawful' - that one give a gift then take it back, except the father in what he gives his child. And the like of one who gives a gift then takes it back is like the dog that ate until when it was full it vomited, then returned to its vomit. Abu Ja'far said: And in what Khalid reported from Husayn, Husayn doubted in what in this hadith of his was attributed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of: It is not lawful for anyone to give a gift, without doubt from him in the rest of the hadith. So this hadith of his returned to that what he has no doubt in of it is that: No one takes back his gift, except the father in what he gives his child. And likewise we found it from the narration of others, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, even if he differed with him in its chain."
"5973","5068 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn 'Abdullah informed us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim - and he is Ibn Tahman - narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from 'Amir al-Ahwal, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one takes back a gift, except a father from his child. And the one who returns to his gift is like the one who returns to his vomit. Then we looked: did anyone other than those we mentioned report it from Tawus?"
"5974","5069 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hatim ibn Nu'aym informed us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah - meaning Ibn al-Mubarak - narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Nafi' - meaning al-Makhzumi - from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for anyone to give a gift then take it back, except a father from his child. Tawus said: I used to hear while I was small: one who returns to his vomit, and I did not think he had struck a parable for it. He said: So whoever does that, his like is like the dog that eats, then vomits, then returns to its vomit. Abu Ja'far said: So this hadith from the narration of al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, returned to being stopped upon him (mawquf) by his mentioning it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) broken (munqati'), and al-Hasan ibn Muslim is of not unknown standing in soundness of narration. Then we looked into the text of this hadith, and found the meaning of 'not lawful', even if it were established in the hadith without doubt in it, does not necessitate preventing the giver or the donor from taking back his gift or his present to other than his child, since it may bear the meaning: it is not lawful for a man to soil himself by making himself, by his taking back his gift and his present, like the dog that vomits then eats from it - as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the earnings of the cupper and informed that it is of ill-gotten gain, upon the forbidding from him for anyone of his community to debase himself, not upon that being unlawful. And we have mentioned that with its chains in what has preceded from us in this book of ours. So like that is what was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: It is not lawful for anyone to take back his gift or his present, except the father for the child - carrying the like of that meaning. And his excepting the father in that, in what he gave and what he granted his child, is upon that he is regarding his child's wealth contrary to him regarding others' wealth, since he had said to one who mentioned to him that his father wants to need his wealth: You and your wealth belong to your father. And we mentioned that with its chains in what preceded from us in this book of ours. So he made his entering his child's wealth, even if from this angle, contrary to his entering with it the wealth of others. And it may be that what he permitted of that of his child's wealth is upon the states by which entering his child's wealth is due for him, so his child may not prevent him from that nor from extending his hand in it then - along with that we considered this hadith and found it attributed to Ibn Umar from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while we had reported from Ibn Umar, from Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - in what preceded from us in this book of ours, of what he narrated from him hearing from him, that he said regarding one who gives a gift: that he is most entitled to it until he is rewarded from it with what pleases. So it is impossible that Ibn Umar, with his knowledge and lofty standing, heard from Umar something he had heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to it, then leaves saying to him: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in this contrary to what you said in it. And impossible also that after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he mention something from Umar - may Allah be pleased with him - saying in it of it for people to use, while having from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it what contradicts that ruling. So the meaning of this hadith of Tawus returned to what al-Hasan ibn Muslim reported upon him, of what we mentioned, by its negation from Ibn Umar to the brokenness with which the like of it is not argued. And we ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for success."
"5975","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what al-Nu'man ibn Bashir mentioned from him, of his father granting him something, and of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to him when he called him to witness that: Did you grant all your children the like of this? He said: No. He said: Then take it back. 5070 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man and Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, both informing him: that they heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: My father granted me a slave boy, so my mother commanded me to go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to call him to witness that, so he said: Did you give all your children? He said: No. So he said: Return it."
"5976","5071 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, and from Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, both narrating to him, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: His father brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have granted this son of mine a slave boy of mine. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you granted all your children the like of this? He said: No. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then take it back. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding Bashir to return what he gave al-Nu'man when he informed him he had not given the rest of his children the like of what he gave him, and al-Nu'man that day was small, with no disagreement among the people of knowledge about that, so his father was the one taking possession for him from himself of what he granted him, and in that is the necessity of its leaving his ownership to the ownership of al-Nu'man his son. So we considered this hadith: did anyone other than Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and his son Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man report it from al-Nu'man contrary to what they reported from him, or not?"
"5977","5072 - So we found Nasr ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih al-Harithi narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: My father went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having granted me a grant, to call him to witness that. He said: Have you granted all your children the like of this? He said: No. He said: Would it please you that they be equal to you in dutifulness, all of them alike? He said: Yes. He said: Then call someone other than me to witness this. Abu Ja'far said: That was in our view - and Allah knows best - upon the threat whose outward is outward command and whose inward is rebuke, like the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in His Book: {Do what you will}. And this was also reported from al-Sha'bi with an added meaning beyond this, which Dawud reported from him."
"5978","5073 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: I heard al-Nu'man upon this pulpit of ours saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Make equal among your children in giving, as you love that they be equal to you in dutifulness."
"5979","5074 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated to us, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from al-Sha'bi, who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: My father gave me a gift, so my mother 'Amrah daughter of Rawahah said: I will not be pleased until you call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness. So he came to the Messenger of Allah and said: I have given my son from 'Amrah a gift, and I call you to witness. He said: Have you given all your children the like of this? He said: No. He said: Then fear Allah and be just among your children."
"5980","5075 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Dawud - meaning Ibn Abi Hind - narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, who said: My father set out with me carrying me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, bear witness that I have granted al-Nu'man such-and-such of my wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you granted all your children? He said: No. He said: Would it not please you that they be equal to you in dutifulness? He said: Yes. He said: Then not so. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported was the dislike of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for what Bashir did in singling out his son al-Nu'man with what he singled him out with of his wealth to the exclusion of his other children, and his commanding him along with that to be just among his children. And there is in none of that mention of returning what he granted him, so this differed with what we reported before it in the first section of this chapter. Then we looked: did anyone other than those we mentioned report this hadith from al-Nu'man?"
"5981","5076 - So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman had narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. 5077 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, then both agreed together and said: from Fitr ibn Khalifah, who said: Abu al-Duha narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: My father took me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to call him to witness something he gave me. So he said: Have you other children besides him? He said: Yes. So he said with his hand: Will you not make equal among them? So what is in this hadith also differed with what Humayd and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man reported from al-Nu'man. So we understood thereby that the meaning of what is in the hadith of Nasr ibn Marzuq - call someone other than me to witness this - was only upon the threat in which is warning him against the cause that sets his children at variance in dutifulness to him by deviating from him, due to his preferring others of them over him in what he gave him, despite their equality in their places with him. Except that others than those we mentioned reported this hadith from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man, with an addition to what those we mentioned reported from him, from al-Nu'man."
"5982","5078 - As Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Mujalid and another narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir - and he was governor over Kufah - saying: My father granted me a slave boy, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to call him to witness. So he said: Have you given all your children? He said: No. He said: I bear witness only to what is right. So the meaning of this hadith also indicated what we mentioned, because what calls children or some of them to fall short in dutifulness to their father is contrary to the right upon which matters should run."
"5983","5079 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi informed us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hayyan narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, who said: al-Nu'man ibn Bashir al-Ansari narrated to me: that his mother, the daughter of Rawahah, asked his father for some gift of his wealth for her son, so he put her off for a year, then it occurred to him and he granted it to him. She said: I will not be pleased until you call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness what you have granted my son. So my father took me by my hand, and I was a boy that day, and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the mother of this one, the daughter of Rawahah, has contended with me for a year over some gift of my wealth for this son of mine, and it has now occurred to me, so I have granted it to him, and it pleases her that you witness what I granted him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bashir, have you children besides this one? He said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you granted all of them the like of what you granted this son of yours? He said: No. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then do not call me to witness, for I do not bear witness to injustice. So we understood thereby that the meaning of his saying in what we reported in other than this hadith in this chapter - call someone other than me to witness this - was only upon threat, not upon his permitting him to call others to witness it, a witnessing by which what he gave would become lawful for him. Then we looked: did anyone other than al-Nu'man report this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So we found Jabir ibn 'Abdullah had reported it from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to what al-Nu'man reported from him."
"5984","5080 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, and as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, then both agreed, each one of them saying: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah al-Ju'fi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, who said: The wife of Bashir said to Bashir: Grant my son your slave boy, and call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness for me. He said: So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the daughter of so-and-so asked me to grant her son my slave boy, and said: Call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to witness. He said: Does he have brothers? He said: Yes. He said: Have you given all of them? He said: No. He said: Then this is not right, and I bear witness only to what is right. So what was in this hadith was Bashir's informing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his wife's asking him what she asked, that he grant her son, and his calling him to witness that, and that the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was to him in seeking guidance with which he guided him, not regarding a gift that had preceded from him before that. And this from Jabir is worthier in these reports due to Jabir's place in age and knowledge and his lofty standing in it, and because al-Nu'man that day was small, not having of precision for what he heard the like of what Jabir has in that - along with that Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah reported, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man, from al-Nu'man, this hadith with a meaning indicating what Jabir reported from him."
"5985","5081 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: I heard Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man, that they heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: My father granted me a slave boy, then walked with me until he brought me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have granted my son a slave boy, so if you permit me to carry it out for him I will carry it out. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith upon what we mentioned of the hadith of Malik and Sufyan at the beginning of this chapter. So that indicated that his granting him was not a concluded grant, and that it was a grant awaiting what the Messenger of Allah would say in it, of carrying it out or of other than that. So one said: How is it allowed to use in this the mention of a grant with no reality with it? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, was: that was due to the breadth of the language of the Arabs, for they allow things to be as being due to their nearness to being, even if in reality they have not been - and of that is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {So when you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge in Allah from Satan the outcast}, with the meaning: when you intend to recite the Qur'an, seek refuge; and of that is their naming the one commanded to be slaughtered of the two sons of Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the sacrificed, not because he was slaughtered, but due to his nearness to slaughter. And the like of this in their speech is much. So it has become clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, that there is no disagreement in what Jabir reported, nor in what al-Nu'man reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. And after this, the people of knowledge differed regarding equalizing among children in the like of this: some said it is upon equality between their males and their females in that - and of those who went to that of them is Abu Yusuf. And some went to that it is running them upon the way of the inheritances by which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made them inherit the wealth of their fathers - and of those who went to that is Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And the saying with us in that was what Abu Yusuf went to in it, because that was returned in these reports to the meaning of dutifulness from children to their fathers, and what is wanted from their females in that is as what is wanted from their males. And it did not become clear to us in any of these reports that the father, when he gives his child a gift completed from him to him - even if he differed in it with what he was commanded in his children - that he may take it back, nor that he nullify it. And we ask Allah for success."
"5986","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the blind in his sight: is attending the congregations upon him as upon others than him of those with no harm in their sight, or not? 5082 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri narrating from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', from 'Itban ibn Malik, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man veiled in sight, and the torrents come between me and the mosque, so is there an excuse for me? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: I find no excuse for you when you hear the call. Sufyan said: And in it is a story I did not memorize. Abu Ja'far said: I heard al-Muzani saying: al-Shafi'i said: I never saw him seat people for a hadith except this one, and his hadith 'O remnants of the Arabs' - and Sufyan used to guard against it and knew he did not retain it precisely."
"5987","5083 - Abu Ja'far said: I heard al-Muzani saying: al-Shafi'i said: He erred in it as we see, and the proof of that - and Allah knows best - is that Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', that 'Itban ibn Malik used to lead his people, while blind, and that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is darkness and rain and torrent, and I am a man blind in sight, so pray, O Messenger of Allah, in my house in a place I shall take as a prayer place. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and said: Where do you love that I pray? So he pointed him to a place in the house, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in it. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith, of what was attributed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that he said to 'Itban when he informed him he heard the call: I find no excuse for you - meaning in leaving attending prayers in congregations - except that this meaning we did not find in other than this hadith, from a narration from Sufyan other than al-Shafi'i."
"5988","5084 - And Yunus narrated it to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: from Mahmud, if Allah wills: that 'Itban ibn Malik al-Ansari was a man veiled in sight, and that he mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) staying behind from prayer. So he said: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. So he did not permit him. And we found this hadith also from the narration of 'Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, agreeing with what Malik reported from al-Zuhri, and differing with what Sufyan reported from al-Zuhri."
"5989","5085 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Mahmud ibn al-Rabi' al-Ansari informed me: that 'Itban ibn Malik - and he is of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of those who witnessed Badr of the Ansar - came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my sight has failed, and I pray for my people, and when there are rains, the valley between me and them flows, so I cannot come to their mosque to pray for them. So I wish, O Messenger of Allah, that you come and pray in my house, so I take it as a prayer place. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I shall do so, if Allah wills. 'Itban said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr came in the morning when the day had risen, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked permission, so I permitted him, and he did not sit until he entered the house, then said: Where do you love that I pray? So I pointed to a side of the house, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and we stood and lined up, and he prayed two rak'ahs, then gave salutation. So this hadith returned broken (munqati'), so it was not of what is argued in this chapter with the like of it. Then we looked: was there reported in this chapter other than this hadith?"
"5990","5086 - So we found Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi 'Abbad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from 'Amr ibn Umm Maktum, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am an old man blind in sight, far in house, and I have a guide who does not suit me, and between me and the mosque are trees and rivers, so is there an excuse for me to pray in my house? He said: Do you hear the call? I said: Yes. He said: Then come to it. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith was of the best we found in this chapter, because Zirr ibn Hubaysh had heard from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, so his hearing this hadith from Ibn Umm Maktum is not rejected, for he remained after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and attended the conquest of al-Qadisiyyah, and was the bearer of the banner that day for its people. And we found in this chapter also."
"5991","5087 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali informed us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from 'Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of the mosque and saw softness among the people, so he said: I intend to appoint an imam for the people, then go out, and I shall not be able to find a man staying behind in his house from prayer except that I burn it upon him. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, between me and the mosque are palms and trees, and I am not every time able to have a guide; shall I pray in my house? He said: Do you hear the iqamah? I said: Yes. He said: Then come to it. He said: So one impugning impugned the chain of this hadith and denied that 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad heard from Ibn Umm Maktum. So we considered that and found 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad had heard from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, and whoever heard from 'Umar, his hearing from Ibn Umm Maktum is not rejected. And some of those impugning the chain of this hadith mentioned: that Shu'bah reported it from Husayn, differing with 'Abd al-'Aziz in it, and he mentioned."
"5992","5088 - What 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad al-Rassasi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had: that Ibn Umm Maktum said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Between me and the mosque are things, and sometimes I find a guide and sometimes I do not find a guide. He said: Do you not hear the call? If you hear the call then walk to it. Then another man asked him about the like of that, so he said: If you hear the call then respond. And he did not permit him. Then he said: I have intended to command a man to pray with the people, then come to peoples who do not witness the prayer, and burn upon them. This impugner said: Shu'bah only reported this hadith from Husayn, saying in it: that Ibn Umm Maktum, and did not say in it as 'Abd al-'Aziz said: from Ibn Umm Maktum. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's success and aid, was: that may be from the differing of Shu'bah and 'Abd al-'Aziz over Husayn, because Husayn narrated it once thus and once thus, and each one of Shu'bah and 'Abd al-'Aziz is a preserving imam, a proof, of those when he is alone with something it is accepted from him. And whoever is thus, what he reported of what he was differed with in, of the like of what we mentioned, is not carried upon error from him in what he reported, so long as proof of that does not stand."
"5993","5089 - And we found in this chapter also what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman - meaning al-Wasiti - narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman - meaning al-Razi - narrated to us, he said: Abu Sinan - meaning Sa'id ibn Sinan al-Shaybani, known as al-Qazwini - narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, who said: Abu Razin narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Ibn Umm Maktum came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I am a blind man, far in house, and I have no guide suiting me; is there a permission for me not to come to the mosque? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. So one impugning impugned the chain of this hadith also, saying: Shu'bah reported it from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Abi Layla, and did not go beyond him with it."
"5994","5090 - And he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Layla saying: A blind man of us said: O Messenger of Allah, between me and the mosque are palms. So the Messenger of Allah said: Do you hear the call? He said: Yes. He said: If you hear the call then respond to it. He said: That indicated that the origin of this hadith is only from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Abi Layla, broken, not from 'Amr, from Abu Razin, from Abu Hurayrah. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's - Majestic and Mighty - success and aid, was: that there has escaped him in these two hadiths what he should not with it have hastened with what he hastened in them, because the hadith of Shu'bah, from 'Amr, from Ibn Abi Layla, in it he said: There was a blind man of us - and that was informing from him about a man of them, meaning the Ansar, for he is of them - and the first hadith which Abu Sinan reported, from 'Amr, from Abu Razin, from Abu Hurayrah, is regarding Ibn Umm Maktum, and he is a man of Quraysh, not of the Ansar. So we understood thereby that they are two hadiths regarding two different men - along with our standing upon the trustworthiness of this Abu Sinan in his narration and his uprightness in it, and the acceptance of the imams of it from him. Then we looked regarding Mahmud ibn al-Rabi': is meeting 'Itban ibn Malik possible from the like of him, or not?"
"5995","5091 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, who said: My father came from Syria as a delegate, I with him, and we met Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', and my father narrated from 'Itban ibn Malik. So my father said: Memorize this hadith, for it is of the treasures of hadith. When we returned we went to Madinah, so I asked about him, and he was alive, an old blind shaykh as if meaning 'Itban ibn Malik. So I asked him about the hadith, and he said: Yes, my sight went during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I said: O Messenger of Allah, my sight has gone, and I cannot pray behind you, so if you would assign for me in my house a mosque, praying in it, I would take it as a prayer place. He said: Yes, I am coming to you tomorrow. When he had prayed the next day he turned to him and stood until he came, and said: O 'Itban, where do you love that I assign for you? He said: So I described a place to him, and he assigned it for him and prayed in it. If this hadith is heavy upon some people due to the place of 'Ali ibn Zayd."
"5996","5092 - Then he narrated to us Husayn ibn Nasr, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Thabit, from Anas, who said: Mahmud ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from 'Itban ibn Malik. Anas said: So I met 'Itban and he narrated it to me, and it pleased me, so I said to my son: Write it, so he wrote it. So in this was the return of this hadith to the agreement of Sulayman al-Taymi with 'Ali ibn Zayd upon it, and Mahmud's reporting it from 'Itban was not rejected. And in that was the necessity of excuse for Ibn 'Uyaynah in what he reported from al-Zuhri, from Mahmud, from him - and due to what stood with these reports, or with what stood of them, of what we mentioned of the obligation of attending congregations upon the blind in his sight as is obligatory upon the sound in his sight. And this chapter was of what the people of knowledge differed in: a party of them said the attending of congregations is obligatory upon the blind as its obligatoriness upon the sound, and they made him like one who does not know the road, so he is not excused by his ignorance of it from staying behind from attending the congregation for that. And others excused him in leaving attending the congregation. And both sayings were reported from Abu Hanifah, except that the sound with us from him is the obligatoriness of attending it upon him, and to that went Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and he does not relate in it disagreement between him and anyone of his companions. And Ibn Umm Maktum addressed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he recited to the people: {Not equal are those who sit of the believers and those who strive in the way of Allah}, before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down upon him in the verse: {except those of harm} - by saying to him: If I could strive I would strive. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that, nor did he say to him: You are blind and there is no obligation in that upon the blind. And in what we mentioned of that is proof that what the blind can do despite blindness, he is in it like the sound who has no blindness. And when the blind is in attending congregations as we mentioned, he is in the obligatoriness of hajj upon him when he finds a way to it, and finds what conveys him to it of provision and of a conveyer to it, like other than the blind. And we ask Allah for success."
"5997","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When one of you wakes from his sleep, let him not put his hand in the vessel until he washes it, for he does not know where his hand spent the night, or in what his hand spent the night. 5093 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr al-Bajali narrated to us - and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, then both agreed and said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me: that Abu Hurayrah used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you rises in the night, let him not put his hand in the vessel until he pours upon them twice or thrice, for he does not know, one of you, in what his hand spent the night."
"5998","5094 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, both saying: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"5999","5095 - And Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you wakes from his sleep, let him wash his hand before he puts it in his ablution water, for one of you does not know where his hand spent the night."
"6000","5096 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
